ual researchers' plates, you free their time for actual science. This aligns with the lab's mission to do science efficiently within budget.\n- The risk is scope creep — \"modular internal library\" can balloon into a months-long engineering project that distracts senior engineers from research milestones. The proposal says \"Python wrapper that integrates with our current orchestration tools,\" which suggests incremental work rather than building from scratch. That's more reasonable.\n- From a funding perspective, this is an operational cost that enables compliance without requiring new headcount or external vendor contracts. It leverages existing staff and infrastructure (current orchestration tools).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal explicitly says it integrates with \"our current orchestration tools.\" This means we're not building new infrastructure but layering a thin compliance capture on top of what already exists. That's good resource efficiency.\n- A shared SDK also means that when the EU AI Act's implementing measures or guidance documents evolve (and they will — the technical standards are still being developed), you update one library rather than N scattered logging implementations. This reduces future maintenance burden significantly.\n- The \"modular\" aspect is important: if not all projects need the same level of logging, modularity lets teams pick what they need without over-implementing.\n\n**Concerns/risks:**\n- Building an SDK requires dedicated engineering time that could go toward research. Who maintains it? If it's a side project for researchers, it will get neglected. If we assign a dedicated engineer, that's a resource commitment.\n- \"Standardized API calls\" — the standardization might be premature if the exact regulatory requirements (especially Annex III specific logging in Art 12(3)) haven't been fully clarified by implementing acts or harmonized standards. But the core requirement (automatic event recording) is clear from the text, so a basic SDK addressing Articles 12(1)-(2) is feasible now.\n- The proposal is scoped reasonably — it's not proposing to build a full monitoring platform, just the logging capture layer. That's appropriate scope.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, resource-efficient infrastructure investment that directly serves compliance while freeing researcher time. It leverages existing tools (current orchestration), reduces per-project overhead through standardization, and has clear maintenance advantages over ad-hoc solutions. The main risk is engineering time allocation, but the proposal is appropriately scoped as a \"Python wrapper\" rather than a full platform build. From my vantage point on resource efficiency and infrastructure use, this clears my bar.\n\nI'd rate this around 78-82. It's clearly valuable and well-scoped, but there's some uncertainty about whether we have enough active high-risk projects to justify it immediately vs. waiting for a few more to materialize. Also, the \"modular\" aspect needs careful scoping to avoid over-building. I'll go with 80.\n",
