        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate proposal P2211 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping, in the context of EU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n\"Semantic Traceability for Model Interpretability. A research project focused on transforming raw event logs into human-readable causal chains of decision making. We will develop algorithms that map automatic log entries to internal model states and activation patterns. This turns mandatory compliance data into a tool for scientific discovery regarding AI behavior. It leverages regulatory requirements to push the knowledge frontier of AI transparency and safety research.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on:\n- Technical viability of solutions\n- Speed/quality of prototyping\n\nLet me think about this carefully:\n\n1. **Technical viability**: The proposal describes \"developing algorithms that map automatic log entries to internal model states and activation patterns\" and creating \"human-readable causal chains of decision making.\" This is an extremely ambitious research problem. Mapping logs to internal model states (especially for complex neural networks) is at the frontier of interpretability research. For large, opaque models, extracting meaningful \"causal chains\" from activation patterns is not a solved problem. The technical feasibility here is highly uncertain — we don't know if such algorithms can be built in any reasonable timeframe or with current techniques.\n\n2. **Speed/quality of prototyping**: This is framed as a \"research project\" explicitly focused on \"pushing the knowledge frontier.\" From a prototyping standpoint, this is not something you can iterate quickly. It's exploratory research with no clear MVP, no incremental deliverables, and high uncertainty about whether the core assumption (that logs can be meaningfully mapped to causal chains) even holds for the types of models we deploy.\n\n3. **Relevance to Article 12**: The regulation requires automatic logging of events. It does NOT require that those logs be interpretable or transformed into \"causal chains of decision making.\" The requirement is about recording: periods of use, reference databases, input data, and identification of natural persons in verification. The proposal goes far beyond what the regulation demands — it's treating a compliance requirement as an opportunity for fundamental interpretability research. While that might be scientifically interesting, it's not what Article 12 asks for.\n\n4. **Resource commitment concern**: Approving this means committing scarce implementation resources to a long-horizon research project with no guaranteed technical outcome. The \"promising\" framing around scientific discovery is attractive but doesn't address whether we can actually deliver on the core technical claim in any reasonable timeline.\n\n5. **Prototyping angle**: Can I prototype this quickly? Not really. The core algorithm — mapping log entries to internal model states and activation patterns, then extracting causal chains — requires:\n   - Access to model internals (which may not be available for all our systems)\n   - Novel interpretability techniques that don't yet exist in a production-ready form\n   - Validation that the \"causal chains\" are actually correct/meaningful\n   - This is not something where you can build a v0.1 in weeks and iterate\n\n6. **Practical concern**: Even if we could do this for one model, generalizing across the portfolio of high-risk AI systems would be extremely challenging. Different architectures have different internal states. The \"human-readable causal chain\" format has no standard or specification to target.\n\nMy assessment: From a pure prototyping and technical viability standpoint, this is too speculative. It's framed as research (\"research project,\" \"pushing the knowledge frontier\"), which means there's no clear path from idea to working prototype. The core technical claim (mapping logs → model states → causal chains) is not currently achievable with confidence for complex models. I cannot commit implementation resources to something where I don't know if the fundamental approach will work, and where even if it does, the timeline is highly uncertain.\n\nHowever, I should note there's a kernel of practical value: making logs more human-readable IS useful for compliance verification and post-market monitoring. But the specific technical approach described (mapping to activation patterns, causal chains) is far beyond what's needed and far too speculative.\n\nDecision: Reject. The proposal is fundamentally a research exploration with high technical uncertainty, no clear prototyping path, and no incremental deliverables. From my vantage point of technical viability and speed of prototyping, I cannot commit resources to this without knowing the core algorithm is even feasible.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely interesting from an R&D perspective — there's something valuable here long-term. But from MY specific vantage point (technical viability now, speed of prototyping), it scores low. I'd say maybe 20-30. It's not zero because the underlying problem (making logs more interpretable) has practical value and some sub-components could be prototyped. But as described, the full proposal is too speculative for my approval bar.\n\nLet me settle on: Reject, promise score around 25.\n",
