        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2675\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal's core technical dependency—a labeled dataset of Article 79(1) 'risk events' sufficient to train a secondary monitoring model—is unsolved and likely absent at this stage, making the stated real-time ML architecture a research-grade effort rather than a buildable prototype within our current cycle. Additionally, the design over-engineers well beyond what Article 12(1) actually mandates (automatic *recording* of events), and deploying its own learned monitor introduces recursive documentation/validation obligations that compound scope without proportional regulatory return for a first iteration.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 28\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2675 is about building an \"Automated Risk Detection from Event Logs\" - essentially a secondary monitoring model that analyzes the logs mandated by Article 12 to detect behavioral drifts or risks per Article 79(1).\n\nLet me assess this from my professional perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The proposal describes training a \"secondary monitoring model\" on a \"labeled dataset of 'risk events'\" for the monitor. This is a significant technical challenge. Who labels these risk events? Where do you get enough labeled examples of Article 79(1) risks to train a meaningful classifier? This is a cold-start problem that's very hard to solve quickly.\n- The proposal conflates \"logging capability\" (what Article 12 actually requires - the system must technically allow automatic recording of events) with an active monitoring/detection layer on top of logs. Article 12(1) says systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" It doesn't mandate a real-time AI-based risk detection system. The logging requirement is about *recording*, not about *analyzing and flagging* in real time.\n- Building a secondary ML model that monitors another ML model's logs introduces its own failure modes, latency concerns, and validation challenges. You now have two models to test, validate, document, and maintain.\n- The \"labeled dataset of risk events\" is the critical bottleneck. In practice, you'd need to wait for actual incidents or engineer synthetic ones, both of which are slow and uncertain.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a heavyweight proposal. Training a secondary model requires: data collection (or synthesis), labeling pipeline, training infrastructure, validation methodology, integration with the logging system, ongoing retraining/maintenance.\n- The \"real-time\" aspect adds complexity - you need streaming architecture, low-latency inference, alerting mechanisms.\n- For prototyping speed: this is not something you can quickly prototype and iterate on because the core challenge (labeled risk event data) doesn't exist yet for a new system.\n- However, there IS value in starting with simpler rule-based or statistical anomaly detection over logs before jumping to a full ML model. But that's not what's proposed here - they're proposing a trained secondary monitoring model.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on the actual technical architecture. \"Train a secondary monitoring model\" could mean many things. What architecture? What features from the logs? What does \"patterns of failure or instability\" concretely look like in log data?\n- The framing as an \"automated safety loop\" sounds impressive but may be overpromising what's achievable in reasonable timeframes.\n- From a prototyping standpoint, you could start with a much simpler approach: structured logging with schema validation, threshold-based alerts on key metrics, and periodic manual review. The full ML monitoring model is the end-state, not necessarily the right first prototype.\n\n**Does it actually serve Article 12?**\n- Article 12 requires the system to \"technically allow for automatic recording of events.\" A basic logging infrastructure satisfies this. The proposal builds something much more than what's required - an active AI monitoring layer. While potentially valuable, it goes well beyond the regulatory minimum and introduces significant technical risk (the monitor itself becoming a high-risk component needing its own documentation under Article 12).\n- There's a meta-issue: if you're deploying a secondary ML model to monitor the primary system, does THAT model need to be documented as part of the AI system? This creates recursive compliance complexity.\n\n**My decision:**\nFrom a prototyping speed and technical viability standpoint, this proposal is ambitious but has significant technical gaps that would slow it down considerably:\n1. The labeled dataset problem is unsolved and potentially blocking\n2. \"Real-time\" ML inference adds architectural complexity\n3. It's vague on concrete implementation path\n4. It over-engineers relative to what Article 12 actually mandates (logging, not AI-based risk detection)\n5. The secondary model introduces its own compliance/documentation burden\n\nHowever, the underlying idea of monitoring logs for anomalies is sound and there are incremental ways to approach this. But as proposed - \"train a secondary monitoring model\" in real-time with a labeled dataset that presumably doesn't exist yet - it's not a quick win. It's a research project dressed as an engineering task.\n\nI'd lean toward reject because:\n- The core technical challenge (labeled risk event data) is unsolved and potentially blocking for months\n- It over-engineers beyond what Article 12 requires\n- \"Real-time\" ML monitoring is architecturally complex to prototype quickly\n- The proposal doesn't specify a concrete, achievable first prototype\n- From my vantage point of speed/quality of prototyping, this is not something I can deliver in a reasonable sprint\n\nBut it's not a zero - the general direction (log analysis for anomaly detection) has merit. Maybe a 30-40 range for promise? It has value but as proposed it's not viable to commit scarce implementation resources to right now without significant scoping work and data availability assessment first. Actually, I'd say around 25-35. The idea direction is okay but the execution plan as stated would consume a lot of time hitting the labeled-data wall before producing anything demonstrable.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 30.\n",
