tandpoint, this creates a binary gate that doesn't match reality — risk classification can be ambiguous, and the technical implementation of logging is often easier to build in a modular way that's cheap to enable/disable rather than requiring a formal governance decision first.\n\n**What it gets right:** There IS value in not building expensive logging infrastructure for every single prototype or low-risk tool. Some triage is needed. But the mechanism proposed (mandatory intake form + quarterly meetings) is heavyweight and slow.\n\n**What's missing from an engineering perspective:**\n- No technical architecture for the actual logging system\n- No discussion of how logging would be implemented technically (structured logs, event sourcing, telemetry pipelines, etc.)\n- No consideration of how to make logging modular/pluggable so it can be toggled without re-engineering\n- The \"intake form\" is a paper process; from an engineering view, I'd want the classification to be encoded as metadata in the project management system or CI/CD pipeline\n- Quarterly cadence is too slow for R&D velocity\n\n**Verdict:** From my vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping), this proposal is a governance/administrative process that doesn't address the actual technical implementation challenge. It adds friction to the development workflow without contributing to the engineering solution. The triage instinct is sound but the mechanism is misaligned with engineering velocity. I'd reject it as not something my function should own or pursue — and even if another function owns it, the quarterly cadence and mandatory form would slow our prototyping cycles.\n\nHowever, I should note: the idea of scoping logging work to only high-risk systems IS technically relevant (it affects architecture decisions about where to invest). But as framed, this is a governance process, not an engineering solution. It doesn't help me build better, faster prototypes with proper logging.\n\