ew process\" — the word \"mandatory\" and the gate nature means no system ships without this sign-off. If the lab has limited engineering bandwidth, this adds serialization to the deployment pipeline, potentially slowing scientific output.\n- However, Article 12 is a legal obligation for high-risk AI systems. You can't skip it. So the question isn't whether you need compliance verification — you do. The question is whether THIS structure (a dedicated committee + checklist) is the most resource-efficient way to achieve it, or whether existing QA/review processes could be extended with minimal incremental cost.\n- A \"compliance checklist\" is low-cost to create and maintain. That part is very efficient.\n- The \"audit committee\" is where resources get committed. But without a committee or at least designated reviewers, who actually does the verification? You need SOME human sign-off. The question is whether it needs to be a formal standing body or can be embedded in existing project review gates.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThe core idea — verify logging compliance before deployment via structured checks — is sound and necessary. Article 12 requires it, so you're not choosing to do this work; the only question is structure. A checklist + designated reviewer sign-off at an existing pipeline stage would be more resource-efficient than a new standing \"audit committee.\" But the proposal as written does capture the right intent: catch issues early, integrate into the pipeline (not bolt-on), and verify technical documentation completeness.\n\nFrom a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, I'd want to see this leaner — embed it in existing review gates rather than create a new committee. But the fundamental approach is correct and necessary. The cost of NOT doing this (regulatory penalties, rework) far exceeds the incremental cost of structured pre-deployment verification.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a necessary compliance function framed efficiently (integrated into pi