        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1216) from my vantage point as Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**Proposal P1216: High-Risk AI Technical Documentation Protocol**\n- A mandatory institutional workflow for documenting algorithms, training data, and risk management processes in a living repository.\n- Transforms static reports into dynamic compliance assets.\n- Version-controlled documentation system where researchers must update system characteristics and limitations at every major milestone of the development cycle.\n- Compliance officers review these entries monthly.\n- Mitigates audit failure risk due to incomplete/outdated technical records.\n\n**My vantage point: resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.**\n\nLet me think about this carefully:\n\n1. **Resource-allocation efficiency**: This proposal mandates documentation at \"every major milestone\" with monthly compliance reviews. This is a significant ongoing operational overhead. Researchers are pulled away from research to update documentation. Compliance officers (a fixed cost) spend time reviewing entries monthly. The question is whether this is efficient use of resources or an unnecessary bureaucratic layer. However, the EU AI Act explicitly requires this under Article 12 and Recital 71 — technical documentation must be \"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" So this isn't optional compliance; it's a legal requirement for high-risk systems. The proposal is essentially implementing what the law already mandates.\n\n2. **Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints**: Adding mandatory documentation workflows could slow down research cycles. Researchers updating docs at every milestone, monthly reviews — this adds process overhead. But without it, you risk non-compliance which could mean losing the ability to deploy high-risk systems entirely (much worse for resource allocation). The question is whether \"every major milestone\" and \"monthly review\" are proportionate or over-engineered relative to what the law actually requires.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: A version-controlled documentation system can leverage existing tools (git, internal wikis, document management systems). This doesn't require massive new infrastructure investment. It's a process change more than a capital expenditure. However, it does add a standing operational cost (compliance officer time for monthly reviews, researcher time for updates).\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\n\nThe proposal addresses a genuine regulatory requirement (Article 12, Recital 71 explicitly calls for technical documentation kept up to date throughout the system's lifetime). From a resource-allocation perspective:\n- The core idea (version-controlled living documentation) is efficient — it avoids redundant static reports and leverages existing tools.\n- However, \"at every major milestone\" could be interpreted very broadly and consume significant researcher time. This is a risk to scientific throughput.\n- Monthly compliance reviews are an ongoing fixed cost that must be budgeted for.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what counts as a \"major milestone,\" which could lead to either under-documentation (risk) or over-documentation (waste of resources).\n\nFrom my perspective, the fundamental direction is correct and legally necessary — I can't reject the need for this. But the specific implementation details (every major milestone, monthly reviews) might be over-specified in a way that could consume disproportionate researcher time relative to the regulatory requirement. The law says \"appropriate throughout the lifetime\" — it doesn't mandate monthly reviews specifically.\n\nHowever, as Lab Director concerned with resource efficiency and effective use of infrastructure:\n- This is a necessary compliance investment (not optional).\n- A version-controlled system leverages existing tools efficiently.\n- The main concern is whether the cadence (\"every major milestone,\" \"monthly\") is right-sized or will become bureaucratic overhead that crowds out research time.\n- The proposal as stated is reasonable but could be more precise about what triggers updates to avoid scope creep in documentation effort.\n\nI think this clears my bar — it's a necessary compliance measure, it leverages existing infrastructure (version control), and the resource cost, while real, is proportionate to the regulatory risk of non-compliance. I'll approve it but note my concern about cadence specification. My promise score should reflect that it's solidly necessary but has some implementation efficiency concerns from a resource perspective.\n\nI'd put this around 72-78. It's clearly needed, leverages existing tools well, but the \"every major milestone\" and \"monthly review\" cadences could be tightened to avoid over-consumption of researcher time. From pure resource-allocation efficiency, it's good but not perfect in its specification. Let me say 74.\n",
