t mathematical impossibility of alteration. A hash chain achieves tamper-evidence without needing full formal verification.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Formal methods research has a very long time-to-value. You're talking about years of research before you get a usable framework, if ever. This is fundamentally incompatible with the speed of prototyping that I care about.\n- The EU AI Act compliance deadline for high-risk systems under Annex III is August 2026 (for most) or February 2025 for some. Formal verification research won't deliver in that timeframe.\n- What Article 12 actually requires is much more modest: implement structured logging with timestamps, event types, and the specific data points listed in paragraph 3. This is a well-understood engineering task (structured logging, append-only storage, audit trails). You can prototype this in weeks, not years of research.\n- The proposal frames itself as \"research\" - but from my perspective, we need working implementations, not research into whether formal methods might eventually guarantee something the regulation doesn't actually require.\n\n**Does it clear my bar?**\nThe regulation requires: (1) technical capability to auto-record events, (2) traceability appropriate to intended purpose, (3) specific minimum data for Annex III 1(a) systems. This is an engineering implementation task with well-understood solutions (structured logging frameworks, append-only databases, cryptographic hash chains for integrity verification). \n\nThe proposal proposes formal mathematical proofs of completeness - which is:\n1. Not required by the regulation (the reg asks for \"technically allow for automatic recording,\" not \"prove mathematically that no event can be missed\")\n2. Technically infeasible for general AI systems in any practical timeframe\n3. A multi-year research endeavor with uncertain payoff\n4. Overkill relative to what's needed\n\nHowever, there IS a kernel of something useful here: cryptographic h