l says it \"directly satisfies Article 12(3)(b).\" From a compliance perspective, I'd want to make sure it's not overselling — the requirement is that logging capabilities \"provide\" this information. A versioning system helps, but the actual logging mechanism (Article 12(1)) must capture the reference. The proposal seems to assume the infrastructure is there and just adds the database tracking layer.\n- However, as a standalone proposal in an ideation exercise, it's identifying a specific gap and proposing a targeted solution. That's appropriate.\n- \"Cryptographically tied\" — this is a design detail that enhances auditability but isn't strictly required by the Act. It's a nice-to-have for evidence integrity, not a compliance requirement per se. But it doesn't hurt and strengthens the institution's position.\n\n**From institutional survival / regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- This maps to a hard legal obligation (shall), specifically for Annex III 1(a) systems. Failure to comply is a direct violation.\n- If a high-risk AI system in biometric identification makes an erroneous match, the regulator will demand to know exactly which database version was used. Without this, the institution cannot demonstrate compliance, faces enforcement risk, and its market reputation suffers.\n- This is not speculative or nice-to-have; it's addressing a specific \"shall\" requirement with a concrete implementation path.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Demonstrating robust data lineage and versioning in compliance documentation builds trust with regulators, clients, and the public. It shows the institution takes traceability seriously.\n- In a sector where AI systems are under increasing scrutiny (especially biometric systems), being able to produce clean, immutable audit trails is a competitive differentiator.\n\n**Decision:** This clears my bar. It addresses a specific, mandatory regulatory requirement with a concrete, implementable technical solution. The scope