e with all aspects of the AI Act (risk management, post-market monitoring data, etc.). But this is more of a marketing/scope statement issue than a technical flaw in the proposal itself.\n2. The proposal mentions \"timestamps and input data automatically\" but Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems also requires: reference database used, input data that led to a match, identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art 14(5)). A generic middleware wrapper might not capture all of these without per-system configuration. However, the proposal says \"standardized event recording\" and is a framework — it could be designed to have configurable hooks for system-specific fields.\n3. The scope seems somewhat narrow: it focuses on model servers and timestamps/input data. For a broader set of high-risk systems (not just biometric identification under Annex III 1(a)), the logging needs differ (Art 12(2)(a), (b), (c)). But as an initial framework, starting with a core middleware that can be extended is reasonable.\n4. \"Throughout the system lifetime\" — this implies persistence and retention strategies need to be addressed. A library alone doesn't solve storage lifecycle management. But this could be a subsequent phase.\n\n**From my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality):**\n- Technically viable: Yes. Middleware wrappers for logging are a mature, well-understood pattern. Integrating with existing model servers is feasible. A shared library reduces complexity. This is a solid, implementable engineering solution.\n- Prototyping speed: Strongly positive. Removing per-experiment logging boilerplate directly accelerates the prototyping cycle. Researchers can focus on model development while compliance capture happens automatically. The \"set and forget\" nature of a middleware approach means new experiments don't require additional compliance work.\n- Quality: A standardized framework ensures consistency across prototypes, reducing the risk 