ulatory minimums are already defined in Art 12(3), so we need to meet those regardless; (b) beyond minimums, we can make engineering judgment calls without a full research program; (c) \"various high-risk systems\" implies cross-system work that fragments our R&D infrastructure.\n- Resource efficiency concern: This is a large open-ended study with no clear deliverable deadline tied to compliance obligations. It could drift indefinitely. The \"thought leadership\" framing suggests it's more about external positioning than internal operational need.\n- Funding constraint concern: We have limited resources. Committing to a multi-system comparative benchmarking program diverts engineers and compute away from actual system development and compliance implementation.\n- However, there IS a legitimate engineering question here: what logging granularity do we implement beyond the minimums? But that can be addressed with targeted profiling on our own systems rather than a broad \"regulatory impact benchmarking study.\"\n\n**Decision:** This proposal is essentially an open-ended research program justified partly by branding aspirations. From my resource-allocation standpoint, it's expensive, diffuse in scope (\"various high-risk systems\"), not strictly necessary for compliance (the minimums are set), and the practical engineering questions it raises could be answered more efficiently through targeted profiling within our own development cycle rather than a standalone research initiative. I would reject this.\n\n**Promise score:** The underlying question (what's the right logging granularity?) is legitimate, but the proposal as framed — multi-system comparative study positioned as thought leadership — is not how I'd allocate scarce R&D resources. Maybe a 25-30? It touches on a real engineering concern but the scope, framing, and resource demands make it poor from my vantage point. I'll say 25.\n",
