gineers to build this tool competes with other priorities.\n- The \"template-driven\" approach may become brittle as the AI Act's implementing regulations add detail or as our systems evolve in ways not captured by standard model card fields.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable and well-targeted proposal that addresses a genuine recurring cost. It leverages existing infrastructure (Git, model cards, logs) which aligns with my preference for effective use of what we already have. The ROI case depends on the volume of high-risk systems we maintain, but the direction is sound. However, I'm somewhat concerned about:\n1. The overclaim that it produces \"legally compliant\" documents without human review\n2. The brittleness risk of a template-driven approach to evolving regulatory requirements\n3. Whether the engineering investment is proportionate to our current portfolio of high-risk systems\n\nI think this clears my bar as a worthwhile investment - it's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, and addresses a real recurring burden. But I'd want scoping to ensure it starts with a focused MVP (covering the most common documentation needs) rather than attempting full coverage on day one. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but not transformative from my vantage point - it's a good operational efficiency play but carries some risk of overengineering or scope creep.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-high promise score, reflecting that it's well-aligned with resource efficiency and existing infrastructure use, but with some caveats about the \"legally compliant\" claim and template brittleness.\n\nScore: I'd say around 72-78. It's clearly useful, leverages what we have, addresses a real cost center. But it's not a no-brainer because of the compliance-accuracy risk and the upfront engineering cost relative to our funding constraints. Let me settle on 75.\n",
