d per-system test cases.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Building a log-audit verification tool is relatively quick to prototype — you define the schema of what should be logged (per Art 12(3) at minimum), write assertions against it, and run them against sample logs. This could be prototyped in days to weeks.\n- The \"adversarial trigger\" simulation layer is where it gets expensive and system-specific. You need to understand each deployed model's failure modes to create meaningful triggers. This is more research-heavy than engineering-heavy.\n- As a unit test for record-keeping, the value proposition is clear: every time you deploy or modify a logging pipeline, you can run this suite and get immediate pass/fail. That's genuinely useful for development velocity.\n- The risk: if it's built too generically before the actual logging architecture is stable, you'll waste prototyping effort on abstractions that don't match reality. Better to prototype the log-audit part first (cheap, high value) and defer the adversarial trigger simulation until the system is more mature.\n\n**My assessment:**\n- The core idea — automated verification of logging compliance as a test suite — is technically sound and valuable for development velocity. It fits naturally into CI/CD pipelines.\n- The \"adversarial triggers\" component is where technical viability becomes uncertain without knowing the specific systems. It could be a massive scope creep or it could be a focused, high-value addition depending on how it's scoped.\n- From a prototyping speed standpoint: the log-audit verification part is highly prototyper-friendly (structured data validation). The simulation of risk conditions is harder to prototype quickly because it requires domain-specific failure injection.\n- Overall: I see clear value in the \"unit test for record-keeping\" framing. It's a concrete, buildable artifact with immediate utility. The adversarial trigger part adds complexity but the proposal doesn't over-c