ongoing costs (infrastructure, maintenance, security). Is this the most efficient use of our resources? However, if we have existing logging infrastructure, integrating with it could be cheap.\n- The SDK itself needs to be built and maintained. This divotes engineering time from research projects. But if done once well, it amortizes across all high-risk projects — that's actually good resource efficiency.\n- \"Python-based\" — this is practical for an R&D institution. Most ML/AI work in Python. Good fit with existing infrastructure.\n- The scope seems reasonable and not overreaching. It doesn't try to solve every compliance problem, just the logging requirement of Article 12(1).\n\nFrom a resource-allocation perspective: Building one SDK that serves all high-risk projects is far more efficient than having each project team implement its own logging. This is classic \"build once, use many\" efficiency. The fact that it's lightweight and integrates with existing frameworks suggests it won't consume disproportionate resources.\n\nFrom the funding constraints angle: A focused SDK is a bounded investment. It doesn't require a new team or massive budget. It could be handled by a small engineering group over a few months while leveraging existing infrastructure for storage.\n\nFrom scientific ambition balance: By abstracting compliance away from research code, scientists can focus on their work. The overhead on individual researchers is minimal (just using decorators/wrappers). This protects the lab's core mission.\n\nFrom effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: It explicitly integrates with \"our existing frameworks\" and pushes to a centralized store (which could leverage existing data infrastructure). This is well-aligned with my domain concern.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-scoped, resource-efficient proposal that directly addresses Article 12(1) while minimizing disruption to research workflows. The investment is bounded, the payback spans multiple projects, and it leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new platforms. I'd approve this with moderate-to-high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong fit for my vantage point. It's efficient, bounded, uses existing infrastructure, protects research time. I'd say around 78-82. Let me settle on 80. It's not a 90+ because there's always risk in building shared internal tooling (adoption friction, maintenance burden, the centralized store has ongoing costs), but from my specific lens of resource efficiency and infrastructure leverage, it's very well-designed.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: 80\n",
