e not doing research. This pulls people away from scientific work.\n- The proposal says it \"reduces the manual burden on researchers during the development phase\" - this is a real benefit but the upfront cost of building the framework must be weighed against what those engineers could otherwise be doing.\n- Is this proportionate? Article 12 requires logging capabilities, but the proposal goes beyond minimum compliance with \"immutable ledger,\" \"open-sourcing for community verification,\" and \"across all our high-risk project pipelines.\" The scope seems to exceed what's strictly required by the regulation in a way that adds cost.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- If we already have monitoring/logging tools in our ML pipelines (e.g., Weights & Biases, MLflow, Prometheus/Grafana stacks), this proposal should build ON those rather than creating parallel systems. The proposal doesn't mention leveraging existing infrastructure - it proposes a \"standardized software layer\" and \"modular API wrapper\" which could duplicate what we already have.\n- Integration with \"all our high-risk project pipelines\" is good if done via the existing pipeline orchestration, but the proposal reads as though it's building something new from scratch.\n- The \"open-sourced to invite community verification\" aspect - this is more of a governance/reputation play than an infrastructure efficiency play. It adds maintenance burden (issue tracking, PR review, versioning for external users) that doesn't directly serve our R&D mission.\n\n**Key concerns:**\n1. Scope creep: \"immutable ledger\" and \"open-source community verification\" go beyond what Article 12 strictly requires and add significant resource demands.\n2. Duplication risk: No mention of leveraging existing logging/monitoring infrastructure already in the lab.\n3. The open-sourcing commitment is a long-term maintenance obligation that pulls engineering resources away from research.\n4. However, the core idea - a unified logging layer for high-risk models - IS aligned with what Article 12 requires and would genuinely improve efficiency across projects.\n\n**Decision:** The core concept has merit (unified logging infrastructure), but as scoped, it overreaches. The immutable ledger, open-sourcing commitment, and \"across all pipelines\" mandate represent a significant resource commitment that exceeds what's proportionate for compliance. From a resource-allocation standpoint, I'd want to see this scaled down: build the minimal compliant logging layer on top of existing infrastructure, skip the open-source community verification (that's a PR exercise, not an R&D efficiency gain), and drop the \"immutable ledger\" if it means building custom infrastructure when existing append-only databases suffice.\n\nBut wait - I need to judge strictly on its own merits. The proposal as written commits us to:\n- Building a new software layer (engineering cost)\n- Integrating across ALL high-risk pipelines (integration cost, ongoing maintenance)\n- Secure immutable ledger (infrastructure cost)\n- Open-sourcing and maintaining community engagement (ongoing resource drain)\n\nThe promise from my vantage point: The unified logging idea is genuinely valuable for efficiency (one system instead of N), but the implementation as described is over-engineered and has significant ongoing maintenance costs. I'd give it maybe a 55-60. It's not terrible, the core idea is sound, but the packaging adds too much non-compliance overhead that will drain our limited engineering resources away from actual research.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The fact that it says \"modular API wrapper\" suggests they're thinking about minimal integration cost per new model. And \"across all our high-risk project pipelines\" means we don't have to revisit this decision for each new project. From a pure resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, building once and reusing is better than building per-project. The open-source