        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2666\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal misidentifies Article 12 as the source of risk-classification criteria (they actually reside in Article 6 and Annexes I & III), which means a rubric 'based on the criteria outlined in Article 12' would be legally unsound from inception. Additionally, framing the primary benefit as avoiding engineering/storage spend inverts the compliance hierarchy: if a system is high-risk, logging is mandatory regardless of cost, so an internal committee whose stated purpose is to determine which systems can skip those obligations creates self-classification exposure that could leave us non-compliant with penalties up to 7 % of global turnover.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 25\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2666) from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n\"Project Risk Classification Rubric. An internal governance framework used to categorize projects as high-risk or low-risk based on the criteria outlined in Article 12. A cross-functional committee will review project charters at the onset to determine which specific logging and record-keeping requirements apply. This ensures we only allocate expensive engineering and storage resources to systems that actually require them for legal compliance.\"\n\nFrom a Compliance Officer's perspective, let me think through this carefully:\n\n**Key concern with this proposal:**\n\n1. **Mischaracterization of the regulatory trigger**: Article 12 applies to \"high-risk AI systems\" — but the determination of whether a system is high-risk is NOT made by internal criteria outlined in Article 12 itself. The risk classification is determined by Annexes I and III of the Act (Article 6). Article 12 merely states what high-risk systems must do regarding logging. A \"risk classification rubric based on the criteria outlined in Article 12\" is fundamentally confused about where the classification criteria actually live. The compliance officer would flag this as a regulatory misreading.\n\n2. **Self-classification risk**: Having an internal committee \"determine which specific logging and record-keeping requirements apply\" is inherently risky from a compliance standpoint. If we self-classify a system as low-risk when it should be high-risk, we face significant regulatory penalties (up to 7% of global turnover or €35 million under Article 99). The EU AI Act doesn't delegate the risk classification determination to internal committees in this way. The classification is objective based on the Act's annexes and intended purpose.\n\n3. **However, there IS a legitimate compliance function here**: Understanding which systems are high-risk IS necessary for compliance planning. A cross-functional review at project onset is good governance practice. But the framing as \"ensuring we only allocate resources to systems that actually require them\" suggests an optimization mindset that could conflict with compliance obligations. From my vantage point, I'd want to ensure that when in doubt, we err on the side of implementing logging (compliance-first), not cost-optimization-first.\n\n4. **The \"based on criteria outlined in Article 12\" is a critical error**: Article 12 doesn't outline classification criteria. It outlines what high-risk systems must log. The classification is done under Articles 6, Annex I, and Annex III. If this rubric is built on the wrong article as its basis, it will produce incorrect classifications, which from my compliance vantage point is a serious institutional risk.\n\n5. **The resource-optimization framing**: \"This ensures we only allocate expensive engineering and storage resources to systems that actually require them for legal compliance.\" This language suggests the primary driver is cost avoidance rather than regulatory compliance. From my perspective as Compliance Officer, this is a dangerous inversion of priorities. The compliance obligation exists regardless of cost. If a system IS high-risk, we MUST implement logging. Cost considerations are secondary and should never drive us to under-implement.\n\n**My assessment:**\n- The general concept (internal governance framework for risk classification at project onset) has merit as a compliance process.\n- However, the specific framing is legally incorrect (citing Article 12 as source of classification criteria).\n- The resource-optimization language inverts the compliance hierarchy.\n- An internal committee \"determining\" applicability introduces self-classification risk without clear escalation to external regulatory guidance when uncertain.\n- From institutional survival perspective, getting this wrong could mean non-compliance and massive penalties.\n- From market reputation perspective, if regulators find we were using a flawed rubric that under-classified systems, it signals poor governance.\n\nI think this proposal has a legitimate kernel (governance framework for risk classification) but is built on incorrect regulatory premises and has the wrong incentive structure. As a Compliance Officer, I would reject this in its current form because:\n1. It misidentifies the source of classification criteria (Article 12 doesn't contain them).\n2. It frames compliance as cost optimization rather than obligation fulfillment.\n3. It could create false confidence that we've \"handled\" classification when the framework is legally unsound.\n\nHowever, I should note there IS value in having a structured internal process for risk assessment. The proposal isn't entirely worthless — it's just built on the wrong foundation and has the wrong priority framing. From my vantage point, I'd want this redone with correct regulatory basis (Articles 6, Annexes I & III) and compliance-first (not cost-avoidance-first) orientation.\n\nDecision: Reject. The proposal as written would create more compliance risk than it solves due to its fundamental misreading of where classification criteria live and its cost-optimization framing that inverts the compliance hierarchy.\n\nPromise score: This is tricky. There IS a legitimate need for what this proposal gestures at (structured internal governance for risk classification). But as written, it's based on incorrect regulatory premises. From my vantage point, I'd give it maybe 25-30 — there's a valid underlying need but the execution as described would be actively harmful to compliance posture if implemented without major corrections.\n",
