oader scientific impact:**\n- If successful, these metrics and frameworks could be adopted beyond the EU AI Act context — useful for any deployment of complex AI systems.\n- The work on log granularity and error detection could inform system design principles more broadly.\n- It contributes to the understanding of model stability and failure modes, which is a core concern in ML science.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal is somewhat broad and ambitious for what might be a single research project. \"Mathematical frameworks\" + \"ablation studies\" + \"various high-risk AI architectures\" is a lot of scope. But this doesn't disqualify it — it just means execution will need to be careful.\n- There's a risk it becomes too application-specific (tied to EU Act compliance) rather than generating transferable scientific knowledge. However, the text explicitly frames it as advancing \"global scientific understanding,\" and the ablation studies on granularity are genuinely generalizable.\n- The connection between log structure and detectability of behavioral shifts is an underexplored question with real intellectual depth.\n\nOverall: This is a genuinely interesting research proposal that sits at the intersection of ML systems science, observability theory, and risk detection. It has clear scientific questions, falsifiable hypotheses (ablation studies), and potential for broader impact beyond regulatory compliance. The intellectual merit is solid — it's not just engineering implementation but asks \"what do we mean by a meaningful event?\" and \"how much information must be logged to detect failure?\" which are legitimate scientific questions.\n\nI'd rate this as promising from the knowledge frontier perspective. It's not the most glamorous ML research, but it addresses a real gap and has clear methodological rigor built in (ablation studies). The broader impact on understanding model stability is meaningful. I'd give it around 72-78. Let me settle on 74 —