 R&D deliverables and compliance obligations.\n- The proposal frames the primary benefit as \"broaden scientific impact\" and \"shaping how the global community implements EU AI Act requirements.\" That's a prestige/ambition framing, not an efficiency one. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is a nice-to-have that doesn't directly serve our compliance deadline or operational needs.\n- The scope is vague: \"interoperable data formats for reference databases and verification logs\" — this could balloon into a multi-year standards effort with no clear endpoint. Standards development is notoriously slow and politically charged (who gets to define what?).\n- We already have existing R&D infrastructure. The question is whether we can adapt it to Article 12 requirements with internal engineering effort, or whether we truly need to wait for an external standard to be defined first. Building our own logging/traceability capability now while a consortium slowly debates schemas would leave us non-compliant in the interim.\n- \"Lead\" implies organizational overhead: governance meetings, version control of the spec, community management, responding to feedback from multiple academic labs. This is not a small task for a lab with finite staff.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what internal compliance work this actually accelerates. It's more of an ecosystem play than a direct implementation step.\n\nMy assessment: From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is a high-overhead, low-immediate-return activity. Leading a consortium to define open standards is a multi-year commitment with uncertain timeline and significant coordination costs. While the long-term benefit of interoperable formats is real, it doesn't address our immediate compliance obligation under Article 12 (which requires us to have logging capabilities NOW for our high-risk systems). The \"scientific impact\" framing suggests this is more about positioning and prestige than about efficiently meeting our regulatory deadline with existing infrastructure.\n\nHowever, I should note: if the lab already has staff who are doing traceability work anyway, contributing a schema definition as a byproduct could be low marginal cost. But the proposal explicitly says \"lead a consortium,\" which escalates the commitment well beyond just documenting what we're already building.\n\nDecision: reject. The resource commitment of leading a multi-lab consortium to define open standards is disproportionate to our immediate compliance needs and diverts scarce engineering capacity from directly implementing Article 12 logging in our own systems. It's an ambitious but poorly-leveraged use of limited R&D resources when we have concrete, time-bound regulatory obligations to meet.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 30-35. There's genuine long-term value in interoperable standards, and if it were a \"participate in\" rather than \"lead\" role, or if it were scoped as \"document the schema we're already building for internal use,\" it would be more attractive. But as framed — leading a consortium with open-ended scope for community impact — it's a resource drain relative to our core obligations.\n",
