ding this middleware + tamper-proof ledger + CI/CD integration is a non-trivial software engineering project. It requires dedicated engineers for a period before it delivers value. Given funding constraints, this is a real investment.\n2. **\"Tamper-proof ledger\"**: This could be over-engineered depending on the actual regulatory requirement. Article 12 says \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" — it doesn't explicitly mandate blockchain or cryptographic immutability. A standard append-only log with access controls and audit trails might suffice, reducing complexity and cost.\n3. **Scope risk**: \"Integrates directly into the model inference engine\" could be more invasive than \"hooks into API calls.\" If it requires modifying the inference engine itself, that's a deeper engineering commitment. But the text says middleware hooks into API calls, which is less invasive — good.\n4. **Maintenance burden**: Once built, who maintains this framework? It becomes shared infrastructure with ongoing support costs. This needs to be factored into resource allocation.\n5. **\"All high-risk projects\"**: The scope is broad. If we have many high-risk projects, the ROI is higher. If few, the fixed cost may not justify it. But as a lab developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, presumably multiple projects will need this.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a well-conceived infrastructure investment that directly addresses regulatory requirements while protecting researcher time and leveraging existing CI/CD pipelines. The modular, once-built-many-uses design is exactly the kind of resource-efficient approach I'd want to see. The main risk is over-engineering (tamper-proof ledger vs. adequate logging) and the upfront development cost before it pays off. But for a lab with multiple high-risk AI systems, this is clearly more efficient than each project team handling compliance individually.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's not perfect — I'd want to pu