ng requirement entirely — that's a compliance failure.\n- \"Allocating logging resources only where legally mandated\" — this is correct in principle but the boundary isn't always clean. Some systems evolve; an experimental prototype today might become a deployed product tomorrow. The classification needs to be revisitable.\n- From a prototyping speed standpoint, having a clear triage step early IS valuable because it prevents me from over-engineering logging into every prototype (which slows iteration) while also flagging which projects need the Article 12 technical design built in from day one.\n\n**Technical viability assessment:**\n- The proposal itself is low-cost to implement — it's essentially a review process, not an engineering build. Very technically feasible.\n- It directly supports my core concern: speed of prototyping (by not over-building) and quality (by ensuring high-risk projects get proper logging architecture from the start rather than retrofit).\n- However, it's somewhat indirect for my domain — it's more of a program-management/governance activity. But it has a clear technical payoff: better architecture decisions made earlier.\n- The risk is that \"triage\" becomes a bureaucratic gate that slows down prototyping if not kept lightweight.\n\nOverall: This clears my bar. It's technically trivial to implement (a review process), it directly improves my ability to prototype at speed by reducing wasted logging infrastructure work on non-high-risk systems, and it ensures I architect logging correctly from the start for high-risk ones. The main risk is classification errors, but that's more of a legal function's problem than mine. From my engineering vantage point, this is a net positive for prototyping velocity.\n\nI'd rate this around 72-78. It's useful and technically easy to execute, but it's somewhat indirect for my specific domain (it's more program management). The technical payoff is real but secondary. I'll go with 74.\n",
