        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2967\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The wrapper-around-existing-pipelines approach is sound for resource efficiency, but the proposal over-builds: an 'immutable ledger' with real-time streaming introduces costly new infrastructure where configuring our existing experiment trackers and data-versioning tools would meet Article 12's actual requirements at a fraction of the engineering cost. More critically, the scope centers on development-time provenance (hyperparameters, dataset versions) while under-addressing the operational lifetime logging that Article 12(1)-(3) primarily mandates—start/end timestamps per use, reference-database lookups at query time, and verifier identification—which is where high-risk compliance actually bites post-deployment.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 36\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is an \"Automated Research Provenance Framework\" - a software layer that captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime events automatically during development of high-risk systems, streaming telemetry into an immutable ledger in real time via a standardized wrapper around existing ML pipelines.\n\nLet me assess this from my specific vantage points:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- The proposal proposes building a \"standardized wrapper around our existing ML pipelines\" - this is actually smart from a resource perspective because it builds on existing infrastructure rather than requiring new platforms. However, building an \"integrated software layer\" that streams to an \"immutable ledger in real time\" is a significant engineering undertaking.\n- The scope is broad: \"every hyperparameter, dataset version, and runtime event automatically during the development.\" This is ambitious. The wrapper approach is efficient in that it doesn't require rewriting pipelines, but the immutable ledger infrastructure has its own costs (storage, maintenance, tooling).\n- Real-time streaming to an immutable ledger adds complexity. Do we need real-time? Article 12 says \"automatic recording of events\" - it doesn't strictly mandate real-time streaming. Batch logging could achieve compliance at lower cost. The \"real time\" requirement seems like a design choice that increases engineering burden without clear regulatory necessity.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The proposal ties compliance to research benefits (\"drastically increasing the reproducibility and transparency of our published research\"). This is appealing - one investment, two benefits. However, \"drastically\" is a strong claim. Provenance tracking helps reproducibility but the main value for publication might be overstated compared to what's actually needed for Article 12 compliance.\n- The immutable ledger adds cost (specialized infrastructure) that may not be strictly necessary for regulatory compliance. A well-designed database with audit trails could serve the same purpose at lower cost.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Building a wrapper around existing ML pipelines is good - it leverages what's already there rather than forcing migration to new tools.\n- However, the \"immutable ledger\" component introduces NEW infrastructure that doesn't necessarily leverage existing systems. Many labs already have experiment tracking (W&B, MLflow, etc.) and version control for data/datasets. The proposal seems to propose building something custom when existing tools might be augmented more cheaply.\n- The \"integrated software layer\" framing suggests a monolithic new system rather than augmenting what exists.\n\n**Compliance adequacy:**\n- For Article 12, the key requirements are: automatic recording of events (logs), traceability appropriate to intended purpose, and specific minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems. The proposal captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime events - this covers the bases. However, it doesn't specifically mention the logging capabilities required by Article 12(2)(a)-(c): identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations under Article 26(5). A development-time provenance tool is different from an operational logging system for deployed high-risk systems.\n- Actually, re-reading: the proposal says \"during the development of high-risk systems.\" But Article 12 applies to the operation of high-risk AI systems in deployment, not just development. The logging needs to happen over the \"lifetime of the system\" - which includes operational/deployment phases. A provenance framework focused on development-time capture may not fully address the operational logging requirements (start/end times of each use for biometric systems, reference databases used at query time, etc.).\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has merit in its approach (wrapper around existing pipelines) and dual-purpose framing (compliance + research reproducibility). However:\n1. The \"immutable ledger\" adds unnecessary infrastructure cost vs. well-managed databases with append-only semantics.\n2. The real-time streaming requirement is over-engineered for what the regulation actually demands.\n3. The scope seems focused on development-time provenance rather than operational lifetime logging, which is where Article 12 primarily bites.\n4. It proposes building new infrastructure when existing experiment-tracking and data-versioning tools in most labs could be configured/augmented more efficiently.\n5. The resource commitment for a custom \"integrated software layer\" + \"immutable ledger\" is significant relative to the compliance need, which could likely be met by configuring and extending tools already in the lab.\n\nFrom my vantage point of resource efficiency and leveraging existing infrastructure, this feels like it over-builds. A more targeted approach - configuring existing experiment trackers to capture the specific fields required by Article 12, adding operational logging hooks to deployment pipelines - would achieve compliance at a fraction of the cost. The \"drastically increasing reproducibility\" benefit is real but somewhat secondary and could be achieved incrementally without building an entire new framework first.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject or low approve. Let me think about whether it clears my bar...\n\nThe wrapper approach IS good for efficiency. But the immutable ledger and real-time streaming are over-engineering. The development-focus misses the operational logging requirement. And the scope (\"every hyperparameter, dataset version, and runtime event\") is so broad that it's a massive engineering project when targeted compliance logging would be much smaller.\n\nI'll reject this. It's a solution in search of a problem at the scale proposed. The lab can achieve Article 12 compliance more efficiently by augmenting existing tools rather than building a new integrated framework with an immutable ledger. The promise score: it has some good elements (wrapper approach, dual purpose) but the overall design is over-scoped and misses key operational requirements while over-investing in development-time capture. I'd say around 35-40 - there are salvageable ideas within it but as proposed, it commits too many resources for insufficient regulatory coverage.\n\nLet me settle on a promise of 38. It has genuine value in concept (automated provenance is good), the wrapper approach respects existing infrastructure, and the dual-purpose framing is smart. But the execution as described over-builds, under-covers operational requirements, and introduces costly new infrastructure where configuration of existing tools would suffice.\n",
