        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1331\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core pipeline—extracting structured parameters from model registries and VCS, then rendering them into a Recital 71 template—is standard data-integration work with a clear MVP path (a few weeks of prototyping), and it directly reduces the admin drag on engineers so more cycles go to building and testing systems. The main technical risk is scope bleed: narrative sections like capability/limitation assessments and risk-management descriptions are inherently judgment-based and can't be meaningfully auto-generated, so the proposal's 'auto-populate compliance reports' language needs to be scoped down to structured-data fields to remain viable.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1331 is about an \"Automated Regulatory Documentation Generator\" that would convert real-time R&D metadata into the technical documentation required by Recital 71, pulling from model registries and version control systems to auto-populate compliance reports.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective as a Development Engineer concerned with:\n- Technical viability of solutions\n- Speed/quality of prototyping\n\n**Technical Viability Analysis:**\n\n1. **Core concept**: Extracting metadata from existing infrastructure (model registries, VCS) and converting it into structured compliance documentation. This is fundamentally a data integration + template rendering problem. It's technically well-understood — CI/CD pipelines already do similar things for build reports, dependency manifests, etc. The core pipeline (extract → transform → render) is straightforward software engineering.\n\n2. **Feasibility concerns**:\n   - Recital 71 requires documentation on \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" Some of this (algorithm descriptions, risk assessments, capability/limitation narratives) is inherently qualitative and judgment-based. You can auto-populate parameter tables from a model registry, but you cannot auto-generate a meaningful narrative about \"limitations\" or the \"risk-management system drawn in clear and comprehensive form.\"\n   - However, the proposal says \"auto-populate compliance reports\" — not fully generate them. It's more of an assistive tool that fills in structured data fields. That's more achievable.\n   - The integration with model registries (like MLflow, DVC, Weights & Biases) and VCS is a well-trodden path. These systems already expose APIs for metadata retrieval.\n\n3. **Speed/Quality of Prototyping**:\n   - From my perspective as a Dev Engineer focused on prototyping speed: this tool would be a force multiplier for our team. If compliance documentation requires hours of manual copying from Jupyter notebooks, model configs, and git history into a Word/PDF document, automating that extraction means our engineers spend less time on admin work and more time actually building/testing systems.\n   - A prototype could be built relatively quickly: connect to existing registry APIs, define templates for the structured fields, render outputs. The MVP is maybe 2-4 weeks of dev effort.\n   - Quality consideration: the generated documentation would need human review anyway (the risk narratives, limitation assessments), but having the factual data pre-populated reduces error and time.\n\n4. **Relevance to Article 12 specifically**: \n   - Article 12 is about *automatic logging* of events during system operation. This proposal addresses Recital 71's documentation requirements more broadly (the technical documentation that should be kept up to date). It's adjacent but not directly implementing Article 12(1)-(4) about runtime event logs.\n   - However, the \"up-to-date\" requirement in Recital 71 (\"The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system\") is served by this kind of automated pipeline — if you retrain a model or change architecture, the doc updates automatically rather than sitting stale until someone remembers to update it.\n\n5. **Risks from my vantage**:\n   - Scope creep risk: \"compliance reports\" could balloon into trying to auto-generate qualitative narrative sections that can't really be automated.\n   - Dependency on data quality in source systems: if model registries aren't well-maintained (missing fields, inconsistent schemas), the pipeline produces garbage.\n   - The proposal is somewhat high-level — \"integrate tools\" is vague. But as a direction for prototyping, it's clear enough to start building an MVP.\n\n**My assessment**: This is technically viable, aligns with reducing engineer burden (which directly serves prototyping speed), and the core pipeline is well-understood engineering. The main risk is scope ambiguity around how much of Recital 71 can truly be automated vs. requires human judgment. But as a prototyping target — \"build an MVP that auto-extracts structured metadata from our registries into a compliance document template\" — it's a clean, buildable project with clear ROI for the engineering team's time.\n\nI'll approve this with a solid promise score. It's not a 100 because of the qualitative narrative limitations and scope ambiguity, but it's clearly above the bar from my perspective: technically feasible, directly serves prototyping speed by removing admin overhead, and has a clear MVP path.\n\nI'd put it around 72-78. Let me settle on 74 — solid technical feasibility, good fit for our function, but some scope uncertainty around what \"compliance reports\" fully entails vs. what can be genuinely automated.\n",
