        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1733 is about \"Research into Log-Based Model Interpretability\" — using the mandatory logs required by Article 12 to develop new methods for explaining AI decisions through causal inference and temporal analysis.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- The core idea is to use logs (which we're going to build anyway per Article 12) as a data source for interpretability research. This is an interesting repurposing of mandatory infrastructure.\n- However, there's a fundamental technical question: can you actually reverse-engineer model decisions from event logs? Logs typically record I/O events, timestamps, database references — they don't capture internal feature contributions, attention weights, or intermediate activations. Causal inference on black-box inputs/outputs is possible in principle (e.g., SHAP-like approaches), but calling this \"log-based\" interpretability is somewhat misleading if the logs only contain what Article 12 mandates (start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, verification person IDs).\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, the mandated logs are quite narrow: period of use, reference database, input data that matched, and verification persons. That's not a rich dataset for causal inference about model internals.\n- The proposal conflates \"recording events\" with having enough granularity to do meaningful interpretability. Article 12 logs are designed for traceability and post-market monitoring, not for capturing the internal decision pathway of a model.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- This is framed as a \"research project\" — it's exploratory, open-ended. It has no clear deliverable tied to compliance or product development.\n- From my vantage point, approving this would mean committing implementation resources to an R&D project with unclear technical feasibility and no direct path to shipping something that helps us meet Article 12 or build a compliant system faster.\n- The \"transforming a legal requirement into intellectual contribution\" framing is appealing for publications but doesn't serve the engineering goal of building compliant systems quickly.\n- There's also a risk: if we build logs specifically to enable interpretability research, we might over-engineer the logging system beyond what Article 12 requires, adding complexity and slowing down prototyping of the actual compliance features.\n\n**Key concerns from my angle:**\n1. Technical feasibility is questionable — Article 12's mandated log fields are quite limited for doing causal inference about model behavior. You'd need to supplement with much richer instrumentation (feature attributions, intermediate state captures) which goes well beyond what Article 12 mandates and enters a different engineering scope entirely.\n2. This is pure research with no defined prototype or deliverable timeline. From a speed-of-prototyping standpoint, it doesn't move the needle on getting a compliant system to market.\n3. The proposal risks conflating \"we have logs\" with \"logs contain enough information to do interpretability.\" In practice, the Article 12 log schema is designed for audit/traceability, not for model introspection.\n\nHowever, I should give credit where due: the idea of leveraging mandatory infrastructure for secondary research value isn't crazy, and if we're building logging pipelines anyway, some side-channel analysis capability could be added at low marginal cost. But as framed — a dedicated \"research project\" with \"advanced causal inference techniques\" — this is well beyond what my function should commit to. It's not technically viable in the near term with Article 12's prescribed log fields, and it doesn't contribute to speed or quality of prototyping compliant systems.\n\nDecision: reject. The technical foundation (using Article 12 logs as an interpretability data source) is underdeveloped — the mandated log content is too sparse for meaningful causal inference about model behavior. And from a prototyping/speed perspective, it's an open-ended research effort with no clear engineering deliverable.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 20-25. The kernel of the idea (repurposing compliance infrastructure) has some appeal, but the technical viability is weak as stated, and it doesn't serve my core mandate.\n",
