        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2055 is about conducting a formal internal review process to categorize all current and planned projects based on EU AI Act risk tiers, mapping the R&D portfolio against Annex III to identify which systems trigger Article 12 mandates, repeated quarterly.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- This is fundamentally essential for regulatory compliance. Before we can comply with Article 12 (record-keeping/logging requirements), we MUST know which of our systems are actually high-risk and therefore subject to it. You cannot implement logging capabilities in a system you haven't identified as high-risk.\n- The quarterly repetition addresses the reality that R&D portfolios evolve - new research paths may cross into high-risk territory.\n- It directly serves institutional survival: deploying a high-risk system without required logging is a direct violation of Article 12(1), which carries significant penalties (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for non-compliance with other provisions).\n- It's a prerequisite for all downstream compliance work - you can't implement what you haven't scoped.\n- The involvement of both legal AND technical leads is appropriate - classification requires understanding both the regulatory text and the actual system architecture/functionality.\n- Mapping against Annex III specifically is the correct approach since Article 12(3) has specific additional requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification).\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Quarterly\" cadence - is this sufficient or too rigid? In a fast-moving R&D lab, new projects can start between quarters. However, quarterly is reasonable as a minimum; the process should also allow ad-hoc classification when new projects are initiated. But this isn't a dealbreaker.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what happens after classification - it's essentially a scoping exercise. But that's fine because it IS a prerequisite step. You need to know what you're building compliance into before you build it.\n- It's described as \"formal internal review process\" which implies governance structure, documentation of the classification decisions (which themselves become records relevant to traceability under Recital 71), and accountability.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThis is arguably one of the most fundamental steps in any compliance program for the EU AI Act. Without knowing WHICH systems are high-risk, you literally cannot comply with Article 12 because you don't know what needs logging capabilities built in. The \"catastrophic failure\" language isn't hyperbolic - deploying a biometric system (Annex III) without the specific logging requirements of Article 12(3) would be a direct, easily-detectable violation.\n\nFor market reputation: having a documented, systematic classification process demonstrates to regulators and customers that we take compliance seriously, which is a positive signal.\n\nThis clearly clears my bar. It's a prerequisite for virtually everything else in the compliance program related to Article 12. I'd approve it with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is fundamental infrastructure for compliance. I'd rate it very high - maybe 90-95. The only slight deduction is that quarterly cadence alone might not be sufficient (should also trigger on new project initiation), and the proposal doesn't explicitly address how classification decisions are documented as part of the technical documentation file (which Recital 71 requires to be kept). But these are refinements, not disqualifiers. I'll go with 92.\n",
