        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2732) from the vantage point of the Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P2732:\n\n\"Regulatory Engineering Budgetary Allocation. A revised resource allocation model that earmarks a fixed percentage of every project's funding specifically for compliance infrastructure. We will integrate a mandatory compliance cost assessment into the initial grant proposal phase to ensure logging and documentation are funded as distinct line items. This prevents regulatory requirements from cannibalizing funds originally intended for scientific exploration and experimentation.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The core insight is valid: if compliance costs are not budgeted explicitly, they will be absorbed by whatever budget line is most convenient, which in practice means eating into the funds meant for actual research/experimentation. This is a real problem in R&D labs.\n- Making compliance a distinct line item at the grant proposal phase is a structurally sound approach to resource allocation. It forces visibility and prevents silent erosion of research budgets.\n- \"Fixed percentage\" is simple, predictable, and easy to administer — reduces overhead in budget planning.\n- Integrating it into the initial grant proposal phase means compliance is baked in from day one rather than being an afterthought that derails projects mid-stream.\n\n**Weaknesses / concerns:**\n- A \"fixed percentage of every project's funding\" is a blunt instrument. Not all AI systems developed at the lab will be high-risk under the Act. Some might be limited risk or minimal risk, which have much lighter (or no) record-keeping obligations under Article 12. Applying a uniform percentage to ALL projects would over-fund compliance for non-high-risk work and potentially under-fund it for complex high-risk systems where logging infrastructure is genuinely expensive.\n- \"Fixed\" is problematic because the cost of compliance infrastructure varies enormously depending on whether you're building a biometric identification system (Annex III 1(a), which triggers paragraph 3's specific logging requirements) versus a medical device AI. A flat percentage doesn't account for this variance.\n- That said, the proposal says \"fixed percentage\" but also says \"mandatory compliance cost assessment into the initial grant proposal phase.\" There's a tension: if you're doing a proper cost assessment per project, why fix the percentage? The fixed percentage seems to be a simplification that might undermine the very assessment it proposes.\n- However, from a pure resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, the principle of ring-fencing compliance costs is strong. It protects scientific ambition by preventing regulatory work from being an unfunded mandate on top of research budgets.\n- \"Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — this proposal doesn't really address leveraging existing infrastructure (e.g., shared logging platforms, centralized documentation systems). It's purely about budget structure.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe fundamental principle is sound and well-aligned with my domain: separating compliance costs from research costs is a legitimate resource-allocation strategy that protects both scientific ambition AND regulatory compliance. The risk of not doing this is that compliance becomes a hidden tax on every project, making it harder to fund ambitious work.\n\nHowever, the \"fixed percentage\" mechanism is too crude and potentially counterproductive for an R&D lab with diverse projects. A better approach would be the cost assessment component (which they do include) but without forcing it into a rigid fixed-percentage bucket that doesn't differentiate between project types. Still, as a starting framework that creates accountability and visibility, it has real merit.\n\nThe proposal is directionally correct but mechanically imperfect. It addresses a genuine pain point in resource allocation at an R&D lab. I would lean toward approving it because the core structural change (compliance as distinct line item, assessed upfront) is valuable, even if the \"fixed percentage\" detail needs refinement during implementation. The principle of preventing regulatory costs from cannibalizing research funds is exactly what my domain cares about.\n\nDecision: approve — but with moderate promise score reflecting that the mechanism needs refinement while the principle is sound.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. The principle is right and addresses a real resource-allocation failure mode, but the fixed-percentage mechanism is too blunt for an R&D lab's diverse portfolio. It's promising enough to back as a framework that will need tuning, but not so clean that I'd give it 90+.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
