mpliance engineering.\n\n**Arguments against (resource efficiency perspective):**\n- Building a \"unified software library\" is itself a significant upfront investment of engineering resources. The lab has limited implementation resources.\n- \"Mandatory API hooks\" and embedding into \"core runtime environment\" requires coordination across all active and future projects — this is a large organizational change with transition costs.\n- The \"immutable ledger\" infrastructure has ongoing operational costs (storage, security maintenance, access management).\n- There's a risk of over-engineering: if the lab has few high-risk AI systems currently, building a full framework might be premature compared to lightweight per-project solutions.\n- However, the proposal says \"across all high-risk AI projects\" — if there are multiple, the framework approach is clearly more efficient than N separate implementations.\n\n**Balance assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is actually a strong proposal IF the lab has multiple or growing high-risk AI systems (which it does, being an R&D institution subject to EU AI Act). The upfront cost of building one framework is amortized across all projects. It reduces long-term compliance burden, eliminates redundant work, and integrates with existing infrastructure by embedding into the runtime rather than requiring separate parallel systems.\n\nThe key resource question: Is the lab already building per-project logging (wasteful) or does it have an existing logging infrastructure this could build on? The proposal says \"eliminates fragmented logging practices\" which implies current state is fragmented — so there IS waste to capture.\n\nThe \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" angle: embedding into the core runtime means leveraging what's already deployed rather than building parallel systems. This is efficient.\n\nThe main risk from my vantage point is the upfront engineering commitment and the \"imm