blem. Not novel, not particularly hard architecturally. The hard part is ensuring completeness of the generated documentation against regulatory requirements.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- From a prototyping standpoint, this is relatively straightforward to build an MVP: pull metadata from MLflow API, parse git log, template into a document format. A prototype could be built in days to weeks.\n- BUT — and this is critical — the value proposition (\"minimizes manual administrative burden on scientists\") oversells what's achievable. The pipeline would automate maybe 30-50% of documentation (the structured metadata parts: model versions, hyperparameters, data references). The qualitative assessments (limitations, risk analysis narrative, algorithm descriptions in \"clear and comprehensive form\") still need human authoring. So the \"focus on innovation rather than paperwork\" promise is partially illusory.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective: yes, you can get a working pipeline quickly. But the question is whether it solves enough of the problem to justify the resource commitment vs. simpler approaches (e.g., structured templates with auto-filled fields that scientists complete).\n\n**Article 12 specific concerns:**\n- Article 12(1) requires \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is about runtime logging, not experiment tracking. The proposal conflates experiment metadata (pre-deployment) with operational logs (post-deployment). These are different technical domains. A compliance dossier generated at checkpoint time does NOT satisfy Article 12's requirement for automatic event recording during system operation.\n- However, the proposal references Recital 71 which covers both technical documentation AND logging. So it's addressing part of the picture but not the full operational logging requirement.\n\n**My professional judgment:**\n- Technically viable to build: Yes, an MVP is achievable relatively quick