ols.\n- However, the legal compliance angle is non-negotiable - we MUST meet Article 12 requirements. So the question isn't \"should we do this?\" but \"what's the most resource-efficient way to do it?\"\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThe proposal identifies a real requirement and scopes it appropriately. The core functionality (logging who verified what and when) is straightforward. However, I have concerns about:\n1. Whether building a new \"specialized interface\" is the most efficient use of resources vs. extending existing logging/audit infrastructure.\n2. The scope is very narrow - only for Annex III 1(a) systems. If we're going to invest in verification auditing, should it be designed with some modularity for other Article 12 requirements?\n3. The framing as \"institutional survival threats\" is appropriate urgency but doesn't change the resource calculus much.\n\nOn balance: The requirement is mandatory (shall), so something must be done. The proposal's scope is reasonable and bounded. The technology is well-understood and low-complexity. From a pure resource efficiency standpoint, building a minimal, focused module for this specific requirement is actually efficient compared to over-engineering a grand system. The main concern would be whether it leverages existing infrastructure or builds new - but the proposal doesn't specify that level of detail; it's at the right abstraction level for an ideation output.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It addresses a mandatory compliance requirement with a well-scoped, bounded solution using well-understood technology. The resource commitment is moderate and justified by legal obligation. I'll approve it but give a moderate-high promise score since while it's necessary, the implementation details matter for efficiency (reuse vs. build new).\n\nLet me settle on a promise score: It's a compliance necessity, so there's no question of \"do we need this?\" But from my resource perspective, it's a bounded, achievable task with