h background flusher), and choose a serialization/compression format. That's an engineering project, not research.\n- \"State-of-the-art inference speeds\" is a red flag for me. The EU AI Act doesn't require you to maintain SOTA inference speeds — it requires automatic event recording. If the overhead of logging is, say, 2-5% on modern hardware with proper async design, that's not really a problem worth a \"dedicated research initiative.\" You just engineer around it.\n- The proposal conflates \"regulatory compliance\" with \"scientific ambition.\" From my perspective as a dev engineer, I'd want to prototype the logging pipeline early and measure actual overhead rather than front-loading a research phase. If overhead turns out to be negligible (which is likely for most inference workloads), this entire project was unnecessary.\n- The framing \"prevents regulatory requirements from compromising scientific ambition\" suggests an adversarial relationship between compliance and performance that I don't think exists in practice. Proper async logging at the infrastructure level adds microseconds, not milliseconds of meaningful overhead to inference.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- A dedicated research project would slow down our ability to get a compliant prototype working. The faster path is: implement a straightforward synchronous or simple-async logger that captures what Article 12(3) requires, measure the actual overhead in our specific pipeline, and only optimize if measurements show a real problem.\n- By the time \"research\" produces results into async architectures (which are well-understood), we'd have spent months on something that could have been prototyped in weeks with straightforward engineering.\n\n**My decision:**\nThis is a case where I see the underlying concern as valid but the proposed approach as misaligned. The problem doesn't warrant \"research\" — it warrants measurement and straightforward engineering. From my vantage point, committing scarce implementation resources to an open-ended research project on well-known patterns (async logging, compression) would slow our prototyping velocity without clear justification. I'd reject this as a standalone research initiative but would support embedding performance considerations into the actual compliance implementation from day one.\n\nI'll rate it around 35-40. The problem space is real and relevant to my domain, but the approach (dedicated research project on well-known engineering patterns) doesn't align with how I'd actually want to solve this in a prototyping context. It's not zero because if we did have genuinely novel high-frequency inference workloads where even async logging creates bottlenecks, some investigation into specialized techniques could be warranted — but that would come after measurement, not as a preemptive research phase.\n\nI'll go with 35 and reject.\n",
