        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2487\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Placing a single, bounded logging-verification gate at the existing beta-testing milestone is well-timed: it catches Article 12 gaps before any significant deployment investment is sunk, leverages the project lifecycle infrastructure we already run, and avoids the far costlier alternative of retrofitting logging or facing EU enforcement action post-launch. The main efficiency caveat is that the text says 'any system' rather than scoping the gate strictly to high-risk systems, which risks adding a low-value checkpoint for non-high-risk projects; in practice this should resolve to a quick pass/fail, but it does introduce an incremental dependency on compliance-office throughput that could become a bottleneck if staffing doesn't scale with our pipeline.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2487 from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is a \"Regulatory Traceability Audit Protocol\" — a mandatory compliance gate in the project lifecycle requiring formal record-keeping sign-off before any system moves to beta testing. It involves:\n1. A verification checklist based on Recital 71 and Article 12\n2. Developers must provide technical demonstrations of logging functions\n3. Institutional approval for deployment requires this sign-off\n4. Implemented by the compliance office\n\nFrom my Lab Director vantage point focused on resource-allocation efficiency, scientific ambition vs. funding constraints, and effective use of R&D infrastructure:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- A single gate before beta testing is actually efficient — it's one checkpoint rather than scattered compliance checks throughout development. This minimizes rework.\n- It prevents costly late-stage compliance failures (rebuilding logging at deployment would be far more expensive than verifying at a mid-point).\n- Integrating into existing project lifecycle means leveraging existing infrastructure (project management workflows) rather than building new systems from scratch.\n- The \"technical demonstration\" requirement is concrete and bounded — developers show what they built, it's not an open-ended audit.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- It adds a sequential gate that could delay scientific progress. If the compliance office becomes a bottleneck, projects stall waiting for sign-off. This impacts resource allocation efficiency if the office can't scale.\n- The proposal says \"the compliance office will implement\" — this is an additional operational dependency. Does the lab have sufficient compliance staffing? Adding this to existing compliance workload could strain resources.\n- However, it's a one-time gate per system (before beta), not continuous monitoring. That's relatively low overhead compared to ongoing audits.\n- The risk of NOT doing this is severe: releasing non-compliant high-risk AI into the EU market carries significant legal and financial penalties. From a resource-efficiency standpoint, preventing a multi-million-euro fine or forced recall is far cheaper than implementing logging verification early.\n- It's mandatory for ALL systems moving to beta — but wait, only HIGH-RISK AI systems are subject to Article 12. The proposal says \"no high-risk AI is deployed without Article 12 capabilities\" which correctly scopes it. But if the gate applies indiscriminately to all projects (including non-high-risk), that's wasteful. Re-reading: \"requires a formal record-keeping sign-off before any system moves to beta testing\" — this could be read as applying to ALL systems, not just high-risk ones. That would be inefficient. But then it says \"This ensures no high-risk AI is deployed without Article 12 capabilities\" which suggests the intent is scoped to high-risk systems. The ambiguity here is a minor concern but the text does say \"any system moves to beta testing.\"\n\nActually, re-reading more carefully: \"requires a formal record-keeping sign-off before any system moves to beta testing. This ensures no high-risk AI is deployed without Article 12 capabilities.\" The first sentence says \"any system\" which is broader than necessary. But the purpose statement clarifies it's about high-risk AI compliance. In practice, for non-high-risk systems, this gate would be a quick pass (no logging requirements to verify). So the overhead might be minimal for most projects while being critical for the few that are high-risk.\n\n**Resource allocation assessment:**\n- The cost is relatively bounded: a checklist, a sign-off meeting, a technical demo. This isn't building new infrastructure or hiring large teams.\n- It leverages existing project lifecycle gates (beta testing is already a milestone).\n- The return on investment is avoiding regulatory penalties and rework.\n- From an efficiency standpoint, catching logging gaps at beta rather than post-deployment is the most resource-efficient timing.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\n- This doesn't constrain what science we pursue; it constrains how we ship. Scientific work can proceed to alpha/early development without this gate.\n- It's a reasonable, proportionate requirement that doesn't fundamentally alter research trajectories.\n\nOverall: This is a well-scoped, resource-efficient compliance measure. The main risk is if the \"any system\" language creates unnecessary overhead for non-high-risk projects, but even then it should be a quick formality. The gate is at an appropriate point in the lifecycle (before beta = before significant deployment investment). It's not building new infrastructure; it's adding a checkpoint to existing processes. From my vantage point, this clears my bar — it's proportionate, efficient timing, leverages existing workflows, and prevents costly downstream rework or legal exposure.\n\nI'll approve with a solid promise score. Maybe 72-78 range. It's clearly the right thing to do and is efficiently structured, but the \"any system\" language and the added dependency on compliance office capacity give it some friction. I'll go with 74.\n",
