umentation. Regulators need substantive understanding, not just change logs.\n- The proposal focuses heavily on the technical documentation aspect but doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3)'s specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): recording of use periods, reference databases, input data matches, and verification personnel identification. These are operational logging requirements that go beyond what a CI/CD documentation pipeline would naturally capture.\n- \"Real-time reports on system capabilities\" - this is somewhat vague. Capabilities and limitations require expert assessment, not just automated generation from code changes. A model retrained with new data doesn't automatically mean the compliance officer can understand the capability implications without human review.\n- The proposal says it \"ensures our technical documentation remains current\" but in practice, automation can create a false sense of completeness. Regulators may still require human sign-off and substantive risk assessment updates that go beyond what automated pipelines capture.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This is highly aligned with what I need to demonstrate to regulators: we have systematic processes for maintaining documentation currency. It shows institutional commitment rather than ad-hoc compliance.\n- Market reputation: having an automated pipeline signals to business partners and regulators that we take compliance seriously at the engineering level.\n- However, it's not a complete solution for Article 12 compliance alone - particularly the operational logging requirements in paragraph 3 for biometric systems. But as a component, it addresses the documentation currency requirement strongly.\n- The risk is over-reliance on automation: if the automated pipeline produces incomplete or inaccurate documentation, and we rely on it without human review, that could be 