t.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is highly prototypable. You can build a working MVP quickly: hook into your existing model registry, pull data manifests from DVC or similar, run validation scripts, and template the output into a structured document (PDF, JSON, HTML). A prototype could be in production in weeks, not months.\n- The \"speed of prototyping\" angle is strong here - this tool directly accelerates the lab's ability to iterate on models while maintaining compliance artifacts. Every model iteration automatically produces its documentation trail. This means engineers don't slow down their work for admin tasks.\n- Quality: The generated docs will be as good as the structured inputs. If validation results are properly recorded, the output is reliable. The risk is \"garbage in, garbage out\" - if someone skips a validation step, the doc will note that (which is actually a feature, not a bug).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Scope creep risk: \"automatically generates technical documentation\" could balloon into trying to auto-write narrative explanations of model behavior, which is much harder. But as stated, it's focused on structured data linking and manifest generation, which is tractable.\n- The proposal says \"as models evolve\" - this implies continuous tracking across model versions, which aligns well with how ML systems actually work (multiple versions in parallel). This is a natural fit for version-control-based approaches.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is technically very viable, builds on existing MLOps patterns, and directly addresses the Article 12 requirement of keeping technical documentation \"up to date\" throughout the system's lifetime. It accelerates prototyping by removing a manual bottleneck. The core engineering is well-understood. I see no fundamental technical blockers. The main risk is scope management (keeping it to structured data linking rather than trying to auto-generate free-form narrative), but as des