., LLMs vs. classical ML vs. hybrid systems) may have very different logging needs. A truly universal middleware could become bloated or force awkward abstractions that slow down research cycles. However, the proposal says it logs \"timestamps, input matches, and data references\" which are relatively architecture-agnostic fields.\n2. The proposal mentions logging \"input matches\" which is specifically relevant to Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification). For other high-risk categories, the relevant events differ under Article 12(2)(a)-(c). A truly universal framework would need to be parameterizable per intended purpose, not just one-size-fits-all. This could add complexity.\n3. \"Without manual intervention\" — while the automatic capture is good, some logging requirements (like identification of natural persons in verification under 12(3)(d)) may still require human-in-the-loop data that a middleware can't fully automate. But this is a minor point; the middleware can prompt for it.\n4. Resource commitment: Building and maintaining a universal middleware framework is a significant engineering investment upfront. However, compared to building per-model logging solutions, it's clearly more efficient at scale. The DevOps team maintaining it as shared infrastructure is the right allocation — compliance infra shouldn't consume research FTEs.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: HIGH. One build serves all models. This is the most cost-effective approach vs. N separate logging implementations.\n- Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints: The proposal explicitly frees researchers from compliance plumbing, letting them focus on science while DevOps handles the regulatory burden. This is a good balance.\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: It integrates with *current* pipelines rather than requiring new ones. Good fit.\n\n**Verdict:** This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. The main risk is over-engineering (trying to be truly universal when architectures diverge significantly), but the core concept — shared compliance logging infrastructure maintained by DevOps, integrated into existing pipelines, freeing researchers — is exactly what an R&D lab director would want. It's a high-leverage investment that amortizes compliance cost across all projects.\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. High because the resource-efficiency argument is compelling and it aligns perfectly with using existing infrastructure. Slightly below 90+ because \"universal regardless of architecture\" carries some over-engineering risk, and the Annex III specificity (1(a) requirements vs. other categories) means the framework needs to be somewhat flexible/parameterized, which adds complexity that isn't fully addressed in the proposal text. But fundamentally, it's a sound architectural decision for resource-constrained R&D.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
