f a regulator finds we cannot produce logs, that's a direct violation of Article 12. The penalties under the AI Act for non-compliance with high-risk system requirements can be up to €35M or 7% of global turnover. Catching gaps internally before a regulator does is a core compliance function.\n- Market reputation: demonstrating proactive internal verification of our logging infrastructure signals maturity to regulators, clients, and partners.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- It's periodic — frequency matters. If \"periodic\" means annually, there could be long windows where a degraded system goes undetected. But the proposal doesn't specify frequency, so it's not inherently flawed.\n- \"Random point\" is good but might miss systematic issues (e.g., if logging for a particular event type is consistently broken). However, as a complement to other monitoring, random sampling is sound.\n- The scope is specifically about retrieval of traceability records — this is well-scoped and doesn't overreach.\n\n**Resource considerations:**\n- This is a focused, bounded exercise. It requires the compliance team plus engineering support to simulate an audit request. It's not building new infrastructure; it's testing existing systems. Relatively low resource commitment for high assurance value.\n- It's not \"funding to explore whether an idea might someday work\" — it's a concrete operational practice with clear methodology and deliverables.\n\n**My assessment:** This is squarely within my domain of concern. Article 12 creates an ongoing obligation that degrades over time (logs must persist, systems change, infrastructure updates can break logging). A periodic verification exercise is not optional — it's the natural corollary to having a record-keeping obligation. Without some form of verification, we're flying blind on whether our core compliance mechanism actually works. This protects institutional survival (avoids catastrophic non-compliance findings), supports regulatory compliance