er, add compliance-specific fields, and have a working demo in days/weeks.\n  - It doesn't require novel AI research; it's infrastructure engineering.\n- The \"speed\" angle: moving from manual to automated documentation directly reduces the time engineers spend on compliance paperwork vs. actual prototyping work. This is a force multiplier for our team's throughput.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Digital management system\" could become a scope-creep monster if not tightly bounded. But as stated, it's focused on linking artifacts to releases - that's a narrow, well-defined problem.\n- The proposal says \"ensures we meet the requirement for up-to-date documentation without incurring excessive manual labor costs\" - this is a reasonable engineering goal. The technical challenge is ensuring the automation actually captures what the regulation requires (especially the Article 12(3) specifics about biometric systems, if applicable).\n- One risk: \"automatically links... to specific model releases\" assumes we have good versioning discipline already. If our current prototyping culture doesn't maintain clean release boundaries, the tool will just encode chaos. But that's a process issue, not a technical viability issue.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-scoped proposal that directly addresses Article 12 requirements. From my vantage point:\n- It's feasible with existing or near-existing technology (high confidence).\n- It can be prototyped quickly because the problem space is well-understood infrastructure engineering.\n- It has a clear success criterion (automated linkage working end-to-end for a representative model release).\n- It improves our team's throughput by reducing manual documentation overhead.\n\nI'd rate this as quite promising from my perspective. It's not groundbreaking, but it's exactly the kind of pragmatic, well-scoped infrastructure work that I can get a prototype running fast and iterate on with confidence. The main risk i