xists.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal explicitly integrates into existing shared training pipelines — good, not starting from zero.\n- Recording hyperparameters, data versions, and execution timestamps is relatively standard ML engineering practice (MLflow, DVC, or similar tools already do this). So the \"new\" part might be less than it appears if we build on existing MLOps tooling.\n- The dual purpose (regulatory compliance + research reproducibility) means researchers get value beyond just ticking a regulatory box. This improves adoption and reduces friction.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Scope: \"across all high-risk projects\" — how many do we actually have? If it's 2-3 projects, a centralized framework might be overkill. If it's 10+, it makes sense. As Lab Director, I'd need to know the count, but the proposal seems reasonable for a lab with multiple high-risk projects.\n- The proposal doesn't specify build vs. buy. Using existing MLOps platforms (MLflow, Weights & Biases, DVC) could achieve much of this more cheaply than building custom APIs. However, the regulatory requirements are specific enough that some customization is needed.\n- \"Comprehensive audit trail for reproducing experimental results\" — this is a nice co-benefit but also scope creep. The core requirement is Article 12 compliance; reproducibility tooling is a separate (though related) concern. Conflating them might inflate the project scope and cost.\n\n**Verdict:**\nThis is fundamentally a sound, necessary proposal. Article 12 is mandatory for high-risk systems. A centralized approach leveraging existing pipelines is resource-efficient. The dual-use benefit (compliance + reproducibility) improves ROI on the engineering investment. My main concern is that \"centralized system\" and \"API-driven logging layer\" could be interpreted as a from-scratch build when off-the-shelf or semi-off-the-shelf MLOps tools might get us 80% there at 20% of the cost. But the proposal as stated is directionally correct and efficient.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's necessary (regulatory requirement), reasonably scoped, leverages existing infrastructure, and provides dual value. I'd dock points slightly because the framing suggests more custom development than might be needed, and the scope (\"all high-risk projects\") should be validated against actual project count.\n\nI'll give it around 78-82. Let me settle on 80 — it's clearly necessary and well-directed, but the implementation path could be leaner than described.\n",
