ructure. From my vantage, I'd want this to be part of a broader implementation plan, and I'd want it delivered in a form that maps directly to regulatory obligations (Article 12(2)(a)-(c), Article 79(1) risk definitions, Article 72 post-market monitoring triggers).\n\nStill, the core idea — creating a formal taxonomy of loggable events mapped to Article 12(2) purposes — is something I would support. It reduces regulatory ambiguity for us and provides a defensible basis for our logging design decisions. The \"prevent over-logging\" angle also has compliance value (data minimization under GDPR intersects with AI Act logging).\n\nI'll approve it, but with moderate confidence because it's framed as research rather than implementation, and it doesn't directly build the compliance artifact (the actual logging system or the technical documentation section). It's a useful input to compliance work but not sufficient on its own.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 62-68. It's clearly relevant and useful for my domain, but it's an intermediate/research deliverable rather than a direct compliance implementation, and the framing as \"intellectual merit of safety research\" makes me slightly wary that it might produce an academic paper rather than a regulatory artifact I can hand to an ENF during inspection.\n\nLet me settle on 65.\n",
