        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1270\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The underlying instinct—avoid building full Article 12 logging infrastructure for throwaway prototypes—is sound from an engineering standpoint, but the proposed mechanism is a governance taxonomy and living-document process rather than a technical architecture. More critically, 'delaying full record-keeping until deployment readiness' invites expensive retrofits: if logging isn't architected into the data pipeline early (retention windows, event correlation, input-data capture per Art 12(3)), retrofitting it mid-sprint will cost far more engineering time than a minimal logging scaffold built from week one. The proposal also inserts a classification decision-gate ('which tier are we in?') before I can start building, which adds friction to the exact exploratory phase it claims to protect.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 30\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P1270 is about creating a \"Dynamic Risk Classification Taxonomy\" that would categorize AI modules by risk level based on development stage, allowing them to toggle logging intensity based on project maturity and delay full record-keeping until deployment readiness.\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nKey considerations:\n\n1. **Technical viability**: The proposal is essentially an internal governance/classification framework, not a technical solution per se. It's a process/administrative mechanism. From a development engineering standpoint, the question is whether this actually translates into something technically implementable or if it's just a policy document that adds bureaucratic overhead without clear engineering deliverables.\n\n2. **Speed/quality of prototyping**: The core promise is \"prevents the compliance tax from stifling early-stage exploration by delaying full record-keeping until a project reaches deployment readiness.\" From my vantage point, this is actually appealing in principle — I don't want to be building full logging infrastructure for every exploratory prototype that will never ship. However, there are serious concerns:\n\n   - **The \"toggle\" concept is technically problematic**: Article 12(1) says high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" The obligation attaches to the *system*, not to a project stage. If I'm building a prototype that will eventually become a high-risk system, the logging architecture needs to be designed in from the start or it becomes an extremely painful retrofit. \"Delaying full record-keeping until deployment readiness\" could mean you discover at week 12 of a 16-week sprint that your data pipeline wasn't architected for the required log retention, event correlation, etc. That's a massive rework cost.\n   \n   - **Classification ambiguity**: Who decides when a project \"reaches deployment readiness\"? This is inherently fuzzy and will create disputes between engineering (who wants to keep iterating) and compliance/legal. The \"living document\" approach adds maintenance overhead without clear technical deliverables.\n\n   - **The framework itself has no technical content**: It's a taxonomy/classification exercise with legal collaboration. From my perspective as someone who needs to build working systems, this doesn't tell me *what* to build or *how*. It tells me when I need to build things, which is useful, but the mechanism (a \"living document\" maintained with legal) is not something that accelerates prototyping — it's a governance artifact.\n\n   - **Risk of misclassification**: If we classify something as \"exploratory\" and then it gets picked up by a business unit for deployment, we've been non-compliant the whole time. The cost of that discovery in production is far higher than building logging from day one with appropriate architecture.\n\n   - **What's actually useful here**: The core insight — that not every internal experiment needs full Article 12 compliance overhead — is valid and I agree with it. But the mechanism proposed (a taxonomy + living document + legal collaboration) doesn't translate into engineering acceleration. What WOULD help me technically is a clear technical architecture pattern: \"here's what minimum logging scaffolding you need at prototype stage vs. full production stage\" with concrete API specs, database schemas, etc. This proposal doesn't deliver that.\n\n   - **Speed of prototyping**: Adding a classification step before I can start building (\"which tier are we in? do I need logs now?\") actually *slows down* prototyping for the exploratory work. It adds a decision gate. And the \"living document\" needs to be consulted and updated, which is overhead.\n\n3. **Quality**: The proposal conflates risk classification (a legal/regulatory determination) with development stage (an engineering reality). These don't always align cleanly. A system at \"research\" stage might already be processing real user data in a pilot, making it functionally high-risk even if internally classified as exploratory.\n\nMy assessment: The underlying concern is legitimate — I don't want to waste engineering cycles building full compliance logging for throwaway prototypes. But the proposed mechanism is a governance/policy artifact, not a technical solution. It doesn't give me concrete architecture guidance, it adds classification overhead, and the \"delay until deployment\" approach risks expensive retrofits. From my vantage point on technical viability and prototyping speed, this proposal is more likely to create friction than remove it.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge that the *principle* of staged logging intensity has some merit — you could argue for a minimum viable logging layer at prototype stage that's cheap to implement but captures enough to avoid total rework later. But this proposal doesn't specify any of that technical content.\n\nDecision: I'm leaning toward reject. It's a policy/governance idea dressed in technical language, it doesn't give me anything buildable, and the \"delay\" mechanism is technically risky (retrofit cost). The useful kernel (staged logging) isn't well-served by this particular framing.\n\nPromise score: Maybe 30-35. The instinct is right but the execution as described would not help my team prototype faster or build more viable solutions. It's a document, not a technical artifact.\n",
