istent logging eliminates the \"I forgot to add that log\" problem, which improves the quality of compliance artifacts.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal says \"across all R&D pipelines\" - this is a big integration surface. If we have 50 different pipeline types, building adapters for all of them is significant work. But modular design mitigates this.\n- There's a risk this becomes a \"big bang\" infrastructure project that delays actual prototyping in the short term while promising long-term gains. However, if scoped as incremental (core library first, then pipeline integrations), it's manageable.\n- The technical approach is sound and proven. No exotic tech needed. This is very viable.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a technically straightforward, well-motivated infrastructure investment. From a prototyping speed perspective, the initial build has a cost but the ongoing benefit (every new project gets compliance logging for free) is real and significant. The modular API approach shows good engineering sense. I'd approve this - it's low technical risk, well-scoped in its architecture, and directly serves the Article 12 requirement in the most maintainable way possible.\n\nThe promise score: It's a solid, proven engineering approach with clear value for prototyping speed (once built). The main risk is execution scope creep, but the design addresses that. I'd say around 78-82. It's not a 90+ because it's infrastructure investment with upfront cost and the \"all R&D pipelines\" language could stretch timelines. But it's clearly viable and well-designed for my domain concerns.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
