rchitectures\" in real-time is genuinely hard and potentially an open research problem. Committing implementation resources (not just exploration funding) to something that might not reach peer-review quality while also meeting compliance deadlines is dangerous.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The proposal doesn't mention leveraging existing infrastructure at all. It talks about developing a new framework, implementing a graph-based tracking system. This sounds like building from scratch rather than extending what the lab already has. For an R&D institution that likely already has some logging, monitoring, and data pipeline infrastructure, starting from zero seems wasteful.\n\n4. **Scope vs. actual regulatory need**: Article 12 requires automatic recording of events (logs) with specific capabilities for traceability, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. The proposal goes well beyond this - \"data lineage,\" \"system states to model outputs,\" \"every transformation from raw input to final inference\" is much broader than what's required. This over-engineering risk means we'd be spending resources on capabilities that aren't legally required, while potentially under-delivering on the specific compliance items in Article 12(3) (for Annex III 1(a) systems: start/end times, reference database, input data matches, natural person identification).\n\n5. **Open-source aspect**: While open-sourcing could generate goodwill and external contributions, it also means maintaining a public API surface, dealing with community expectations, security reviews for public code, etc. This adds ongoing resource costs that may not align with the lab's core mission.\n\n6. **\"Real-time\" constraint**: Recording \"every transformation... in real-time\" is technically demanding and potentially expensive in terms of compute/storage. For a lab operating under funding constraints, this could be prohibitively costly at scale without clear ROI beyond compliance