e, appropriately throughout the lifetime.\" A CI/CD-triggered update on each commit is sufficient and much simpler than a true real-time system.\n- The scope mentions linking git commits AND dataset changes directly to compliance dossiers. Dataset changes can be complex (large files, incremental updates) and may not map cleanly to git-style versioning without additional infrastructure.\n- \"Minimizes the risk of outdated records during audits\" - this is a reasonable benefit but the proposal frames it in terms of \"market reputation and legal standing,\" which is somewhat generic. The actual compliance requirement (Article 12, Recital 71) is about traceability and enabling post-market monitoring. The tool should be evaluated on whether it actually supports those specific regulatory functions, not just general audit readiness.\n- However, for high-risk AI systems under Annex III point 1(a), the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) (recording periods of use, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of verifying persons) are operational logs, not really technical documentation. This proposal seems more targeted at the technical documentation side (Recital 71's mention of \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations... algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\") rather than the automatic event logging required by Article 12(1). So it addresses one aspect but not the full scope of Article 12.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a solid, practical infrastructure proposal that addresses a genuine compliance need with efficient use of existing tools. It's not scientifically ambitious (nor does it need to be) - it's protective infrastructure. The resource cost is moderate and the benefit (avoiding audit failures, reducing recurring manual documentation work) is concrete. The main risk is scope creep around \"real time\" and the dataset-change integration complexity. But as a concept, it's well-aligned with efficient use 