stems where you need to record \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" Knowing exactly which database version was in use at any point is essential compliance evidence.\n- \"Training logs via unique IDs\" — supports the requirement to document training processes and maintain records throughout lifetime.\n- CI/CD integration ensures this isn't a one-time documentation exercise but continuous, automatic recording over the system's lifetime (Article 12(1)).\n\n**Concerns / limitations:**\n- The proposal focuses on \"reproducing failed experiments\" which is more of an R&D productivity framing rather than a compliance framing. However, the technical substance still serves compliance.\n- It doesn't explicitly mention the logging of \"events\" in the operational sense (start/end times of use, identification of natural persons for verification). Those are Article 12(3) requirements specific to Annex III 1(a) systems that this proposal doesn't directly address.\n- However, the metadata store and lineage registry infrastructure is a necessary foundational layer upon which those operational logs can be built. Without knowing which model version was deployed, you can't properly attribute operational logs.\n\n**From institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- Having cryptographically verifiable chain of custody is strong evidence for auditors and regulators. It demonstrates good faith compliance beyond minimum requirements.\n- CI/CD integration means this scales with the lab's output — important as they develop more systems.\n- This reduces risk of a systemic compliance failure (e.g., \"we can't tell which model version was in production on date X\") which could trigger enforcement action or market withdrawal orders.\n\n**Decision:**\nThis is a strong, well-scoped infrastructure proposal that directly supports multiple Article 12 requirements and Recital 71 obligations. It's not the whole compliance picture (doesn't cover op