al logs at all. It's purely about the development phase documentation.\n- The proposal is framed as a \"reporting system\" and \"digital ledger where researchers must log\" — this sounds like manual, human-driven record-keeping rather than the technical capability for automatic event recording that Article 12(1) mandates.\n- However, it does address part of Recital 71: keeping records and technical documentation up to date throughout the lifetime.\n- It doesn't mention post-market monitoring logging (Article 12(2)(b)) or operation monitoring (Article 12(2)(c)).\n- The \"before moving to the deployment phase\" gate is a reasonable compliance control but incomplete — Article 12 requires logs OVER THE LIFETIME, not just pre-deployment.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n\nThis proposal addresses a real and important part of the compliance obligation (technical documentation / development records per Recital 71), but it's significantly incomplete relative to Article 12's actual text. The critical gap is that Article 12(1) mandates AUTOMATIC event logging over the system's lifetime, which this proposal doesn't address. It's a manual reporting/ledger system, not a technical logging capability. A regulator examining compliance would look for the automatic logging mechanism first.\n\nHowever, it does represent a meaningful and necessary piece of the overall compliance picture. The development documentation and traceability it provides is genuinely required by Recital 71 and supports Article 12(2)'s traceability goals. It's not a waste of resources — it's a legitimate component. But approving it as if it \"satisfies\" the requirement would be misleading, because it only partially addresses the obligation.\n\nAs compliance officer: I'd approve this because it addresses a real gap (development documentation and provenance tracking) that is explicitly required by Recital 71. The technical documentation IS part of the Article 12 ecosystem (Recital 71 explicitly says \"This requires keeping records AND the availability of technical documentation\"). But my promise score should reflect that it's partial — it handles the development-phase documentation but not the runtime automatic logging that is the core of Article 12(1).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal claims to \"satisfy Recital 71's requirement.\" From a compliance perspective, this is an overstatement. Recital 71 covers both technical documentation AND automatic recording capability. This proposal only addresses the former. But as a standalone component of the broader compliance program, it IS necessary and valuable.\n\nDecision: Approve — because it addresses a genuine, required piece of the compliance obligation (technical documentation and development traceability), even though it's not sufficient on its own for full Article 12 compliance. It's not a waste; it's a necessary building block. But my promise score should be moderate because it's partial and the framing slightly overstates what it achieves, and it doesn't address the critical automatic logging requirement.\n\nI'll give it around a 62-65. It's clearly useful and aligned with the regulation, but incomplete for full compliance, and the \"satisfies Recital 71\" claim is an overreach that could create false comfort internally.\n",
