ment.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This directly leverages what we already have (GPU cluster). It's not asking for new servers, new software licenses, or new headcount necessarily.\n- The \"segregated\" aspect is important — it prevents compliance testing from contaminating production environments or interfering with primary development.\n- The \"without slowing down the primary development branch\" claim suggests good architectural thinking about decoupling.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- What fraction of the GPU cluster? If it's a large partition, that could meaningfully reduce capacity for core R&D. But the proposal is framed as \"a specific partition,\" suggesting a bounded, defined allocation.\n- Is this truly a new build or repackaging existing QA/testing infrastructure into a compliance-specific frame? If we already have test environments, this might be more of an organizational/relabeling exercise than a genuine new resource commitment. That would actually make it cheaper to approve.\n- The \"stress tests on Article 12 requirements\" framing is reasonable — logging capabilities need to be verified under load to ensure they work in practice, not just in theory.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal scores well:\n- Uses existing infrastructure (no new capital)\n- Addresses a mandatory legal requirement (not discretionary)\n- Early identification of gaps is cost-effective\n- Segregated environment protects primary development\n- Bounded resource commitment (\"a specific partition\")\n- The \"financial risk mitigation\" framing is honest — it's cheaper to find logging bugs in a sandbox than in production\n\nThe main question mark is the exact size/cost of the partition and whether we already have something like this under a different name. But as stated, the proposal is well-aligned with my mandate. It's efficient, uses what we have, addresses a non-negotiable requirement, and the early-detection framing is sound econom