        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2458\",\n      \"rationale\": \"While efficient logging infrastructure is operationally relevant to our compliance burden under Article 12, the proposed R&D track commits a dedicated team to explore compressive-sensing and hashing techniques that are technically misaligned with what the regulation actually requires—readable, queryable records of specific events (timestamps, reference databases, input data, verifier identities) over the system's lifetime. Standard structured-logging engineering already meets these obligations at scale; funding an exploratory research track for novel compression methods risks producing artifacts that cannot satisfy traceability demands and diverts scarce R&D capacity from higher-leverage work where our infrastructure genuinely has a gap.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 32\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me evaluate this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P2458 suggests creating a new R&D track to develop \"low-overhead, privacy-preserving logging mechanisms\" using \"compressive sensing or hashing techniques\" for traceability in large-scale AI systems. It mentions dedicating \"a small team.\"\n\nLet me think through this from my resource-allocation and efficiency perspective:\n\n**Arguments for:**\n- Article 12 requires automatic logging over the lifetime of high-risk AI systems. If we're building these systems, we need to implement compliant logging anyway. Understanding efficient approaches is directly relevant to our operational needs.\n- The proposal positions this as \"small team\" which suggests contained resource commitment.\n- It aligns with the regulatory necessity — we HAVE to do compliance logging regardless, so investing in R&D around how to do it efficiently could save implementation costs later.\n- It connects to our scientific mission of advancing AI infrastructure efficiency, which is a legitimate research direction.\n\n**Arguments against:**\n- \"Compressive sensing or hashing techniques\" for traceability raises immediate technical concerns. Compressive sensing is about reconstructing signals from fewer measurements — applying it to logging means you might not be able to retrieve the actual event data when needed. Article 12 requires recording specific events (start/end times, reference databases, input data, identification of persons). You can't meaningfully \"compress\" away the actual log entries and still comply with traceability requirements. The regulation demands that logs enable identifying situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations — these require readable, queryable records, not compressed representations.\n- \"Privacy-preserving logging\" is interesting but somewhat orthogonal to Article 12's core requirement. Privacy in logging is more of a data protection concern (GDPR) than an AI Act record-keeping concern. The lab might conflate two different regulatory frameworks here.\n- Creating a \"new R&D track\" with a dedicated team, even small, means opportunity cost. What else could that team be doing? If we're a resource-constrained lab, spinning up a new research direction is expensive in terms of coordination overhead, onboarding, and diverting people from existing commitments.\n- The framing as \"research into efficient traceability architectures\" sounds like exploratory R&D rather than engineering implementation. We need compliance logging WORKING for our systems, not necessarily novel research on how to do it more cleverly. Standard logging infrastructure (structured logs, databases, etc.) already handles this reasonably well at scale.\n- The proposal seems to conflate the regulatory obligation (we must log) with a research question (can we log more efficiently?). We could simply implement standard compliant logging without needing an R&D track. The marginal benefit of \"compressive sensing\" over standard database logging for compliance purposes is unclear and potentially insufficient.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective, this proposal has some merit in that understanding efficient logging is relevant to our operations. However, the specific technical approach (compressive sensing/hashing) seems misaligned with what Article 12 actually requires — which is readable, queryable, complete records of specific events. The \"privacy-preserving\" angle is a different regulatory framework concern. Creating a dedicated R&D track for this when standard engineering solutions likely suffice represents poor resource allocation. We'd be committing scarce research resources to solve a problem that has well-understood, conventional solutions, while the novel approaches proposed may actually undermine compliance (if you can't reconstruct the original log entry from a compressed representation, you haven't \"recorded\" it in any meaningful sense).\n\nThe promise score: There's a kernel of reasonable thought here — we do want efficient logging infrastructure. But as framed, this is an R&D track to explore potentially inappropriate techniques for a problem with conventional solutions, at the cost of diverting resources from other commitments. I'd put this around 30-40. It's not terrible thinking, but it misallocates research effort and the technical approach is questionable for compliance purposes.\n\nLet me settle on my decision: reject. The core issue is that this commits R&D resources (a team, a track) to explore novel techniques when standard engineering suffices for compliance, and the specific techniques proposed may actually conflict with what the regulation requires. From a resource efficiency standpoint, this is not the best use of our limited implementation capacity.\n",
