atency\" constraint is a performance optimization problem, not a research problem per se.\n\n3. **Broader scientific impact**: Would this framework advance the broader field of AI science? Marginally at best - it might create infrastructure that other labs could use, but the content (timestamps, input hashes, database references) doesn't generate new knowledge about how AI systems work or improve them. It's compliance plumbing. The \"single source of truth for system behavior\" framing is useful operationally but doesn't constitute a scientific contribution.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does building this infrastructure enable research? In a limited sense, having comprehensive logs could support post-market monitoring and understanding system drift over time. But the proposal as stated is about compliance, not about generating new knowledge or enabling novel research capabilities beyond what standard logging already provides.\n\nThe proposal is essentially: \"We will build the logging system the regulation requires.\" From my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this is a necessary engineering task but it does not constitute a research contribution. It doesn't advance understanding of AI systems, doesn't explore novel methods, and its impact on the broader scientific community is negligible - any lab can implement standard logging to meet Article 12.\n\nThat said, I should be fair: if implemented well with attention to what events are \"relevant\" (Article 12(2) mentions identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring), there could be some modest intellectual content in deciding WHAT to log and how to structure it for future analysis. But the proposal as written doesn't emphasize this - it focuses on the engineering mechanics (sidecar, immutability, read-only repos).\n\nMy assessment: This is a compliance engineering task that any competent systems team can execute. It has low intellectual merit from a