sented at a project-architecture level rather than a compliance-governance level. It doesn't address:\n- Log integrity/tamper-evidence\n- Retention policies aligned with system lifetime (Recital 71 emphasizes \"throughout the lifetime\")\n- Accessibility for regulatory authorities\n- GDPR data minimization in logs (especially for biometric data)\n- How it interfaces with Article 72 post-market monitoring systems\n- Audit trails for the logging system itself\n\nStill, as a foundational infrastructure project that addresses the core \"shall\" requirement of Article 12(1), this is clearly something we need. The risks of NOT having it (non-compliance, inability to demonstrate conformity, market surveillance findings) far outweigh the risk of approving it. Even with gaps, it's directionally correct and necessary.\n\nMy decision: This clears my bar for approval. It addresses a hard regulatory \"shall\" requirement in a systematic way that protects institutional survival. The promise score should reflect that while I strongly support it, there are compliance-specific gaps that need to be addressed before implementation.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 78-82. It's clearly needed and directionally correct, but as presented it's an engineering solution sketch rather than a fully compliant governance framework. There are important regulatory details missing (retention, GDPR interplay, authority access, tamper-evidence) that would need to be layered on top. But the core concept is exactly right and I'd back it without hesitation in terms of strategic direction - just with conditions on completeness.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
