d hooks for start/end times and input metadata - not an open-ended platform.\n- Does it fully cover Article 12? It mentions start/end times (Art 12(3)(a)) and input metadata (relates to Art 12(3)(b),(c)). The \"reference database\" logging might need some project-specific configuration, but the API structure could accommodate that.\n- Risk: if it becomes a bottleneck or requires changes for each new framework version, it could actually slow things down. But as a middleware layer with defined hooks, this risk is manageable.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped infrastructure investment that directly serves my domain concerns. It's efficient (one build, many users), preserves scientific momentum (removes boilerplate), and leverages existing frameworks rather than requiring new ones. The scope is bounded - it's not \"build a logging platform\" but \"create a thin wrapper with standard hooks.\" This is exactly the kind of force-multiplier investment that makes sense for an R&D lab under regulatory pressure.\n\nI would approve this. It's high promise from my vantage point because:\n- High ROI (amortized across all projects)\n- Low relative cost (lightweight, uses existing infra)\n- Directly addresses the efficiency mandate\n- Doesn't constrain scientific ambition - it accelerates prototyping by removing friction\n- Bounded scope makes it fundable without major budget reallocation\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 82. It's very well-aligned with my concerns, but I can't give it a 90+ because there's some residual risk about long-term maintenance as frameworks evolve, and \"lightweight\" is aspirational until proven. Also, the proposal doesn't mention how it handles project-specific logging requirements (e.g., the specific reference database for Annex III point 1(a) systems), which might require per-project configuration that adds complexity.\n",
