s enormously by use case\n   - Validation that your filtered logging still meets compliance requirements — which is a legal/regulatory judgment, not purely technical\n\n4. **Speed of prototyping:** This is framed as a \"research project\" and \"study.\" From my vantage point, committing scarce implementation resources to a study that may produce a taxonomy that's specific to one deployment context isn't efficient prototyping. It doesn't give you a shippable component or a reusable library. It's an analysis exercise that produces recommendations, not code.\n\n5. **The \"maintaining full compliance\" claim is doing enormous hidden work.** The proposal asserts it can filter noise AND maintain full compliance. But the technical architecture for selective logging (what to record vs. what to drop) introduces complexity: you need a real-time classifier deciding what's \"critical\" at inference time, which adds latency, failure modes, and its own compliance burden. This is technically more complex than just logging everything and storing it cheaply.\n\n6. **Storage costs are relatively trivial compared to the engineering effort.** Modern object storage (S3, etc.) is pennies per GB. The cost of \"irrelevant telemetry\" is minimal. The engineering complexity of building a risk-predictive filter that runs in real-time, handles edge cases, and can be audited by regulators would far exceed the storage savings.\n\n7. **Regulatory risk:** A regulator reviewing your system would ask: \"Why did you choose to log X but not Y?\" Your answer would be \"our research showed Y wasn't predictive of failures.\" This is a defensible position only if the research was robust, and it introduces an additional layer of documentation and justification that actually *increases* compliance burden rather than reducing it.\n\n**From my development/prototyping vantage point:**\n- This doesn't produce a fast, shippable prototype\n- It's technically more complex than the naive \"log everything\" approach