bligation under Article 12(1) is that the system \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" That's an engineering requirement on the AI system itself, not a research project to analyze those logs afterward.\n- However, the analysis tools could be seen as enhancing our ability to meet Articles 72 and 26(5) obligations regarding post-market monitoring and operation monitoring.\n- The risk: if leadership interprets this as \"we're doing research instead of implementing compliance,\" that's a problem. But the proposal explicitly says it uses \"mandated Article 12 logs\" - meaning the logging itself is assumed to be in place. This is an enhancement layer on top of the baseline compliance.\n- The proposal doesn't directly implement Article 12(1) (the technical capability for automatic recording). It's a downstream analytical tool. So it doesn't replace the core compliance obligation but complements it.\n- From a resource commitment perspective: this is a \"new research thread\" which means ongoing personnel allocation. But if it genuinely strengthens our post-market monitoring and incident response capabilities, it has clear compliance value.\n\n**Decision:** As Compliance Officer, I see this as a strong complement to our Article 12 obligations. It directly supports the traceability, post-market monitoring, and risk identification purposes stated in both Recital 71 and Article 12(2). Having automated forensic analysis of logs would:\n1. Strengthen our ability to demonstrate compliance during supervisory audits\n2. Improve our incident response (Article 73) capabilities\n3. Support the continuous monitoring obligations under Articles 26(5) and 72\n4. Protect market reputation by showing we go beyond minimum compliance\n\nThe main caveat is that it's framed as \"research\" rather than a direct engineering deliverable, but the practical output (anomaly detection tools on logs) has clear regulatory utility. I would approve this with a strong pro