ources get pulled into building a meta-tool rather than doing the actual research or implementing direct compliance (the logging capabilities themselves).\n- However, if we need to maintain documentation for multiple high-risk systems over their lifetimes, an automated approach could be more resource-efficient long-term than manual updates.\n- \"Without manual entry\" is somewhat aspirational - technical documentation requires judgment about what's relevant, how to frame limitations, etc. Pure automation may not capture the nuanced scientific understanding needed.\n\n**Resource allocation assessment:**\n- This is a one-time build cost that could amortize across multiple systems and over time\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (VCS, logging) rather than requiring new hardware/funding\n- BUT it competes with other compliance work for engineering resources\n- The \"intelligent\" framing may inflate the scope beyond what's needed - a structured pipeline extracting from VCS and logs could be much simpler\n- For a lab that needs to balance scientific ambition with funding constraints, building an AI documentation generator is a meaningful resource commitment\n\n**Scientific ambition vs compliance:**\n- This frees researcher time (good for science)\n- But the engineering effort to build it diverts resources from research (bad for science short-term)\n- Net effect depends on scale: if we have many systems over long lifetimes, it pays off; if one or two, manual might be cheaper\n\n**Effective use of existing infrastructure:**\n- Yes, builds on VCS and logging that already exist\n- Doesn't require new compute infrastructure necessarily\n- Could be a moderate software engineering effort rather than a major capex item\n\nOverall: This is a reasonable proposal from a resource-efficiency standpoint IF scoped appropriately. The risk is that \"intelligent tool\" becomes an over-engineered project when a simpler automation pipeline would suffice. But the core idea - reducing manual documentation burden by leveraging existing data sources (VCS, logs) - aligns well with protecting research time and using infrastructure we already have.\n\nI think I'd lean toward approving this but with moderate promise score, because:\n- The underlying principle is sound (automate what you can from existing sources)\n- It uses existing infrastructure\n- It protects researcher time long-term\n- BUT the \"intelligent/AI\" framing may over-scope it, and the resource cost of building even a simpler version needs to be weighed against just having a dedicated compliance engineer update docs\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise score around 62-68. It's a reasonable use of engineering resources that leverages existing infrastructure, but the \"intelligent\" scope risks over-investment for what could be a simpler automation task. The resource-allocation efficiency is decent if scoped right, and it does protect research time.\n\nI'll go with 64 - approve but with reservations about scope.\n",
