lementer. So \"mapping to a standardized template\" requires us to first define that template, which is more of a legal/compliance exercise than an engineering one.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core value proposition is eliminating manual labor in drafting compliance reports. This is legitimate — writing technical documentation by hand after each experiment or model iteration is tedious and slows down prototyping cycles.\n- However, the scope is somewhat narrow: it addresses documentation generation, not the actual logging capabilities required by Article 12(1)-(3). The proposal title says \"Record-keeping\" but the content is about technical documentation synthesis (Recital 71), not the automatic event logging that Article 12 mandates.\n- Wait — actually re-reading: Recital 71 does mention \"the availability of technical documentation, containing information which is necessary to assess compliance.\" So it's adjacent to record-keeping but more about documentation than logs. The proposal addresses the documentation aspect, not the logging aspect (Article 12(1) automatic recording of events).\n- For prototyping speed: if we can auto-generate a draft technical doc from our experiment data, that does save time per iteration. But the \"compliance\" part means it needs to be correct and complete, which adds complexity. A wrong or incomplete automated doc is worse than none (creates false sense of compliance).\n- The parser itself is not hard technically. The hard parts are: (1) defining what fields map where in a way that satisfies regulators, (2) capturing information NOT in the experiment tracker (risk management docs, system architecture), (3) maintaining the template as regulations/interpretations evolve.\n\n**My assessment:**\n- Technically viable for the narrow part it addresses (extracting structured data from MLflow/W&B and formatting it into a document). This is maybe 2-4 weeks of engineering work for a basic version.\n- But it oversells what automation can do here — the \"comprehensive form required by Recital 71\" includes qualitative, narrative content that doesn't live in experiment trackers. The proposal implies more automation than is realistically achievable without significant human review.\n- It's not addressing the core Article 12 requirement (automatic logging of events) which is where the real engineering challenge lies for high-risk systems.\n- As a prototyping speed tool: it has modest value. We already have Jupyter notebooks, READMEs, and experiment reports. Automating the *formatting* into a compliance template saves some time but doesn't fundamentally change our prototyping velocity because the bottleneck in prototyping is building/iterating on models, not writing documentation (though documentation is necessary for shipping).\n- The risk: if we build this and it produces incomplete or subtly wrong documents that pass internal review but fail regulatory scrutiny, that's a liability. The \"automated\" part could create a false sense of completeness.\n\nOverall: It's technically feasible in its narrow scope but the proposal overstates what can be automated and doesn't address the harder parts of Article 12 (the actual event logging infrastructure). For my function (technical viability + prototyping speed), I see it as a modest, low-risk tool that saves some documentation time but doesn't meaningfully accelerate our core prototyping cycles. The technical risk is low (it's just data transformation), but the value is also limited and somewhat overstated in the proposal text.\n\nI'll lean toward approving at a moderate promise score — it's technically easy to build, does save real time on documentation, and doesn't add significant complexity to our prototyping pipeline. But I won't give it a high score because: (a) it oversells what can be automated, (b) it doesn't address the core Article 12 logging requirement, (c) its impact on actual prototyping speed is modest.\n\nLet me settle on: approve at