s NOT align with rapid prototyping.\n- The deliverable (a schema) is relatively simple technically, but the process of getting multiple institutions to agree on it adds massive non-engineering overhead.\n- From a prototyping standpoint, we could just build our own logging schema that meets Article 12 requirements in weeks, not years spent in multi-institutional meetings.\n- The \"open-source\" and \"global scientific community\" framing suggests this is more of an advocacy/standards-body activity than a development engineering task. It's more suited to policy or research communications teams.\n\n**Does it actually help us meet Article 12?**\n- Article 12 requires that OUR systems technically allow for automatic recording of events. The practical need is: build logging infrastructure into our high-risk AI systems. A shared schema might be nice-to-have for interoperability, but it's not a prerequisite for compliance. We can comply with Article 12 by building internal logging that captures the required fields without needing a global standard.\n- The proposal doesn't describe any concrete technical solution — no architecture, no data model specifics, no implementation plan. It's aspirational and process-heavy rather than engineering-focused.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping), this proposal is:\n1. Technically feasible but trivially simple in its core deliverable (a schema definition).\n2. Processually heavy and slow (multi-institutional collaboration, standardization).\n3. Not directly necessary for Article 12 compliance — we can build internal logging that meets the requirements without a global standard.\n4. Vague on technical specifics — no mention of format choice, storage architecture, real-time vs. batch logging, integration with model serving pipelines, etc.\n5. The \"open standard\" framing suggests this is more of a policy/reputation play than an engineering deliverable that advances our prototypi