icated user identity)\n2. It introduces a new system boundary where integration with existing logging would be faster to prototype\n3. \"Digital signatures\" adds PKI complexity that isn't strictly required by the regulation text (it says \"identification,\" not \"digital signature\")\n\nHowever, there's value in the concept - ensuring the identification link is robust and auditable. But as proposed, it's a heavyweight solution to what should be a lightweight logging enhancement.\n\nI'll reject it with a moderate promise score - maybe 40-50 range. The problem is real but the solution architecture is wrong for speed of prototyping, and it over-engineers beyond what the regulation text requires.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Rationale: The regulatory need (Article 12(3)(d) identification logging) is real, but a dedicated authentication portal with digital signatures massively over-scopes what's needed; the requirement is to log *who* verified, which can be met by capturing existing SSO identity into event logs as a lightweight module, not by building new PKI infrastructure. From a prototyping speed standpoint, this adds weeks of auth-infrastructure work before you can demonstrate compliance value.\n- Decision: reject\n- Promise: 42 - the underlying problem is valid and I'd want to address it, but this specific architecture would slow us down significantly and over-build relative to what Article 12(3)(d) actually mandates.\n",
