 requirements differ significantly across projects. Article 12(3) specifies different minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems vs. others. A truly universal framework might need to be flexible enough to handle these variations without becoming a Rube Goldberg machine.\n- However, even with this flexibility concern, the core infrastructure (event capture, timestamps, immutable storage) is genuinely shared across all high-risk systems per Article 12(1).\n\nOverall assessment: This is squarely within my domain of concern and hits it well. It's a resource-allocation efficiency play — one investment serves many projects. It's effective use of R&D infrastructure (building shared capability rather than duplicated silos). The upfront cost is real but amortized across all current and future high-risk AI projects. The \"universal\" scope has some risk of over-engineering, but the core value proposition (don't rebuild logging per project) is sound and directly addresses funding constraints by reducing per-project compliance overhead.\n\nI would approve this. It's a strong fit for my vantage point. Promise score: I'd give it around 82-85. It's clearly efficient infrastructure investment, but the \"universal\" ambition carries some risk of scope creep or over-engineering that could consume more engineering resources than the simpler alternative of a lightweight shared library per project category. Still, the core idea is very well-aligned with resource efficiency.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
