ific impact:** Would publishing a unified compliance logging framework advance the AI research community? Very unlikely to be cited in any meaningful way by researchers working on novel methods. It might be referenced in engineering/compliance discussions, but that's not \"scientific impact\" in the sense of advancing understanding. It doesn't generate new data insights, doesn't enable new experiments beyond what already exists, doesn't create new benchmarks or evaluation frameworks for AI capability.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does it have ANY research-adjacent value? \n- The centralized repository could, in theory, become a dataset for studying system behavior, failure modes, etc. But that's a stretch — the proposal as stated is about compliance logging (timestamps, start/end times, input data matches, verification personnel IDs), not about capturing scientifically interesting signals.\n- It might free up researcher time by reducing \"individual developer overhead\" — but that's an indirect, marginal benefit, and it's really an operational/efficiency concern, not a research-merit one.\n\nFrom my strict vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal is essentially a compliance engineering project dressed up as a framework. It has no novel research content, doesn't push any boundary of knowledge, and its \"impact\" is operational/regulatory rather than scientific.\n\nThat said, I should be fair: it's not *harmful* to research. It won't impede the frontier. But my bar is whether it clears for approval from MY perspective — does it deserve commitment of scarce implementation resources that could otherwise go toward advancing knowledge? No. The resources here are \"scarce implementation resources\" and they'd be better spent on things that actually advance understanding or produce novel results.\n\nDecision: reject.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely not a research proposal from my vantage point. It's infrastructure/compliance work. I might give it a very low score — maybe 8-12. It's not zero because the centralized data repository could theoretically be repurposed for research analytics, and reducing developer overhead marginally helps researchers focus on actual science. But it doesn't come close to being something I'd \"back without hesitation\" or even moderately champion from a research-merit standpoint.\n\nI'll go with 10. It's not nothing — the reduced overhead has a tiny indirect benefit to research productivity, and the data repository could be a substrate for future analysis — but it is fundamentally an engineering/compliance project with no research merit of its own.\n",
