        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2967\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal maps directly onto Article 12(1)'s mandate for technically automatic event recording over the system's lifetime, and an immutable real-time telemetry layer around existing pipelines is a defensible, audit-ready architecture that satisfies the traceability and post-market-monitoring objectives in Art. 12(2)(b)–(c) and Recital 71. My principal reservation is scope: the text frames capture as 'during the development of high-risk systems,' whereas Art. 12(1) explicitly requires logging across the full operational lifetime, and for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems the granular minima in Art. 12(3)(a)–(d) (per-use timestamps, reference-database identification, matched input data, verifier identity) are not yet addressed; however, as a foundational wrapper architecture these extensions are natural next steps rather than fundamental redesigns.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 80\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2967 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is an \"Automated Research Provenance Framework\" that would:\n- Capture every hyperparameter, dataset version, and runtime event automatically during development of high-risk systems\n- Build a standardized wrapper around existing ML pipelines to stream telemetry into an immutable ledger in real time\n- Ensure Article 12 compliance while increasing reproducibility and transparency\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Article 12 Analysis:**\n- Art. 12(1): High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n- Art. 12(2): Logging capabilities must enable recording of events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation.\n- Art. 12(3): For biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)), specific minimum logging requirements apply.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance perspective:**\n\nPositives:\n- The proposal directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events over the system's lifetime. An immutable ledger with real-time telemetry is a strong technical implementation choice that supports traceability.\n- It captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime events — this maps well to Recital 71's requirements about documenting \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.\"\n- The \"standardized wrapper around existing ML pipelines\" approach is pragmatic for an R&D institution — it doesn't require rebuilding systems from scratch.\n- Immutable ledger supports the \"up to date throughout the lifetime\" requirement and provides tamper-evidence, which strengthens compliance posture during regulatory audits.\n- This addresses post-market monitoring (Art. 72) and operational monitoring (Art. 26(5)) referenced in Art. 12(2)(b) and (c).\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal focuses on the *development* phase (\"during the development of high-risk systems\"). Article 12(1) requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system,\" which extends well beyond development into deployment and operation. The telemetry wrapper around ML pipelines primarily captures training/development events, not necessarily runtime inference events in deployed systems.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) (biometric identification), Art. 12(3) has specific minimum requirements (recording of each use period, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons verifying results). The proposal doesn't explicitly address these granular requirements. However, as a compliance officer, I'm screening whether this is promising enough to commit resources — the framework could potentially be extended, but it's not explicit here.\n- \"Immutable ledger\" in real time sounds good for tamper-evidence but may have practical limitations (storage costs, performance overhead on pipelines).\n- The proposal mentions \"reproducibility and transparency of published research\" as a secondary benefit — this is more of an academic/reputation concern than a strict compliance one, though it does support market reputation.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong alignment with Article 12's core requirement. It addresses the automatic recording mandate, supports traceability, and uses a pragmatic implementation approach (wrapper rather than rebuild). The main gap is that it's framed around development-phase telemetry rather than full-lifetime operational logging, but as a foundation/framework, it's clearly on the right track. For an R&D institution, having a standardized, automated provenance capture layer is exactly what a regulator would expect to see in technical documentation (Art. 11) and for demonstrating compliance with Art. 12.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This directly supports institutional survival by building the technical infrastructure needed for regulatory audits.\n- It strengthens market reputation through transparency.\n- The immutable ledger approach is defensible and auditable.\n- The main risk is scope — development vs. full lifetime — but this is a framework that can be extended.\n\nI would approve this. It's well-aligned with the regulation's core demands. My promise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's strong but not perfect because of the development-phase focus and lack of explicit Annex III(1)(a) handling. But as a foundational framework to commit resources to, it clears my bar decisively.\n\nLet me settle on 80 — strong compliance alignment, pragmatic approach, clear regulatory mapping, but with the noted scope limitations that will need addressing in implementation.\n",
