        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2347\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core technical problem is real: Article 12(3)(c)-(d) mandates logging input data and verifier identities, which collides directly with GDPR data-minimisation obligations, so a privacy-preserving log architecture is genuinely needed. Cryptographic hashing for integrity-chained logs is mature and quick to prototype (weeks on existing models), but bundling secure multi-party computation into the same scope introduces significant latency overhead and implementation complexity that could slow delivery considerably without being strictly necessary to clear the regulatory bar; the proposal would benefit from an explicit MVP cutline separating 'hash + field-level encryption' (shippable fast) from 'SMPC-based verification' (stretch research), as currently written it risks six months of SMPC tuning before any prototype is in a testable state.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 72\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2347 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Privacy-Preserving Audit Trail Research\" — developing logging methods that maintain regulatory traceability without storing sensitive personal data in plain text, using cryptographic hashing and secure multi-party computation (SMPC) to verify log integrity. They'd prototype on existing high-risk models.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Cryptographic hashing for log integrity is well-understood and technically feasible. Hash-chained logs, Merkle trees — these are mature technologies.\n- Secure multi-party computation (SMPC) is where it gets tricky. SMPC is computationally expensive, adds significant latency, and is notoriously difficult to implement correctly at scale. For a logging system that needs to record events \"over the lifetime of the system\" (Article 12(1)), adding SMPC overhead could be problematic for real-time or near-real-time logging requirements.\n- However, the proposal doesn't strictly require SMPC for all logging operations. You could use hashing for integrity verification and only invoke more complex crypto for specific sensitive fields. This is technically viable but the scope as stated (\"cryptographic hashing AND secure multi-party computation\") broadens things considerably.\n- The tension between Article 12 and GDPR is real: Article 12(3)(d) requires \"identification of the natural persons involved in verification\" — that's personal data you need to log. And Article 12(3)(c) requires logging input data for matches. So there IS a genuine conflict with GDPR data minimization principles.\n- Prototyping on existing high-risk models is reasonable — it gives you a concrete testbed rather than building from scratch.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is framed as an R&D project, not a quick compliance patch. The scope (hashing + SMPC) is ambitious for a prototype timeline.\n- However, the hashing component alone could be prototyped quickly — hash-chained logs are straightforward to implement and test. You could have a working MVP in weeks.\n- SMPC integration would be significantly harder. If the team includes experienced cryptographers, maybe 2-3 months for a basic prototype. Without that expertise, it could drag on.\n- The proposal says \"research\" and \"investigate\" — this is appropriate framing. It's not promising immediate production deployment.\n- Prototyping on existing models gives you real-world constraints to test against (data volumes, latency requirements, storage patterns).\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- SMPC for logging is overkill in most cases and adds complexity that could slow prototyping significantly. A simpler approach (field-level encryption with separate key management, or pseudonymization with a reference table) might achieve 80% of the privacy benefit at 20% of the engineering cost.\n- The proposal doesn't specify which parts are MVP vs. stretch goals. Without that scoping, there's risk of spending months on SMPC when hashing + field encryption would suffice for Article 12 compliance while respecting GDPR data minimization.\n- \"Transforms a compliance burden into a proprietary technological advantage\" — this is marketing language, not an engineering concern per se, but it suggests the project might get pulled in directions that serve PR rather than technical necessity.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea (privacy-preserving logging) is technically sound and addresses a real conflict between Article 12 and GDPR. The hashing component is highly viable and quick to prototype. The SMPC component adds significant complexity but isn't strictly necessary for the regulatory requirement — it's more of a \"nice to have\" or future-proofing element. As an R&D project with prototyping on existing models, this is reasonable. The main risk from my perspective is scope creep toward SMPC when simpler solutions might suffice.\n\nI think I'd lean toward approving but with moderate confidence. It's technically viable, the core problem is real, and prototyping on existing infrastructure is smart. But the SMPC component introduces uncertainty in delivery timeline and adds complexity that may not be needed for the regulatory bar. From a pure \"can we build this quickly and well\" standpoint, it's feasible but the scope as stated makes me slightly cautious about speed.\n\nI'll approve it because:\n1. The technical problem is real (Art 12(3)(c)-(d) requires logging personal data; GDPR says minimize)\n2. Hash-chained logging is a proven, fast-to-prototype solution\n3. Prototyping on existing models is the right approach for speed\n4. Even if SMPC proves too complex, the hashing + encryption path delivers value\n\nBut I won't give it a super high promise because of the SMPC scope risk and the \"research\" framing that could mean unclear deliverables.\n\nPromise: around 72-75. Solid technical foundation, real problem, but scope ambiguity (SMPC vs. simpler approaches) and the research framing introduce delivery uncertainty from my speed/quality perspective.\n",
