igid if different research projects have very different structures. Need flexibility within the framework.\n3. The proposal says it \"aligns development milestones with requirements of Recital 71\" — this is appropriate scope. It's not overreaching into areas outside my function (like legal compliance strategy or post-market monitoring operations).\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped proposal that directly addresses resource efficiency by preventing the most common and costly failure mode in regulatory compliance: retrospective documentation. The funding-gate mechanism aligns financial commitment with demonstrated progress, which is exactly what a Lab Director managing scarce resources would want. It uses existing development phases as natural integration points rather than creating parallel structures.\n\nThe main risk is implementation overhead — adding gates to every phase could slow R&D velocity. But for high-risk AI systems where these records are legally mandated anyway (Article 12(1) requires automatic logging over the system's lifetime), building this in from day one is strictly more efficient than bolting it on later.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a pragmatic, resource-efficient approach to compliance that aligns with how R&D actually works and protects against the most expensive failure mode (discovering at audit time that documentation is missing or incomplete). The funding-gate mechanism is particularly well-suited to a lab managing constrained budgets across multiple competing projects.\n\nDecision: Approve.\nPromise score: I'd say around 82-85. It's strong and well-aligned with my concerns, but the execution risk around gate overhead (potentially slowing iteration cycles in a competitive R&D environment) keeps it from being a perfect 100. The principle is sound; the implementation details will matter a lot.\n",
