ging what already exists rather than building parallel systems. The decorator/middleware approach is non-invasive — it wraps around existing code rather than requiring rewrites. This respects the sunk investment in current research pipelines.\n4. **Scalability**: As the lab takes on more high-risk projects (which the EU AI Act will likely push them to), this framework scales without linear cost growth. Without it, each new project would require a fresh compliance implementation.\n5. **Risk reduction from an efficiency standpoint**: Inconsistent per-project logging implementations create fragmentation, make audits harder, and increase the chance of non-compliance in one corner while another is fine. A unified approach reduces the \"long tail\" of compliance bugs.\n\n**Arguments AGAINST or concerns:**\n1. **Upfront development cost**: Building a robust, regulation-compliant library takes engineering time that could go toward actual AI research. If the lab has few high-risk projects currently, the ROI might be slow to materialize. However, this is mitigated if the lab expects growth in high-risk work (which is likely given regulatory pressure).\n2. **Maintenance burden**: A shared library becomes a maintenance dependency. Bugs in it affect all projects simultaneously. This is a real cost but manageable with good engineering practices.\n3. **\"Regulation-compliant structured format\"**: The regulation evolves, and the library needs updates. But this is actually an argument FOR centralization — one place to update vs. N places.\n4. **Potential rigidity**: A \"standardized\" framework might not fit every project's unique logging needs (e.g., Annex III point 1(a) systems have specific minimums in Art. 12(3)). However, the proposal says \"at a minimum\" and provides a consistent API, so it should be extensible.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is strongly aligned with resource-allocation efficiency. It's a classic \"build once, use many\" infrastructur