esn't explicitly address Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.) — the specific data points required there (period of each use, reference database, input data matches, natural person identifiers) may not be captured by a generic \"event logs, timestamps, and input references\" wrapper. The proposal seems to focus on general telemetry rather than the specific data fields mandated for certain high-risk categories.\n2. \"Compatible with our primary frameworks\" — what about edge cases or newer models that aren't in the primary set? Coverage gaps could create compliance blind spots.\n3. Data retention and access controls: A centralized time-series database holding potentially sensitive input data (especially for biometric systems) raises GDPR concerns and requires careful access management. The proposal doesn't address this, but as a compliance officer I'd flag it as something that needs to be part of the implementation.\n4. The proposal is about infrastructure, not about governance — who validates that the logs are complete? What's the verification process? But this might be beyond the scope of what the proposal commits to.\n5. \"Removes manual burden from engineers\" — while operationally attractive, compliance doesn't just need data capture; it needs meaningful interpretation. But that's a separate function.\n\n**Institutional survival and market reputation angle:**\n- Having a standardized, auditable logging infrastructure is a strong signal to regulators and clients during audits. It demonstrates organizational maturity.\n- If a high-risk system incident occurs, being able to produce consistent, complete logs quickly is critical for limiting liability and maintaining market trust.\n- The \"sidecar\" architecture means it can be updated without touching the AI models themselves — this helps when regulatory requirements evolve (the Act has implementing regulations still coming).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is 