nts. However, as a general framework, it could be extended to cover these specific fields.\n\n**Recital 71:** Emphasizes traceability, comprehensible information, technical documentation kept up to date, automatic recording of events via logs over lifetime.\n- The proposal aligns well with the \"automatic recording\" and \"lifetime\" aspects. The standardized schema supports \"clear and comprehensive form.\"\n\nFrom my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses the core technical requirement of Art. 12(1) — automatic logging capability\n- Standardization across projects reduces compliance risk (inconsistent logging is a major audit failure mode)\n- Reduces manual intervention, which reduces human error in log capture\n- Integration into existing compute clusters means it's practical and implementable\n- Consistent schema supports the \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement from Recital 71\n- Addresses institutional survival by creating a reusable compliance asset rather than project-by-project ad hoc solutions\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level; it doesn't specify how it maps to the specific minimum requirements in Art. 12(3) for biometric systems\n- \"Without requiring manual developer intervention\" — this needs careful design because some log fields (like identification of natural persons in verification, Art. 14(5)) inherently require human involvement and cannot be purely automated\n- The scope is broad (\"all high-risk projects\") which could dilute focus on the specific categories with stricter requirements\n- Doesn't explicitly address retention periods or data protection implications of logging (GDPR intersection)\n- \"Middleware layer\" description is vague — compliance requires demonstrable, auditable technical implementation\n\n**Compliance risk assessment:**\nThis is a foundational infrastructure proposal. Without something like this, the institution faces a significant risk of non-compliance with