ns \"high-impact scientific publication.\" That's a signal that the primary driver here is publication output rather than operational necessity. As Lab Director concerned with resource allocation, I need to ask: does the lab have an immediate operational problem with logging that justifies a new R&D project? Or are we chasing a publication opportunity at the expense of other priorities?\n- \"Theorists will investigate\" — this is exploratory research, not implementation. The prompt says approving means committing resources to pursue it, \"not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\" Wait, let me re-read: \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\" This is key. This proposal is explicitly exploratory — \"investigate compressed event-logging formats and cryptographic proofs of compliance.\" It's a research project, not an implementation project. The framing as \"transforms a regulatory constraint into an opportunity for high-impact scientific publication\" makes clear the primary output is papers, not deployed systems.\n- However, if our AI systems ARE subject to Article 12 and we DO have logging infrastructure that needs to be built or upgraded, then understanding efficient approaches has practical value. But the proposal as written doesn't connect to a specific operational need — it's a general research initiative.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Does the lab already have logging infrastructure? If our systems are high-risk under the AI Act, we likely already have some logging in place or planned. A new theoretical project on compressed formats may not leverage what we already have.\n- \"Benchmark against standard logging methods\" — this is reasonable if it feeds into a real deployment decision, but as written it sounds like an academic exercise.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has intellectual appeal and the regulatory context is genuine (Article 12 does require robust logging). However:\n1. It's explicitly exploratory/theoretical (\"investigate,\" \"theorists will investigate\") rather than tied to a concrete operational need.\n2. The primary stated motivation is publication output, not solving an actual lab problem.\n3. The scope (compressed formats, cryptographic proofs) exceeds what Article 12 actually mandates — the regulation asks for event recording and specific data points for biometric systems, not novel cryptographic compliance mechanisms.\n4. \"Privacy-preserving\" adds scope beyond Article 12's requirements (that's GDPR).\n5. As a new initiative requiring dedicated theorists and benchmarking infrastructure, it competes with more directly operational priorities.\n\nHowever, there IS some merit: if our lab develops large-scale AI systems that will be deployed as high-risk, understanding efficient logging architectures does have practical value. And the regulatory context is real — we DO need to implement Article 12 compliance. The question is whether a dedicated research project on novel compressed formats is the right vehicle versus engineering solutions.\n\nI think this falls short of my bar. It's more publication-driven than operationally necessary, it over-scopes beyond what the regulation requires, and it's explicitly exploratory rather than implementation-focused. The lab would be better served by directing its engineering teams to implement standard logging solutions that meet Article 12, while perhaps dedicating a small portion of existing research capacity to evaluate whether novel approaches are warranted at scale. A full new R&D initiative is overkill.\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise score: I'd say around 35-40. There's intellectual merit and the regulatory context is real, but it doesn't meet my bar for committing scarce resources as a dedicated new initiative. It's more of a \"nice to explore if we have spare capacity\" rather than \"must pursue.\"\n\