s structural, not ad-hoc.\n\nConcerns/Weaknesses:\n- The proposal mentions \"intercepts inputs, outputs, and system states\" but Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires specific data points (period of use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). A generic middleware might not capture all the specific fields required by paragraph 3 without additional configuration per system type.\n- \"Without duplicating engineering effort\" is aspirational - in practice, different high-risk systems will have different logging needs, especially under Article 12(3) which has specific minimum requirements for biometric identification systems. The unified architecture may need system-specific adapters.\n- However, this doesn't make it a bad proposal - it's a foundational layer that can be extended with system-specific modules.\n- The \"immutable databases\" aspect is good but I'd want to ensure it also supports the retention period requirements (logs must be kept for the lifetime of the system per Recital 71).\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, directly relevant proposal from a compliance standpoint. It addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) and provides infrastructure that supports all sub-paragraphs. The mapping to regulatory text shows the right mindset. From my perspective on institutional survival (avoiding enforcement actions), regulatory compliance (meeting the letter of Article 12), and market reputation (demonstrating robust compliance architecture to regulators and clients), this is a high-value investment.\n\nThe main risk is that \"universal\" might be slightly overreaching - different Annex III categories may need different logging granularity. But as a foundational architecture with system-specific extensions, it's sound.\n\nI'll approve this. My promise score: This directly addresses my core mandate. The standardized approach reduces compliance risk across the portfolio. The immutability feature is exactly what regulators want to see. The explicit mapping to Article 12 shows regulatory literacy. I'd rate this highly - maybe 85-90. It's not a 100 because the \"universal without duplication\" framing slightly underestimates the need for system-specific configurations (especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems with their specific logging requirements), but it's still an excellent foundation.\n\nI'll go with 87.\n",
