aditional ML, LLM-based, multi-agent) may have fundamentally different event streams that need capturing. A truly unified framework might end up being too generic to capture the right events or too rigid to adapt.\n- The proposal conflates two things: the logging infrastructure AND the compliance interpretation (what events are \"relevant\" under Art 12(2)(a)-(c)). The latter is a legal/regulatory question that may change, while the former is engineering. Building in pre-configured templates couples them somewhat tightly.\n- For prototyping speed specifically: a framework that integrates with training/deployment pipelines adds integration overhead to every new project. If the pipeline changes (new architecture, new framework), the logging layer needs updating too. This could slow down rapid prototyping if not designed carefully.\n- The \"immutable ledger\" claim is technically ambiguous - what does immutability mean here? For a high-throughput system processing many events, true immutability can have performance implications.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\nThe core idea is technically viable and well-motivated. A shared logging library IS the right architectural pattern for this problem. The risk to prototyping speed is real but manageable if the framework is designed with low-intrusion integration (e.g., middleware/decorator patterns rather than deep pipeline coupling). The \"regardless of architecture\" claim is the weakest point - in practice, different model types generate different event streams. But a good modular API could handle this through adapters.\n\nOverall: This is a solid engineering proposal that addresses a real need. It's technically viable as described (though high-level). The main risk to prototyping speed is over-engineering or too-tight coupling, but the \"modular\" framing suggests awareness of that. I'd lean toward approve with moderate-to-high confidence. It's not a wild idea, it'