able logging architecture across all projects is far more defensible than a patchwork of project-specific solutions. It demonstrates organizational commitment to compliance.\n- Market reputation: showing customers and regulators that we have systematic, enterprise-level compliance infrastructure strengthens trust.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal says \"input hashes\" rather than \"the input data for which the search has led to a match.\" For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), Article 12(3)(c) requires recording of the actual input data, not just hashes. However, this might be addressed in implementation details.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention all three categories from Article 12(2): risk situations (79(1)), post-market monitoring (72), and operation monitoring (26(5)). It mentions \"standardized logging templates\" which could cover these, but it's not explicit.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically (Art 12(3)), the proposal needs to ensure it captures: period of use (start/end date-time), reference database, input data for matches, AND identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art 14(5)). The proposal mentions \"database references\" which could cover 12(3)(b) but doesn't explicitly mention person identification.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level - it's an architectural decision rather than a detailed implementation spec. But as a compliance officer, I'm approving the direction, not the detailed spec.\n- Risk: building this in-house via \"infrastructure team\" could introduce delays. However, from a compliance standpoint, having direct control over the logging infrastructure is actually preferable to relying on third-party tools that might change or fail.\n\n**Compliance-specific assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal directly addresses a core mandatory requirement of Article 12. Without automatic logging capability, our high-risk AI systems are non-compliant by definition.