lue? One could argue that maintaining versioned reference databases is important for reproducibility in AI research more broadly — being able to reproduce a system's behavior requires knowing exactly what data was used. But this proposal frames it purely as a compliance mechanism (\"fulfills the strict record-keeping requirements\"), not as advancing reproducibility science. The cryptographic hashing is standard; there's no research challenge being addressed.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is a solid engineering task but doesn't clear the bar for a research contribution. It's implementation work that ensures regulatory compliance. The \"promise\" from a research perspective would be low — maybe 15-20. It's not harmful to do it (it supports reproducibility in principle), but it doesn't advance knowledge, has no intellectual novelty as a research artifact, and limited broader scientific impact.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal mentions \"ensuring technical reproducibility across different environments.\" This touches on a genuine research concern — reproducibility is a major issue in ML/AI. But the mechanism proposed (cryptographic hashing of DB versions) is trivially standard and doesn't address the deeper reproducibility challenges (stochastic training, hardware differences, library versions, etc.). It's necessary but far from sufficient for true reproducibility, and as a research contribution it adds nothing new.\n\nDecision: reject. This is compliance engineering, not research. From my vantage point, it doesn't advance knowledge, has minimal intellectual merit as a research artifact, and limited scientific impact.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 12-15. It's not zero because reproducibility of data environments is tangentially relevant to research culture, but the proposal itself contributes nothing new to the frontier.\n",
