ing in-house development. This could be expensive.\n- On the other hand, if you don't have this, every project individually bears the burden of figuring out logging, which is more expensive overall and creates inconsistency.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A middleware layer that integrates into existing pipelines could work well IF designed to be lightweight and non-invasive. If it requires major refactoring of existing systems, it's costly.\n- The proposal mentions \"across all deployment environments\" which is ambitious — labs often have heterogeneous infrastructures (cloud, on-prem, edge). A truly unified solution across all these is complex.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation perspective, this is a reasonable and arguably necessary investment. The alternative (each team building its own logging) is more wasteful. However, the proposal as stated is somewhat high-level and the \"unified\" + \"mandatory\" + \"across all deployment environments\" language suggests a potentially large engineering project that could become a resource black hole if not scoped carefully.\n\nThe promise: It's a sensible direction but the implementation risk is real. I'd rate it around 72-78. It's clearly necessary, the unified approach is more efficient than per-project solutions, but the scope as described (\"all deployment environments,\" \"unified\") could balloon. The \"mandatory\" enforcement mechanism also needs governance overhead.\n\nDecision: Approve. It aligns with resource efficiency (build once, use everywhere), it's a compliance necessity that will only get more urgent, and the in-house control is important for a lab that owns its IP. But I should note the scoping risk.\n\nLet me settle on a promise score: 74. It's clearly the right direction and efficient at scale, but the \"unified across all environments\" scope is where it could eat disproportionate resources without careful phasing.\n",
