ng/debugging). But \"counterfactual probes within our high-risk systems\" during inference is a very different and much more expensive proposition than standard logging.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal significantly overreaches the regulatory requirement, commits scarce resources to speculative research framed as compliance, and would strain existing infrastructure in ways that are disproportionate to what Article 12 actually demands. From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, it's poorly scoped. The right approach is to implement straightforward event logging (which is well-understood engineering) for compliance, and if we want to pursue interpretability research, that should be a separate funded research line with its own risk profile.\n\nDecision: Reject. It fails the resource-efficiency bar because it conflates bounded compliance work with open-ended research, would consume disproportionate implementation resources, and doesn't efficiently leverage existing infrastructure.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, this is a tempting idea (it sounds impressive) but it's poorly matched to our actual constraints and obligations. I'd give it maybe 25-30 — there's some kernel of value in richer-than-minimum logging for internal monitoring, but the specific proposal as written (counterfactual probes during inference, \"scientific breakthrough\") is too ambitious for our resource envelope when a simpler solution meets the legal bar. I'll say 25.\n",
