rk that automatically links these would satisfy this better than manual documentation.\n- From a regulatory compliance standpoint, having logs tied to specific versions means if a regulator asks \"what was the system doing at time X?\", you can answer definitively.\n- This supports the \"comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems have been developed and how they perform throughout their lifetime\" from Recital 71.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal focuses heavily on training version and hyperparameter traceability, which is important but may not fully cover Article 12(3)'s specific requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (period of use start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). However, the proposal doesn't say it ONLY does this — it says it's a \"framework\" that links logs with commits and hashes. The framework could be extended.\n- The framing as \"reproducible research\" is somewhat academic rather than regulatory, but the substance addresses compliance needs.\n- One concern: linking Git commits to Article 12 event logs assumes that the operational deployment matches exactly what's in the repo. In practice, there may be configuration differences, environment variables, etc. But this is an implementation detail.\n- From a resource commitment perspective (approving means committing scarce resources), this is a substantial infrastructure build — a \"versioned data lake\" is not trivial. However, it directly serves Article 12 compliance and Recital 71's traceability requirements.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This proposal directly addresses the letter and spirit of Article 12.\n- It supports institutional survival by creating defensible records that can withstand regulatory scrutiny.\n- It protects market reputation because if an incident occurs, having full provenance allows rapid response and demonstrates good faith.\n- The \"automatic\" linking aspect is crucial for