nfiguration or separate modules to handle these specific requirements. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention handling the Annex III 1(a) specifics.\n- \"All deployment environments\" is a broad claim — if the system is deployed in multiple jurisdictions, cloud instances, on-premise, etc., the framework needs to work across all of them. This adds implementation complexity.\n- The proposal says it will be \"mandatory for any project flagged as high-risk\" — this creates a dependency on accurate classification. If a project is misclassified as not high-risk but should be, the logging wouldn't trigger. However, that's a classification problem, not a logging framework problem per se.\n- The framework needs to handle the \"lifetime of the system\" requirement — logs must persist for the entire operational lifetime. Storage and retention policies are implied but not explicitly addressed.\n- From a compliance audit perspective, having a single unified framework actually makes it EASIER for auditors (including the European Commission or national competent authorities) to verify compliance across all systems. This is a strong positive.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis proposal is strongly aligned with what Article 12 demands. It's essentially building the technical infrastructure that the law requires. Without it, each project would need to implement its own logging, creating inconsistency and audit risk. The unified approach reduces compliance risk across the institution.\n\nThe fact that it's \"mandatory\" for high-risk projects is good governance. The automation removes a common failure point (human error in manual logging). \n\nMy main reservation is that the proposal is somewhat generic — it doesn't explicitly address the Article 12(3) specific requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems, or the data retention/lifetime storage aspects. But as a framework-level proposal, these would be implementation details within the scope of \"according to Article 12 specifications.\"\n\nOverall: This is a strong, directly relevant proposal that addresses the core regulatory requirement. From my compliance perspective, I'd want this to exist. It's not just helpful — it's arguably necessary for institutional survival under the Act. The promise score should be high.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. Maybe 82-85. It's very well aligned but the slight generality and lack of explicit mention of Annex III 1(a) specifics or retention policies keep it from being a perfect 90+. Actually, re-reading: \"according to Article 12 specifications\" could be read as encompassing all sub-sections including 12(3). And \"across all deployment environments\" shows breadth. I'll go with 84.\n",
