earch milestones\" is good from my perspective - it protects R&D velocity.\n- However, the fixed quarterly cadence might be too rigid. If a system is being actively developed and is close to deployment, you'd want logging built in from the start (shift-left), not retrofitted later. The proposal only addresses legacy systems, which implies new development isn't getting this treatment proactively.\n- A \"specific percentage of compute\" for compliance work is concerning - compute allocation for refactoring/logging infrastructure is quite different from research compute needs. This feels like it's forcing a one-size-fits-all resource model onto heterogeneous tasks.\n- The promise of \"bringing past work up to code\" in quarterly chunks assumes the technical debt is modular and can be addressed incrementally. In practice, logging architecture often requires end-to-end changes across data pipelines, inference engines, storage layers, and monitoring dashboards - these are cross-cutting concerns that don't decompose neatly into quarterly sprints.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- The structured approach is good in principle, but the execution details reveal a lack of technical depth about what \"logging capabilities\" actually entail for AI systems. It reads more like a project management framing than an engineering plan.\n- The quarterly cadence without any mention of prioritization criteria (which systems are closest to deployment? which have highest risk exposure?) could lead to suboptimal sequencing.\n- For prototyping speed: if new prototypes need to incorporate logging from day one, this proposal doesn't address that at all - it's purely reactive/retroactive. This could actually slow prototyping because you'd build something without logging, then wait for the next quarterly sprint to add it before deployment.\n- The \"specific percentage\" language is a planning placeholder, not a concrete technical commitment. It lacks engineering specificity about what \"logging capabilities\" means architecturally.\n\nOverall assessment: The idea of ring-fencing compliance work so it doesn't continuously interrupt R&D has merit for protecting prototyping velocity. But the proposal as stated is too coarse-grained and somewhat naive about the technical complexity of retrofitting logging into AI systems. It doesn't address the forward-looking problem (new prototypes need logging built in from scratch), which is where my day-to-day work actually lives. The quarterly cadence might create artificial deadlines that force rushed, low-quality implementations of complex logging infrastructure.\n\nI'd say this is a middling proposal - the instinct to protect R&D time is right, but the technical execution plan is underdeveloped and potentially counterproductive for prototyping speed if it creates a \"build now, log later\" culture. I'll lean toward reject because from my vantage point, it doesn't actually solve the problem well and might create worse outcomes (rushed quarterly refactors vs. building logging in properly during development).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal does protect active research milestones, which is important for prototyping speed. And it's better than the alternative of ad-hoc compliance work interrupting everything. But the technical naivety and lack of forward-looking consideration (new systems) are significant concerns from my angle. I'll give it a moderate-low promise score and reject it because the execution as described would likely produce lower-quality implementations under time pressure, which hurts both the compliance outcome AND the quality of our prototyping infrastructure long-term.\n",
