itecture or experimental approach might need to conform to the framework's interface, adding friction for exploratory work.\n- The claim that it works across \"all high-risk prototypes\" and \"different model architectures\" is ambitious. In practice, highly novel architectures might not fit neatly into a standardized wrapper without customization.\n\n**Quality Considerations:**\n- Standardization does improve quality - consistent logging means you can compare systems, audit them uniformly.\n- But over-abstraction can reduce flexibility needed for rapid prototyping. If the framework forces a particular interface pattern, experimenting with unconventional architectures becomes harder.\n- The \"modular\" aspect helps - if it's truly modular, teams can opt into specific logging capabilities rather than being locked into one monolithic interface.\n\n**My Assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and aligned with what Article 12 requires (automatic event recording, timestamps, session tracking). The technical approach (middleware + API wrapper + immutable store) is viable and well-trodden in software engineering. The integration into base templates is the smartest part - it makes compliance a default rather than an afterthought.\n\nThe main risk from my prototyping-speed perspective is:\n1. Upfront build time before any prototype benefits\n2. Potential rigidity of a \"standardized\" wrapper slowing experimental work\n3. The \"universal\" claim may lead to over-engineering\n\nHowever, the modular design and template integration mitigate these risks significantly. This is essentially building an infrastructure component that pays for itself across multiple projects. From a development engineering standpoint, this is a reasonable investment IF it's kept lightweight and truly modular.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this - it addresses a real technical need (Article 12 compliance) in a way that actually HELPS prototyping speed by making logging automatic and standardized rather than per-project manual work. The key risk is execution scope creep, but the proposal as stated sounds appropriately scoped.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's technically viable, aligned with my domain (speeds up prototyping by removing per-project compliance overhead), and the approach is sound. It's not a 90+ because \"universal\" across all architectures is a stretch goal that could bloat scope, and the upfront investment is non-trivial. But it clears my bar clearly.\n",
