ates\" — this means leveraging what we already have rather than building from scratch or requiring external tooling.\n- It's a software library/middleware, not hardware procurement or major platform migration. Low capital expenditure, high leverage.\n- However, I should note: there's some risk here. Building a \"unified framework\" can become its own project that takes longer than expected, requires maintenance, and may resist adaptation to novel system architectures. But the core idea is sound.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The proposal says \"high-risk prototypes\" — but Article 12 applies to high-risk AI systems throughout their lifetime, not just prototypes. If the framework is designed only for prototyping phase, it may need significant rework for production deployment and long-term logging (the Act requires logs over the full lifetime of the system).\n- The scope mentions \"timestamps, input hashes, and system state changes\" — this covers some but maybe not all requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III systems (e.g., reference database, specific input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal might be underscoped for the full regulatory requirement.\n- \"Integrated into our base model templates\" — if we have diverse architectures beyond base models, this may not cover all cases.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea is excellent from a resource-efficiency standpoint. It's a force multiplier: one build serves many systems. It uses existing infrastructure (base model templates). It preserves engineering capacity for research. The scope might need to be expanded to fully meet Article 12(3) requirements, but that's an implementation detail. The strategic direction is right.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — it's well-aligned with my mandate of resource efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure. Maybe 82-85. It's strong but has the caveat about scope (prototypes vs. full lifetime) and completeness relative to Annex III requirements that slightly tempers my enthusiasm. I'll go with 84.\n",
