get blocked waiting for modifications to the shared library.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- On the positive side: if the framework works as described, research teams wouldn't waste cycles building basic logging scaffolding for every new prototype. This could genuinely accelerate prototyping by 1-2 sprints per project for the compliance layer.\n- On the negative side: during early prototyping, you often don't know exactly which events will be \"relevant\" under Article 12(2)(a)-(c). A rigid framework might either over-log (wasting storage/compute) or under-log (creating compliance gaps that require rework). The iterative nature of AI development means the logging requirements crystallize late in the process, not at the start when you'd want to \"plug in\" the library.\n- There's also a risk that teams will rush to use the framework before it matures, creating technical debt in a compliance-critical layer. If the shared library has a bug in timestamp handling or event sequencing, every project is affected simultaneously — a single point of failure for compliance.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe concept is directionally correct and addresses a real pain point. However, as written, it's somewhat over-scoped (\"across all high-risk AI projects,\" \"different model architectures\") and under-specified on the hard parts (how does one plug-and-play API handle the very different logging needs of biometric verification vs. employment screening vs. critical infrastructure monitoring?). The prototype speed benefit is real but conditional on the framework actually fitting each use case without heavy customization — at which point it's no longer really \"plug-and-play.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point, I'd say this has moderate promise. It's a reasonable infrastructure investment IF scoped appropriately and built iteratively alongside 2-3 actual projects rather than trying to be universal from day one. As stated, the scope is too broad for a single team to deliver quickly, and the \"c