tually counterproductive in the short term. Teams will either wait (slow) or bypass it and build their own (defeating the purpose). \n\nI'll reject with moderate promise — the idea has merit but as scoped it's not the right investment for prototyping speed.\n\nWait, let me reconsider once more. The proposal says \"minimizes the resource drain on individual research teams by eliminating redundant development.\" This is a real and valid concern. If 5 teams each spend 2 weeks building basic logging, that's 10 person-weeks wasted. A shared library could save that. But the question is whether the shared library will be ready when those teams need it, and whether it will actually fit their use cases without modification.\n\nI think the fairest assessment: approve with moderate promise (maybe 55-62). The concept is right, but execution risk is high given the scope. I'll go with approve at 58.\n\nHmm, but let me be more decisive. My bar: does this clear my threshold for committing scarce implementation resources? \n\nArguments for approve:\n- Real redundancy problem exists\n- Core concepts (timestamps, event logging, user ID) are well-understood and low-risk to implement\n- Could save 10-20 person-weeks per year across teams if it works\n\nArguments against:\n- \"Across all high-risk AI projects\" is enormous scope for one team\n- \"Plug-and-play\" across heterogeneous architectures is a stretch claim\n- During prototyping, logging needs are still being defined; a rigid framework fights iteration\n- Creates a single point of failure/dependency\n- Core infrastructure team becomes bottleneck\n\nI'll go with reject at 52. The idea has legs but as proposed it's too broad and centralized for the speed I need to maintain in prototyping. A lighter-weight approach (shared conventions + minimal shared utilities, built alongside actual projects) would be better, but that's not what this proposal is.\n\nFinal: reject, promise 52.\n",
