ther than the runtime logging requirement. Though you could argue it's adjacent.\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- Direction is correct: static PDFs are indeed inadequate for living systems that iterate frequently.\n- Git-based versioning is the right technical foundation - it's proven, auditable, and integrates with existing developer workflows.\n- The problem it solves (documentation drift during rapid R&D) is real and painful.\n- An MVP could be prototyped quickly even if full production readiness takes longer.\n\n**My decision:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and addresses a genuine pain point. The git-integrated approach is the right technical choice from an engineering standpoint - it's incremental, testable, and fits existing dev workflows. However, there are significant open questions about scope, trigger definitions, and the workflow integration that could make this either very useful or very slow to deliver. From a prototyping speed perspective, I can see a quick MVP (git hooks + auto-generated doc stubs on model/dataset changes) being built in 2-3 sprints. But the \"real-time compliance review\" component is where it gets murky for our R&D velocity.\n\nI'll lean toward approve because:\n- The technical foundation (git-integrated, version-controlled docs) is clearly viable\n- It addresses a real gap that will become critical as we ship high-risk systems\n- A prototype is achievable in reasonable time\n- Even a partial implementation (auto-generating doc stubs on model changes) has immediate value\n\nBut my promise score won't be extremely high because:\n- The scope as stated is large and somewhat vague\n- \"Real-time compliance review\" could become a prototyping bottleneck if not carefully scoped\n- It doesn't directly address the Article 12(1) logging requirement (runtime event logs), which is arguably the more technically challenging part of the regulation\n- The trigger mechanism (\"every major change\") needs much more specification before it's buildable\n\nI'll give it around a 65. It's a good direction, technically viable core, but the proposal as stated has enough ambiguity and scope risk that I wouldn't back it \"without hesitation.\"\n",
