every new prototype. If the lab's priority is speed of prototyping, there's tension here — you could argue that forcing every new prototype through a centralized logging layer slows initial development cycles.\n- On the other hand, if you're going to need this for compliance anyway (and Article 12 makes it mandatory), building it once and reusing it across projects is more efficient than each project team reinventing their own logging. This actually IMPROVES prototyping speed in the medium term by removing repetitive work.\n- The quality aspect: a standardized API means consistent data formats, which improves debuggability and reproducibility of prototypes.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Without manual effort\" is a red flag for over-promising. Article 12(3) requires recording specific things like \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" and \"identification of the natural persons involved in verification\" — these require project-specific hooks that can't be fully automated by a generic library.\n- The proposal doesn't address storage architecture, retention policies, or performance overhead — but those are implementation details that would come later.\n- Risk of over-engineering: building a \"unified framework for ALL high-risk projects\" before understanding the specific logging needs of each project type could lead to a framework that's either too rigid (can't handle edge cases) or too generic (doesn't capture what's actually needed).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically viable — it's essentially building an internal telemetry/observability library, which is standard engineering practice. The prototyping argument is strong: you build the logging infrastructure once and every new high-risk project gets compliance-ready instrumentation from day one, reducing per-project overhead. The main risk is scope creep in the \"unified\" part and the unrealistic \"without manual effort\" claim, but the core idea is sound and well-aligned with what Article 12 requires.\n\nFrom a prototyping speed perspective: this would actually accelerate prototyping for high-risk projects because engineers don't have to figure out logging compliance each time. It's an enabling infrastructure piece. I'd approve it, though I'd want to scope the \"unified\" aspect carefully and not over-promise on zero-effort integration.\n\nPromise score: This is a solid, technically feasible proposal that directly addresses a regulatory requirement in a way that reduces per-project friction. I'd say around 78-82. It's not a 100 because the \"without manual effort\" language suggests some over-engineering risk and the truly project-specific logging (like identifying verification personnel) can't be fully abstracted away. But it's clearly viable and useful.\n",
