og (append-only database with cryptographic checksums) would serve the same compliance purpose at far lower cost and complexity. The word \"ledger\" suggests a potentially more expensive and complex implementation than needed.\n- \"Develop an asynchronous telemetry system\" - building new infrastructure has significant upfront cost. The question is whether existing logging/monitoring infrastructure in the lab can be extended rather than building something from scratch. If the lab already has monitoring pipelines, this might duplicate effort.\n- \"Across all lab models\" - ambitious scope. This requires coordination across potentially many teams and projects. From a resource-allocation perspective, this could spread thin if not phased carefully. However, it also means one investment serves all projects, which is efficient in the long run.\n- The proposal captures some but not all of Article 12's requirements. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), you need reference database info, input data matching, and natural person identification - this proposal doesn't explicitly address those. But as a \"baseline compliance\" framework, it may be positioned correctly as a foundation that can be extended.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound from my perspective: automate the logging to reduce researcher burden, make it modular so it scales across projects (efficient use of infrastructure), and make it asynchronous so it doesn't impede research workflows. The \"immutable ledger\" language is a concern for cost/complexity but the underlying concept (tamper-evident records) is achievable with simpler tools. The scope (\"all lab models\") is ambitious but appropriate if done modularly - one framework serving all projects is more resource-efficient than per-project solutions.\n\nThe main risk is over-engineering (the \"ledger\" aspect) and potential duplication of existing monitoring infrastructure. But the fundamental approach - centralize, automate, modularize compliance logging to free researcher time - aligns well with my domain concerns.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this but with moderate confidence. It's a good strategic direction for resource efficiency, though the implementation details (especially \"immutable ledger\") need scrutiny to avoid cost bloat. I'll approve it because the core principle is right and it addresses a real pain point (manual documentation burden), but I'll give it a moderate-high promise score reflecting that the execution approach needs refinement from an efficiency standpoint.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 72 - The direction is right for resource efficiency, but the \"immutable ledger\" and \"develop\" language suggest potentially heavy upfront investment that could be more efficient by extending existing infrastructure rather than building new. Still, the modular, all-lab scope is the right call for long-term efficiency.\n",
