s and clients as evidence of systematic compliance commitment.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal mentions \"input data matches and verification IDs\" but doesn't explicitly mention all four elements of Article 12(3): start/end timestamps (a), reference database (b), input data matches (c), and natural person identification (d). However, it does say \"timestamping\" which covers (a), and the broader framework presumably covers (b) and (d) — but it's not explicit.\n- It says \"currently in development\" — what about systems already deployed? The obligation applies over the full lifetime. A new library might not retroactively cover existing deployed systems unless they're integrated.\n- Centralization is a double-edged sword: if the central library has a bug or goes down, all logging stops simultaneously. From a compliance standpoint, this creates a single point of failure that could be worse than distributed (though inconsistent) logging. A regulator might view a centralized system outage as a systemic non-compliance event.\n- The proposal doesn't address log retention periods, data protection implications (GDPR intersection with logging personal data), or access controls for logs. But these may be handled in implementation details.\n- \"Every execution\" — does this capture the full lifecycle? Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system,\" which includes development, testing, and post-market phases. The proposal focuses on runtime execution, which is good but might miss pre-deployment logging events.\n\n**Overall assessment from compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong, well-aligned proposal that directly implements a core Article 12 obligation in a scalable way. It reduces the risk of non-compliance by making logging automatic and uniform rather than relying on individual engineering teams to remember and implement it correctly. For an R&D institution with multiple projects, this kind of centralized infrastructure is arguably the most efficient path to demonstrating systematic compliance to regulators. The gaps I identified (retroactive coverage, single point of failure, explicit enumeration of all 12(3) elements) are implementation details that can be addressed during development without changing the fundamental approach.\n\nThis clearly clears my bar for approval. It's a foundational compliance infrastructure investment that directly addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement. I'd rate it high on promise — maybe 85-90. The main reason it's not 100 is the single point of failure risk and the slight gap in explicitly covering all four elements of 12(3) plus lifetime scope, but these are manageable in implementation.\n\nI'll give it a promise score of 87.\n",
