mpliance into a scientific instrument\" framing:** While intellectually appealing for an R&D institution, this is exactly the kind of scope expansion that burns resources without delivering what's actually needed. The lab needs to meet Article 12 — it doesn't need to publish papers on latent state transitions.\n\n6. **Timeline risk:** A research project \"to develop\" new mechanisms has uncertain timelines and outcomes. Compliance deadlines under the EU AI Act are fixed (high-risk systems have phased implementation). Betting compliance on a novel R&D project is risky from an allocation perspective.\n\n**Decision:** From my vantage point, this proposal is over-ambitious relative to the regulatory requirement, resource-intensive in ways that strain compute and storage budgets, and frames a compliance obligation as an open research question when it could be addressed with well-understood engineering. It doesn't efficiently use existing infrastructure — it proposes building new systems from scratch rather than extending what we have. I would reject this.\n\n**Promise score:** There IS some merit in the standardized instrumentation API concept, and for an R&D institution there's genuine scientific value in understanding model internals. But as a compliance response, it's misaligned with resource constraints. Maybe 35-40? It's not zero because the API standardization idea has reuse value, but from my specific vantage point of efficient allocation and meeting regulatory needs within budget, it scores low. I'll say 35.\n\nLet me finalize:\n- Rationale: The proposal significantly over-engineers beyond Article 12's actual requirements (event-level logging, not latent-state capture), consumes disproportionate compute/storage for frontier-model attention tensor recording, and frames a bounded compliance engineering task as open-ended research — misallocating scarce implementation resources that would be better directed toward straightforward, standards-based log infrastructure.