ists can still explore ambitious architectures; they just need to ensure their systems emit proper logs.\n- The risk: if the CI/CD gate is too brittle or creates excessive friction (e.g., false positives that block merges), it could slow down iteration and frustrate researchers, effectively taxing scientific velocity. But a well-designed gate with clear error messages should have minimal friction.\n- Funding-wise, this is a one-time setup cost (writing test cases, integrating into CI) with ongoing marginal costs near zero (the tests run automatically). It's capital-efficient compared to hiring dedicated compliance reviewers who would need to manually check every PR.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Labs doing AI development almost certainly already have CI/CD pipelines. This proposal leverages that existing infrastructure rather than building parallel verification tooling.\n- The synthetic test cases can be version-controlled alongside the codebase, making them maintainable as regulations evolve or system architecture changes.\n- It integrates naturally into existing developer workflows (merge requests, build status) without requiring new tools or processes.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"If logs are missing, the build fails automatically\" — this is a binary gate. For early-stage prototyping where logging infrastructure might not yet be in place, a hard fail could block exploration. However, you can configure this as a warning for prototype branches and a hard fail for production-bound code. The proposal doesn't explicitly address this nuance, but it's implementable.\n- The scope of \"synthetic test cases\" to cover all of Article 12 (especially the specific Annex III logging requirements in paragraph 3) requires careful design. But again, bounded effort.\n- Risk of over-engineering: if you try to verify every possible logging scenario at CI time, you might create a massive test suite that's slow and hard to maintain. But this is an implementation detail.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-aligned proposal for my domain. It uses existing infrastructure (CI/CD), shifts cost to the point of lowest remediation expense, doesn't constrain research ambition, and has low ongoing marginal cost. The one-time setup investment is modest relative to the savings from avoiding late-stage compliance rework. The \"build fails automatically\" mechanism creates strong incentives for developers to get logging right the first time, which reduces cumulative technical debt in the compliance layer.\n\nI'd rate this highly. It's not a 100 because there are implementation risks (brittleness of the gate, scope creep in test coverage, potential friction for early prototyping) that need careful management, but from my vantage point it's strongly aligned with efficient resource use and leveraging existing infrastructure. I'll give it an 82 — strong approval with minor reservations about execution details that could either make or break its efficiency gains.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal is quite clean in its logic: shift-left compliance verification using existing CI/CD infrastructure. The main risk to my domain (resource efficiency) would be if the test suite becomes so complex it requires significant ongoing maintenance, but that's a standard software engineering concern, not specific to this idea. The core concept is sound and well-matched to my concerns. I'll go with 82.\n",
