ll patterns, state management, and input/output types.\n- \"Integrated into our base model templates\" — this assumes a level of standardization across the lab that may not exist. If projects use heterogeneous architectures, forcing them through a single middleware adds friction.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- The promise is to \"remove manual burden\" and guarantee compliance. In theory, if it works well, this speeds up prototyping because researchers don't need to hand-roll logging.\n- However, building this universal layer ITSELF will consume enormous development resources before any project benefits from it. The lead time is significant — you'd need to design the API, handle edge cases across frameworks, implement tamper-proof storage, test extensively, and then migrate existing projects.\n- A \"modular API wrapper that intercepts model calls\" sounds simple in one sentence but in practice, intercepting arbitrary model calls (including multi-step pipelines, streaming inference, batch processing, RAG systems with retrieval steps) is complex. The Article 12(3) requirements for biometric systems specifically mention \"the reference database against which input data has been checked,\" \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" — these are domain-specific logging needs that a generic wrapper may not capture well without project-specific configuration.\n- Risk of becoming a bottleneck: if every high-risk deployment must go through this layer, any bugs or performance issues in the middleware block all projects. Single point of failure concern.\n- The \"tamper-proof\" requirement adds latency and storage overhead to every inference call, which could impact prototyping speed where you're iterating quickly.\n\n**Verdict from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea (standardized logging middleware) is sound and aligns with industry practice. However:\n1. The scope (\"all lab projects,\" \"mandatory foundation\") makes this a massive undertaking 