es (Article 79(1) risks). But the proposal as stated goes beyond \"some level\" — it proposes building a full secondary diagnostic model with real-time analysis and threshold-based alerting. That's a substantial R&D commitment.\n\nMy assessment: The core idea of automated monitoring has some merit, but the specific implementation proposed (a full AI-driven secondary model) is over-engineered for what Article 12 actually requires and represents a significant resource commitment that may not be proportionate to the compliance obligation. From my vantage point of resource efficiency and balanced ambition, I'd lean toward rejecting this as stated — it's too ambitious relative to the regulatory requirement, and there are more efficient ways to achieve the same compliance outcome (structured logging + rule-based alerts + periodic review).\n\nThat said, the underlying need (automated detection of anomalies in logs) is legitimate. But the proposal as written commits us to building a secondary ML system, which is a significant resource allocation decision that I'm not comfortable approving given our constraints and the fact that simpler approaches likely suffice for Article 12 compliance.\n\nDecision: Reject. The proposal over-commits resources (building a full AI model) for what can be more efficiently addressed through structured logging design and rule-based monitoring, which would leverage existing infrastructure better.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 30-40. There's a kernel of reasonable need here (automated anomaly detection in logs is useful), but the specific implementation is misaligned with efficient resource use and over-scoped relative to what the regulation demands. From my vantage point, it's not something I'd back as stated. Maybe 35.\n",
