peed:\n  - Every new prototype or experiment now needs to integrate this library before it can be tested, even during early exploration phases.\n  - If the framework's API isn't mature or well-designed initially, it creates friction for every team.\n  - \"Mandatory\" means if there's a bug or breaking change in the library, all teams are affected simultaneously — a single point of failure for prototyping velocity.\n- However, the counterargument: if logging is bolted on late (fragmented compliance), that's also slow and error-prone. Having it from day one reduces rework later. The question is whether making it \"mandatory\" from the start vs. recommending it is the right call.\n- A well-designed library with clear, minimal integration points could actually *accelerate* prototyping by removing the need for each team to figure out compliance logging independently.\n\n**Quality:**\n- Centralized approach means one team maintains quality standards, consistent timestamping (important for traceability), uniform data formats. This is a quality positive.\n- The risk is that a \"standard\" framework may not fit edge cases well, leading to workarounds or gaps in coverage.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea — building an internal shared library for compliance logging — is technically sound and a standard engineering practice. It's the right architectural pattern. The \"mandatory dependency\" part is where I have some reservation from a prototyping-speed perspective, but it's not unreasonable: compliance logging isn't something you want to skip in production, and having it baked in early reduces later rework.\n\nThe main technical concern is extensibility across different high-risk AI system types, but that's solvable with a plugin/adapter pattern within the framework.\n\nOverall: This is a viable, well-scoped engineering project that would genuinely help. It's not revolutionary but it's the right approach. The \"mandatory\" aspect slightly concerns me for 