s focus on the AI system rather than compliance plumbing.\n- On the other hand: if the framework is too rigid or requires significant integration work before prototyping can begin, it slows down the speed at which we can spin up prototypes. Researchers often iterate rapidly and change architectures; a logging framework that assumes a fixed pipeline structure could become a bottleneck.\n- The \"researchers integrate into their pipelines\" language suggests a plug-in model rather than a monolithic system, which is good for prototyping speed.\n- However, there's a risk of the library becoming a maintenance burden—if compliance requirements shift or if new use cases emerge, the shared module needs updates that ripple across all projects.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable and technically viable engineering solution. It addresses a real duplication problem in compliance logging. The key risks are:\n1. Over-engineering at the start (trying to be too comprehensive before any project actually needs it)\n2. Integration friction slowing prototyping\n3. Rigid templates that don't fit diverse system architectures\n\nBut fundamentally, building a shared logging SDK with structured templates is well within our technical capabilities and would genuinely reduce redundant effort. The \"database referencing\" component requires careful design but isn't technically infeasible. For speed of prototyping, it helps if done right (drop-in integration) but could hurt if done wrong (heavy wrapper around every pipeline).\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a standard software engineering deliverable with clear requirements derived from the regulation. The technical approach is sound. The main concern is execution—keeping it lightweight and flexible—but that's a design choice, not a fundamental viability issue. I'd approve it but give it a moderate-to-high promise score because while the concept is solid, the risk of it becoming prototyping friction is real, and \"uniform\" templ