d, and teams can extend them. The boilerplate doesn't prevent customization; it just eliminates the blank-slate problem.\n- Funding constraint perspective: This is a low-cost intervention with high leverage. A few developer-weeks to build good templates pays for itself on the second or third project. No major capital expenditure needed.\n\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It's efficient, leverages existing tools, protects scientific time, and structurally ensures compliance rather than relying on individual project teams remembering their obligations. I'd rate this quite highly — maybe 82-85. It's not a 100 because the value depends on maintenance (templates rot if not updated as the Act evolves or as we learn what works), and there's a small risk of rigidity, but those are manageable concerns.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Rationale: Efficient one-time investment that reduces per-project compliance overhead; preserves scientist time for actual research while structurally guaranteeing Article 12(1) logging is present from day one. Low cost, high leverage across the project portfolio.\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 84\n",
