 well (standardized telemetry API), it could become a public or semi-public resource for the interpretability community — similar in spirit to how genome databases transformed biology.\n- The connection between regulatory compliance data and research insight is elegant: you're generating the dataset as a byproduct of mandatory work, then leveraging it for science. This is efficient and scientifically meritorious.\n- Studying \"AI failure modes\" systematically across many deployed systems would advance our understanding of robustness, distribution shift, and model fragility — core open problems.\n\n**Concerns / limitations:**\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) meeting Article 12 compliance requirements (which is relatively straightforward engineering), and (2) building a research-grade interpretability infrastructure (which is much harder). The \"comprehensive logging layer that captures internal state activations, weight shifts, and decision paths\" is actually an enormous R&D undertaking — capturing meaningful \"decision paths\" in modern architectures (transformers with millions of parameters) is far from solved.\n- There's a risk that this becomes compliance theater dressed as research: the Article 12 requirements are relatively shallow (timestamps, input/output data, verification IDs), and bolting on \"activations\" and \"weight shifts\" without a clear scientific hypothesis about what you're studying risks generating enormous datasets with no analytical pipeline to make sense of them.\n- The phrase \"goldmine of empirical data\" is somewhat glib — data without a research question, analytical methodology, and interpretability framework is just storage costs.\n- However, the *intent* and the *direction* are scientifically sound. The gap between what Article 12 requires (shallow event logging) and what would actually be useful for understanding model behavior (deep internal state tracking) is real, and this proposal identifies it correctly.\n\n**My judgment:**\nFrom a pure research-merit standpoint, the *idea* of building longitudinal, in-the-wild interpretability telemetry across deployed high-risk AI systems is genuinely valuable at the knowledge frontier. The standardized API aspect enables cross-project comparability, which is critical for generalizable scientific findings. The centralized lake enables retrospective analysis that individual teams cannot do.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat vague on the actual scientific contribution — it's more of an infrastructure plan than a research program. It says \"feed this rich data into a centralized research lake for retrospective analysis\" but doesn't specify what questions will be answered or what interpretability methods will be applied to the captured state.\n\nStill, from my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this is one of those proposals where the infrastructure investment has clear, high-value scientific upside that no other compliance-focused proposal would capture. The opportunity cost of *not* building this telemetry layer means you're generating massive amounts of operational data and discarding it instead of making it scientifically useful. That's a real loss for research impact.\n\nI'll approve this but with moderate-to-high confidence, acknowledging the vagueness in the scientific program while recognizing the genuine frontier value of the infrastructure. I'd put promise around 72-78 — strong direction, clear scientific upside, but needs more specificity on what interpretability science will actually be done with the data to move from \"data lake\" to \"research output.\"\n",
