g resources that could go toward scientific projects. The question is whether the savings outweigh the investment. For a lab with multiple high-risk AI systems, yes — amortized cost drops significantly.\n- The proposal doesn't specify timeline or resource commitment. It's presented as an aspiration (\"software engineers will develop...\") rather than a scoped project.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The phrase \"integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines\" suggests building something that plugs into existing work. That's good — it leverages what exists rather than forcing researchers onto new platforms.\n- A \"modular API\" approach is sound engineering — it means teams can adopt pieces incrementally rather than a big-bang migration.\n- However, \"mandated for all high-risk project repositories\" could be heavy-handed if not phased in well. It risks disrupting active research projects mid-flight.\n\n**Article 12 specific compliance:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). A unified library directly addresses this.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities for risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input metadata, and user IDs\" — but doesn't explicitly address the specific requirements in 12(3) (period of use, reference database, input data matches, natural person identification for verification).\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), there are very specific minimum logging requirements. The proposal's \"consistent format\" is vague on whether it can actually capture all the Article 12(3)(a)-(d) specifics.\n\n**My assessment from Lab Director perspective:**\nThis is a fundamentally sound infrastructure investment. A single compliance logging library across the lab is more efficient than N separate implementations. It aligns with \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" by creating a shared platform rather than duplicating eff