        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2152\",\n      \"rationale\": \"As written, this proposal reframes Article 12's specific and bounded logging obligations (automatic event recording; the three traceability purposes in Art 12(2); the four minimum data fields in Art 12(3)) into an open-ended interpretability research programme, without committing to any of those discrete requirements. The 'real-time activation-pattern tracing across all high-risk models' scope is neither mandated by the text nor proportionate to it, creating a serious risk that scarce implementation bandwidth is absorbed by a grand observability platform while the auditable, regulator-facing minimums (start/end timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched-input records, verification-personnel IDs) remain under-specified or deferred. From an institutional-survival standpoint, approving this would signal to leadership—and potentially to a market-surveillance authority—that we are treating record-keeping as a science project rather than a legal obligation with concrete, non-negotiable data fields.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 30\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer whose domain is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2152 describes an \"Observability-Driven Interpretability Framework\" that transforms compliance logs into research datasets for analyzing AI decision paths. Let me assess this strictly against Article 12 requirements and my professional concerns.\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires:**\n- Paragraph 1: High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n- Paragraph 2: Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for (a) identifying risk situations/substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, and (c) monitoring operation per Article 26(5).\n- Paragraph 3: For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically: recording use periods, reference databases, input data matched, identification of natural persons in verification.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer's vantage point:**\n\nThe core requirement is that the system technically allows for automatic recording of events (logs). The purpose is traceability, compliance verification, and monitoring.\n\nThis proposal goes well beyond what Article 12 requires. It proposes \"high-fidelity event tracing that maps external inputs to internal activation patterns and final outputs in real time across all high-risk models.\" This is an interpretability/observability research tool, not a compliance logging system per se.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n\n1. **Scope creep / resource misallocation**: Article 12 requires logs that capture specific events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring. The proposal reframes this as a \"scientific instrument for discovery\" — this is not what the regulation asks for. Committing scarce implementation resources to build an interpretability research platform when the actual compliance requirement is more modest (automatic event logging with specific data points) is a misallocation that could leave us non-compliant on the actual requirements while spending resources on something tangential.\n\n2. **Compliance adequacy**: The proposal doesn't clearly articulate how it meets the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3)(a)-(d) for Annex III point 1(a) systems, nor does it explicitly address the three purposes in Article 12(2)(a)-(c). It talks about \"high-fidelity event tracing\" and \"internal activation patterns\" which are research-grade concerns, not compliance-grade logging.\n\n3. **Risk of non-compliance under the guise of over-implementation**: If we build a flashy interpretability framework but don't ensure the basic automatic logging (start/end times, reference databases, matched inputs, verification personnel) is robust and auditable, we fail Article 12. The proposal's language (\"turns a regulatory burden into a scientific instrument\") suggests it might underinvest in the boring, specific compliance requirements in favor of the exciting research angle.\n\n4. **Market reputation**: There's actually some reputational upside — positioning as an AI transparency leader could help market position. However, if this is presented to regulators or market participants and we can't demonstrate clear compliance with Article 12's specific provisions, it backfires.\n\n5. **Institutional survival**: The risk here is that the lab commits resources to a grand research vision while the actual legal obligation (which is relatively straightforward: implement automatic logging with specified data fields) remains under-addressed. If a regulator audits and finds we built an \"interpretability framework\" but can't produce the specific logs required by Article 12(3), that's a compliance failure with real consequences.\n\n6. **The \"real time across all high-risk models\" claim**: This is ambitious and could be a resource sink. The regulation doesn't require \"real time\" — it requires logging over the lifetime of the system. Real-time processing of activation patterns across ALL high-risk models is far beyond what's needed for compliance.\n\n**However, there are some positive elements:**\n- It does address automatic recording of events (the core of Art 12(1))\n- \"High-fidelity event tracing\" could encompass the required data points if properly scoped\n- The commitment to logging across all high-risk models is aligned with the universal scope of Article 12\n\n**My decision:**\nThis proposal, as written, reframes a compliance obligation as a research opportunity. From my vantage point, this is dangerous. It risks:\n- Misallocating resources away from the specific, mandatory data fields required by Art 12(3)\n- Creating an impression of over-compliance that masks under-compliance on specific requirements\n- Committing to \"real time across all high-risk models\" which is neither required nor necessarily achievable without enormous cost\n\nThe proposal doesn't clearly state it will implement the minimum logging requirements of Article 12(2) and (3). It subsumes compliance into a broader research narrative. As Compliance Officer, I need assurance that the specific legal requirements are met first and foremost, not as a byproduct of a research program.\n\nI'm going to reject this. The promise score should reflect that while there's some alignment with logging requirements, the framing and scope make it inadequate as a compliance response. It's not that the idea is terrible — it's that from my specific vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance), it doesn't clear the bar because it doesn't commit to the specific obligations clearly and risks resource misallocation.\n\nI'd give it maybe a 30-40. There's some overlap with what's needed (automatic event logging exists in the concept), but the framing as research tool rather than compliance instrument, the lack of specificity on Art 12(3) requirements, and the \"real time across all models\" scope make it inadequate from my perspective.\n\nLet me settle on 35.\n",
