rces from actually implementing Article 12 compliance for our own systems.\n   - The proposal says \"across different AI architectures\" — this is an extremely broad scope. Our lab's primary obligation is to comply with the Act for OUR systems. Defining standards for ALL architectures is a massive undertaking.\n   - There's no regulatory obligation on us to create industry standards. This is voluntary thought leadership.\n\n3. **Market reputation angle:**\n   - Positioning as \"primary thought leader in AI governance\" has some reputational upside, but it also creates expectations. If our \"standard\" diverges from what CEN-CENELEC or the Commission ultimately prescribes, we could look out of touch or even be seen as trying to game the system.\n   - Publishing a reference implementation means maintaining it, supporting it, and potentially being held responsible if others adopt it and it has gaps relative to actual regulatory requirements.\n   - There's a risk: if our \"standard\" becomes widely adopted and then the final harmonized standards differ, we'd face credibility damage AND potential liability questions.\n\n4. **Key compliance concerns:**\n   - The proposal frames this as defining \"what constitutes an appropriate log under Article 12.\" That's not our role to define unilaterally. The European Commission and notified bodies/standards organizations have that authority. An R&D lab publishing a schema and calling it THE standard could be seen as overstepping or creating confusion.\n   - More critically, if we publish an implementation that others adopt, and it turns out to NOT satisfy the actual regulatory requirements when they're finalized, that creates legal exposure for us (as the originators) and potentially for adopters.\n   - The proposal is framed as \"leadership initiative\" — from a compliance perspective, I need to ask: does this actually help US comply? Or is it a reputation play that distracts from our core obligation?\n\n5. **What would be better 