ome utility for engineering planning. But from my specific vantage point, it's more of a legal/governance concern than a technical one. The \"technical leads\" on the board gives me some input, but I'm essentially being asked to participate in a classification exercise rather than build something technically viable.\n\nI'll lean toward approving with a moderate promise score, because:\n- It does prevent wasted engineering resources (relevant to my function)\n- It's simple and low-cost to implement\n- But it's not primarily a technical solution and the quarterly cadence could be a bottleneck for fast prototyping cycles\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The instruction says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposal that does not.\" What is my bar? As a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping:\n- Does this improve the technical viability of our solutions? Marginally — by ensuring we know which systems need logging before building.\n- Does it improve speed/quality of prototyping? Could go either way. It prevents rework (good) but adds a quarterly gate that might slow things down (bad).\n\nI think this is a \"reject\" from my specific vantage point because:\n1. It's primarily a legal/governance process, not a technical solution\n2. The quarterly cadence conflicts with fast prototyping cycles — in R&D, project scope and intended purpose can shift within weeks, not quarters\n3. It doesn't directly contribute to building technically viable logging solutions\n4. The \"prevention of waste\" argument, while valid, is better served by simpler mechanisms (e.g., a lightweight checklist at project kickoff rather than a formal quarterly board)\n\nBut wait — it does have the benefit of ensuring I don't waste time building logging for non-high-risk systems. That's relevant. And it's not technically complex to implement. Hmm.\n\nI'll go with \"reject\" at a moderate promise score (around 35-40). The reasoning: fr