r budget, but then why allocate a whole \"initiative\" to it? A few engineers on the product teams could do this as part of their regular work.\n- Is resource allocation efficient? Starting a new project has high fixed costs (team formation, project management, reporting overhead). This might be better served by adding performance SLOs for logging to existing system development tickets.\n\nI'll reject it. The promise score: the problem is real (~50), but the proposed solution structure (standalone research initiative) is inefficient for this type of work from my vantage point. I'd say around 35-40. Let me settle on 38.\n",
