e paperwork.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Yes, it builds on ML tracking tools we presumably already have.\n- Uses Git repositories we already maintain.\n- Doesn't require new hardware or cloud services beyond what's standard for our DevOps stack.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-scoped, practical proposal that addresses a real compliance burden with a proportionate solution using existing infrastructure. The main risk is scope creep into full Article 12(3) runtime logging, but as stated, it's focused on documentation generation which is a clear efficiency win. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is a good use of engineering time — moderate upfront investment for recurring savings and compliance readiness.\n\nI'll approve this. The promise score should reflect that it's a solid, practical proposal with some caveats about scope. I'd say around 72-78. It's clearly worth doing but not transformative. It addresses documentation (Recital 71) well but the full Article 12 logging requirements (especially 12(3) for biometric systems) would need additional work beyond this proposal's stated scope.\n\nI'll go with 74.\n",
