 use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This leverages existing human capital (legal experts + senior scientists) rather than requiring new hires or external consultants.\n- It maps onto existing workflows rather than creating new ones - this is efficient.\n- The output (trained researchers who design compliant experiments by default) is a durable asset that compounds over time, unlike one-time compliance fixes.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"A series of workshops\" is vague on scope, duration, and success metrics. As Lab Director, I'd want to know: how many sessions? What's the expected adoption rate? How do we measure whether this actually reduces compliance friction?\n- Pairing legal experts with senior scientists sounds good but could be expensive in terms of researcher time if not well-structured.\n- The proposal doesn't address what happens for novel experimental designs that don't fit existing logging patterns - it assumes the mapping can be done cleanly, which may not always be true.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a sensible, relatively low-cost intervention that addresses a real friction point. It leverages existing personnel and workflows rather than requiring new infrastructure. The main risk is scope creep (open-ended \"series\") and the subtle constraint on research ambition if compliance thinking becomes too dominant in design. But the framing explicitly aims to minimize friction while maintaining velocity, which aligns with my concerns.\n\nFrom a resource-allocation standpoint, this is one of the cheaper ways to build institutional capability around Article 12 compliance. It doesn't require new systems, new hires, or major capital expenditure. The main cost is researcher time for workshops and some legal expert hours.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure (people, workflows), addresses a genuine operational gap, and has reasonable cost relative to the benefit of reduced compliance friction going forward. I'd approve