"timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" but doesn't explicitly address capturing which reference database was queried or the specific input data that matched (just hashes). This is a gap for the Annex III(1)(a) systems specifically.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A middleware logging library is very prototypable. You can build an MVP in days — a decorator/wrapper around inference functions that captures metadata and writes to structured storage.\n- The \"standardized software library\" aspect means it needs to be general enough for different model types (CNN, transformer, etc.) but specific enough to capture the right fields. This is manageable with a plugin architecture.\n- The \"tamper-evident ledger\" adds prototyping overhead — you need to decide on the integrity mechanism. For a prototype, hash-chaining is simple. For production, it gets more complex (key management, availability of signing keys, etc.).\n- Integration into existing pipelines could be slow if those pipelines are heterogeneous (different frameworks, languages, deployment targets). But as a library with clear interfaces, it's feasible.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n1. The proposal is somewhat high-level — \"a middleware layer that hooks into model inference calls\" is the core idea but the actual engineering of making this work across heterogeneous ML stacks (Python/PyTorch, C++ serving, microservices) is non-trivial and could slow prototyping.\n2. \"Tamper-evident centralized ledger\" — for a prototype, this is over-engineered. You'd want to start with structured logging + hash chaining and not get bogged down in \"ledger\" architecture early on. This could slow initial prototyping.\n3. The proposal doesn't address the specific Article 12(3) requirements (reference database, matching input data, verifier IDs for biometric systems). It captures generic metadata but may miss domain-specific fields.\n4. However, the core concept — automated, pipeline-integrated logging as a library — 