ions requiring logging.\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses Article 12(2)(a) — identifying situations of substantial modification\n- Creates an automated mechanism rather than relying on human judgment (which is error-prone in iterative R&D)\n- Links to event logs, which is the core requirement of Article 12(1)\n- Reduces risk of non-compliance during rapid iteration cycles (practical concern for an R&D lab)\n- The \"automated triggers\" concept aligns with the \"automatic recording\" language in the regulation\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- \"AI-driven monitoring tool\" — using AI to monitor compliance is interesting but adds complexity. Who monitors the monitor? This could create its own compliance questions under other articles.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — it doesn't specify what thresholds constitute \"significant deviation\" or how \"substantial modification\" will be operationally defined per Article 3(29) and related provisions.\n- It focuses on model weights and performance metrics, but substantial modification could also involve changes to data, training pipelines, validation processes, etc. (as referenced in Recital 71).\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address the Annex III 1(a) specific logging requirements (Article 12(3)), though that may be out of scope for this particular tool.\n- \"Minimizes risk\" — it doesn't eliminate it, and there's no mention of human oversight or escalation protocols.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong proposal that directly addresses a core obligation under Article 12(2)(a). For an R&D institution doing iterative development, the risk of accidentally making a \"substantial modification\" without logging it is real and could lead to enforcement action. Automating detection reduces this risk significantly. The linkage to event logs ensures the system satisfies the \"automatic recording\" requirement rather than relying on manual entry.\n\nHowever, I have some reservations:\n1. The AI-dr