ons. The proposal seems narrow — it mentions \"model capabilities and limitations\" in the reports, which is good, but doesn't explicitly address the risk-management system documentation or the full scope of what Recital 71 demands.\n- It addresses technical documentation (Recital 71) but does NOT directly address Article 12(1)'s requirement for \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" Logging of operational events is different from documenting training processes. A compliance officer would note this gap.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems, there are specific minimum logging requirements under Art 12(3) that this proposal doesn't address at all.\n- The phrase \"eliminates manual record-keeping overhead\" is slightly concerning — even with automation, a human must verify completeness and accuracy of documentation. Compliance requires accountability. However, the proposal says \"allowing engineers to focus on iterative development,\" which implies humans still review but with less burden. This is acceptable if framed as reducing rather than eliminating oversight.\n- \"Custom exporters for our ML tracking tools\" — this is practical but introduces a dependency: if we switch ML tracking tools, we'd need new exporters. From a compliance standpoint, the documentation must persist regardless of tooling choices.\n\nOverall assessment:\nThis is a solid, well-targeted proposal that addresses a significant portion of what Recital 71 requires regarding technical documentation automation. It's genuinely useful for compliance because automated, versioned documentation is far more defensible in an audit than manually maintained docs. However, it has scope limitations — it doesn't cover the full logging requirements of Article 12(1)-(3) (operational event logs), and it may not fully capture all elements Recital 71 lists.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- It's a good building block toward compliance\n- The versioning aspect is strong for trace