ce for different architectures? But the proposal as stated doesn't grapple with those questions; it just says \"we'll build a wrapper.\"\n- The framing of \"removes documentation burden from researchers while guaranteeing strict regulatory traceability\" is an operational/efficiency argument, not a scientific one.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If it works well, it could improve reproducibility in the lab's own work. But that's internal tooling, not a contribution to the broader scientific community unless it's published/open-sourced (which isn't stated).\n- The \"immutable ledger\" aspect could have some impact on research integrity discussions, but again, this is implementation rather than investigation.\n- It doesn't open new research questions or advance understanding of AI systems in any fundamental way.\n\n**My assessment:** From a knowledge-frontier and scientific-impact vantage point, this is an infrastructure/engineering project with limited intellectual content. It addresses a practical need (logging, provenance) but doesn't push the boundary of what we know about AI systems. The intellectual merit is that of competent software engineering applied to a regulatory requirement. It's not where I'd want the lab's scarce research resources committed if the alternative is work that advances understanding of how these systems work, fail, generalize, or interact with the world.\n\nThat said, I should be fair: reproducibility and provenance ARE genuine scientific concerns. The fact that this proposal frames itself around \"regulatory traceability\" rather than \"scientific reproducibility\" further diminishes its appeal from my vantage point. A scientifically-driven version would focus on what provenance information is *necessary and sufficient* to understand a model's behavior, which would be a research question. But this proposal just says \"capture everything automatically.\"\n\nI'll reject it. Promise score: low single digits maybe 5-8. It's not worthless