re these domain-specific fields without becoming rigid.\n- \"Sidecar pattern or decorator-based\" — the \"or\" suggests this hasn't been fully designed yet. For biometric systems (Annex III 1(a)), the logging is tightly coupled to the matching/verification workflow, so a pure sidecar might miss contextual data that's only available within the processing pipeline. A decorator wrapping specific operations might be more appropriate than a generic sidecar.\n- However, from a prototyping speed perspective, having ANY shared middleware beats each team building their own logging from scratch. Even if it needs iteration per domain, the foundational infrastructure saves significant time.\n\n**Technical viability:** High. Decorator/middleware patterns are standard, well-documented software engineering practices. This is not cutting-edge research — it's solid engineering with clear deliverables. The risk is low.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping impact:** Positive. If researchers can add a decorator or configure a sidecar rather than implementing logging infrastructure per project, prototyping cycles accelerate. The shared library approach means one team builds it, all teams benefit.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a technically sound, practical engineering proposal that directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) (automatic recording). It's implementable with existing software patterns. The main risk is scope — \"unified\" across all systems could be ambitious, but the foundational middleware layer is clearly viable and valuable. From my vantage point, this is a solid engineering investment that reduces per-project overhead and standardizes compliance logging. I'd approve it, though I'd want to see the API design account for domain-specific requirements (especially Annex III 1(a) specifics).\n\nPromise score: This is a strong, practical engineering proposal. It's not groundbreaking but it's exactly the kind of infrastructure that makes prototyping faster and more c