e conventional monitoring infrastructure (e.g., log aggregation tools, threshold alerts, dashboards) at a fraction of the cost.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The lab likely already has some logging infrastructure in place to satisfy Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events). Building a \"separate monitoring agent\" adds another layer of complexity and maintenance burden. Could we integrate monitoring into existing CI/CD or operations pipelines? Could we use off-the-shelf log analysis tools (ELK stack, Datadog, etc.) rather than developing custom AI agents?\n\nHowever, there are merits:\n- Post-market monitoring IS a requirement under the Act (Article 72), so having automated monitoring is aligned with compliance\n- Early warning reduces risk and potential costly breaches\n- If we're already building logging infrastructure, adding a monitoring layer might be incremental\n- The \"predefined safety and performance thresholds\" approach is pragmatic and doesn't require ML — it's rule-based alerting\n\nThe key concern from my vantage: The proposal frames this as developing \"a separate monitoring agent\" that is \"AI-driven.\" From a resource-efficiency standpoint, the actual compliance requirement (Article 12) is about having logs that capture certain events. Post-market monitoring (Article 72) requires the provider to monitor, but doesn't mandate building an AI system to do so. Building an AI-driven agent for what could be threshold-based alerting on existing log infrastructure seems like a resource misallocation — you'd spend significant R&D cycles developing ML models when rule-based alerts on structured logs would satisfy the regulatory requirement more efficiently.\n\nAlso, \"real-time\" analysis adds engineering complexity and cost. The regulation doesn't specify real-time; it says \"over the lifetime of the system.\" Batch or near-real-time monitoring might suffice.\n\nThe proposal's framing as protecting \"market reputation\" and av