bilities and limitations after a hyperparameter change.\n- The proposal says it \"triggers mandatory documentation updates\" — this could mean (a) it generates the updated docs automatically, or (b) it creates a task/alert that forces a human to update docs before deployment proceeds. Interpretation (b) is much more technically feasible; interpretation (a) is significantly harder for substantive content.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- A prototype of this system: you could build a basic version in weeks — a CI/CD gate that checks model artifact hashes against documented versions, blocks deployment if they don't match, and creates a Jira ticket or PR requiring documentation updates. That's very doable.\n- However, the full \"living documentation\" vision (automatically generating updated technical documentation) is a much larger engineering effort. The prototype-to-production gap could be significant.\n- This does NOT directly help with prototyping speed in the sense of building AI systems faster. In fact, adding mandatory documentation gates to the deployment pipeline could slow down iterative prototyping cycles if not designed carefully (e.g., allowing \"draft\" states for experimental models).\n- Quality: from a quality perspective, keeping docs in sync with code is genuinely valuable and reduces audit risk. This is a real pain point.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 specifically:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). This proposal is more about technical documentation than logging per se. Recital 71 does mention \"availability of technical documentation\" and keeping it \"up to date... throughout the lifetime.\" So it's adjacent but not directly implementing Article 12's core requirement (logging capabilities).\n- However, the recital explicitly calls for technical documentation kept up to date, so this is within scope of the broader record-keeping obligation.\n\n**My assessment as a Development Engineer:**\n- The concept is sound and addresses a real gap.\n- Technical viability: HIGH for the \"trigger/gate\" component (detecting changes, blocking deployment), MODERATE for the \"automated documentation generation\" component (if that's what's implied).\n- The risk is overpromising on the automation of doc content generation. But even as a gate + structured changelog system, it's very buildable.\n- For prototyping speed: could be neutral to slightly negative if it adds friction to iteration cycles, but the structured approach actually helps quality.\n- I think this clears my bar. It's technically viable (at least in its core form), addresses a real requirement from the regulation, and is implementable with existing tools (CI/CD pipelines, version control, data lineage). The main engineering challenge is scoping what \"documentation update\" means at different levels of change granularity, but that's a design decision, not a fundamental viability blocker.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, technically feasible, addresses a real need, but the \"automated documentation generation\" aspect (if taken literally) has significant technical uncertainty, and it's more of an infrastructure/platform investment than something that directly accelerates prototyping. It also doesn't directly implement Article 12's logging requirement per se — it's the adjacent documentation-keeping obligation from Recital 71. But as a development engineer, I can see how to build this and it would work.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
