is builds ON TOP of existing frameworks (\"compatible with our primary frameworks\"). It doesn't require replacing anything or building new compute clusters.\n- A centralized time-series database is a well-understood, relatively inexpensive component (e.g., InfluxDB, TimescaleDB, or even ClickHouse).\n- The \"sidecar\" pattern means it's non-invasive to existing pipelines — you wrap them rather than rebuild them. This respects the current infrastructure investment.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Universal wrapper\" is ambitious in scope. Making something truly universal across all frameworks and pipeline types could balloon into a large engineering project. The risk of over-engineering or under-delivering on coverage exists.\n- It needs to be maintained as frameworks evolve, which is ongoing cost.\n- The proposal says \"our primary frameworks\" which suggests scoping is reasonable, not truly universal.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong fit for my vantage point. It's an infrastructure-level solution that:\n1. Amortizes compliance cost across all projects (efficiency)\n2. Doesn't consume research budget per-project (funding balance)\n3. Builds on existing frameworks rather than replacing them (infrastructure use)\n4. Removes repetitive manual work from engineers (freeing capacity for actual R&D)\n\nThe main risk is scope creep — \"universal\" could become a never-ending project. But the proposal as stated (\"compatible with our primary frameworks\") suggests reasonable scoping. The engineering patterns described (decorators, API hooks, sidecar pattern) are standard and well-understood, reducing technical risk.\n\nI'd approve this. It's one of the more efficient ways to handle Article 12 compliance from a resource perspective. Promise score: I'd put it around 82-85. It's clearly valuable and efficient, but the \"universal\" ambition carries some risk of scope creep, and the ongoing maintenance cost isn't addressed. It's not a 95+ because there are execution risks, bu