framework itself requires engineering resources that could be directed at R&D. The question is whether the payback (across projects) justifies the upfront investment. For a lab with multiple high-risk systems, yes; for one or two, maybe not.\n- The proposal positions it as removing per-project logging implementations — this is a force multiplier on limited engineering staff.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A shared library that abstracts storage from model logic fits well with the idea of reusing infrastructure rather than building bespoke solutions.\n- It integrates with \"existing prototypes\" suggesting it's designed to plug into what's already there, not require a rebuild.\n- The centralized time-series database is new infrastructure, but if the lab doesn't already have one, this consolidates logging that would otherwise be scattered across projects (each project having its own ad-hoc logging).\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Unified\" and \"shared\" can become a bottleneck. If one team needs a specific logging behavior not covered by the standard API, they're blocked or forced to fork. This creates maintenance overhead.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — it's essentially \"build a logging SDK.\" This is well-understood engineering, not high-risk in terms of feasibility. The main risk is scope creep and over-abstraction.\n- It doesn't address the full breadth of Article 12(3) requirements (reference database checks, input data matching, natural person identification for verification). It mentions \"timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" which partially covers (a) and (d) but not fully (b) and (c) for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n- From a resource perspective though, even partial coverage via a shared framework is better than nothing scattered across projects.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a sensible infrastructure investment that aligns well with my concerns about resource efficiency and protecting resea