ceived proposal that directly targets the core compliance obligation under Article 12. The standardized approach reduces institutional risk (inconsistent implementation across projects is a major audit finding). The focus on reducing per-project burden increases adoption likelihood, which is critical — compliance tools must actually be used to provide compliance protection. From an institutional survival standpoint, having a unified, auditable logging infrastructure is foundational.\n\nThe main gap is that it's somewhat generic and doesn't explicitly enumerate all the specific data points required under 12(3), but as a framework/library approach, those would be implemented in the hooks. The \"pre-built hooks\" language suggests these are configurable to meet specific requirements.\n\nI'd rate this highly from a compliance perspective. It addresses the core obligation directly, is institution-wide (reduces systemic risk), and has practical adoption considerations built in. I'll approve it with a high promise score. Maybe 82-85 — strong alignment but the description's vagueness on specific Annex III requirements gives me slight pause, though as a framework this is expected at proposal level.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
