ally targets biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)) with more specific requirements - the sidecar could capture those too since they're input/output level.\n- \"Secure vault\" for storage - this is a design choice, not really an architectural problem. Encryption at rest, access controls, retention policies would need to be specified but are standard.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is where I really focus. The key benefit stated: \"preserving prototyping speed and preventing developer burnout.\"\n- From a prototyping standpoint, this is actually quite good. If compliance logging is handled by infrastructure (the sidecar), developers building new models or features don't need to wire up their own logging for each iteration. They can prototype freely, and the sidecar captures everything automatically.\n- However, there's a risk: if the sidecar architecture is built first as a monolithic standard before we've prototyped enough different model types, it might not fit well. A \"one size fits all\" sidecar could be over-engineered or miss edge cases. Better to prototype 2-3 models with manual logging first, identify common patterns, then build the sidecar around those patterns.\n- The \"across our clusters\" scope is ambitious for a first implementation. Starting with one cluster and iterating would be more prototyping-friendly.\n- The asynchronous nature means you don't add latency to inference - this is important for real-time systems but also means debugging/logging issues are harder (eventual consistency in logs).\n- One concern: \"transparent interceptor\" implies it sits in the request path. If it fails, does it fail open (allow traffic through without logging) or fail closed (block traffic)? This needs to be specified. Fail-open is better for prototyping speed but creates compliance gaps.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nFrom a development/prototyping perspective:\n- The architectural pattern is sound and well-proven.\n- It correctly decoup