tandardization reduces risk of individual projects missing logging requirements\n3. Integration into base images ensures no project can bypass the requirement (addresses the \"researcher diligence\" gap)\n4. Secure database schemas suggest data integrity and protection concerns are considered\n5. Mandatory API hooks create a technical enforcement mechanism rather than relying on voluntary compliance\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns from compliance perspective:**\n1. The proposal is somewhat generic — it mentions \"timestamps and system states\" but doesn't explicitly address all the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3)(a)-(d) (reference database, matched input data, natural person identification for verification per Art. 14(5))\n2. It says \"all institutional high-risk AI projects\" — good scope, but I need to verify it covers the full lifecycle including post-market monitoring logs\n3. The proposal doesn't mention retention periods or how long logs must be kept (though Article 12 implies \"over the lifetime of the system\")\n4. No mention of log integrity/immutability — critical for regulatory audits\n5. No mention of how this interacts with data protection requirements (GDPR) when logging user IDs and inputs\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- This is a foundational compliance infrastructure piece. Without it, the lab cannot demonstrate Article 12 compliance at all. Approving this is essentially mandatory.\n- A standardized framework reduces the risk of a single project's failure to log properly creating an institution-wide compliance gap during an audit.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Having a unified, documented logging framework demonstrates to regulators and market partners that the institution takes compliance seriously at a systemic level rather than ad-hoc.\n- It creates a defensible position: \"we have institutional-level technical enforcement of logging.\"\n\n**Resource commitment concern:**\n- This is a significant enginee