tandards. This proposal asks to create an entirely new protocol and committee rather than leveraging or extending what exists. It also mandates this for ALL projects (not just high-risk AI systems), which is a scope creep that would consume resources disproportionately.\n\n4. **Scope issue**: The proposal applies to \"all research publications\" and \"every project submission,\" not just high-risk AI systems subject to the Act. This dramatically expands the resource burden beyond what the regulation requires. A lab developing AI systems may have many projects that are exploratory or non-high-risk — mandating this provenance protocol for all of them is wasteful.\n\n5. **Does it actually address Article 12?**: Partially, at best. The \"structured map linking specific input datasets to model versions and final outcomes\" overlaps somewhat with what technical documentation requires under the Act (Recital 71 mentions \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\"). But the proposal's primary framing is about publication appendices and peer review standards, not about building automatic logging capabilities into the systems themselves (which is the core of Article 12(1)). It's solving a different problem — scientific reproducibility for publications — rather than regulatory traceability.\n\n6. **Opportunity cost**: The resources spent on creating this committee, training staff on new protocols, and adding review gates to publication workflows could be better directed toward actually implementing the automated logging infrastructure that Article 12(1) requires.\n\n**Decision**: This proposal is well-intentioned but misaligned with my resource-allocation mandate. It creates new overhead structures (committee, mandatory pre-publication audits) for a goal (publication reproducibility) that is adjacent to but not identical to the regulatory requirement. It applies universally rather than targeting high-risk systems. The existing infrastructure could be adapte