gnificant manual overhead\" may be optimistic given the integration work required across pipelines\n\nFrom my vantage point, I'd want to see:\n- A scoping that limits initial deployment to high-risk AI systems only (not all models)\n- An assessment of existing infrastructure before committing to building new middleware\n- Phased implementation\n- Clearer cost estimate and resource commitment\n\nThe idea has merit but as a proposal for committing scarce resources, it's too broad and doesn't adequately account for what the lab already has. I'd lean toward rejecting in its current form because it commits significant engineering resources without adequate scoping or consideration of existing infrastructure. However, the underlying concept (automated logging integrated into pipelines) is valuable — so it's not a zero-promise idea.\n\nDecision: reject — the proposal as written commits to building custom middleware across all models without first assessing existing infrastructure, without phasing, and with over-engineered components (immutable ledger). It doesn't clear my bar for resource-efficient allocation. The core concept could be revisited with tighter scoping.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 45-55. The direction is right, the automation argument is strong from an efficiency standpoint, but the execution as proposed would waste resources on custom building and over-scoping. Let me settle on 50 — it's a viable direction that needs significant rework before it's resource-efficient.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My job is to judge whether this proposal clears my bar for committing scarce implementation resources. The proposal:\n- Has the right strategic direction (automate compliance logging in-pipeline)\n- But proposes building custom middleware without assessing existing tools\n- Over-scopes to \"every model iteration\" rather than high-risk AI systems only\n- Adds unnecessary complexity (immutable ledger)\n- No phasing, no cost estimate\n- Risks duplicating existing infrastructure\n\nI'll reject but give a moderate promise score because the core insight is valuable and could be re-scoped.\n",
