rnover. A dynamic pipeline directly mitigates this existential risk.\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrate robust compliance infrastructure is a competitive differentiator, especially for high-risk AI systems.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation (which is more squarely Article 11/Annex IV territory) rather than the specific logging requirements of Article 12(1)-(3). However, Recital 71 does connect technical documentation to record-keeping and traceability.\n- \"Real-time audit trail\" is somewhat vague — what constitutes \"real-time\"? What's the retention period? The EU AI Act has specific retention requirements.\n- Integrating Git and MLflow is practical but doesn't address all aspects of Article 12, particularly the specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics).\n- The proposal is somewhat broad in scope — it's a significant infrastructure project. But from my compliance perspective, having this kind of system would be invaluable.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\nThis proposal directly addresses one of the most critical compliance obligations under the EU AI Act — maintaining up-to-date technical documentation that enables traceability and audit readiness. The risk of non-compliance here is severe (fines, market withdrawal orders). A dynamic pipeline rather than static reports is actually more aligned with the spirit of the regulation which requires documentation to be kept \"up to date... throughout the lifetime.\" This protects institutional survival by ensuring we can demonstrate compliance at any point in time. It also protects market reputation because if competitors are forced to show outdated documentation during audits, having a robust system is a differentiator.\n\nHowever, I note it's not purely an Article 12 solution — it's more of a technical documentation solution that supports the broader record-keeping regime. But Recital 71 explicitly ties these together: \"This requires keeping records and the availability of technical documentation.\" So they are linked.\n\nMy assessment: This is strongly aligned with my compliance mandate. It addresses a real regulatory gap (documentation currency), reduces audit risk significantly, and supports institutional survival. I would approve this. The promise score should be high — perhaps 82-88 range. It's not a perfect Article 12 solution (doesn't specifically address the logging event requirements in Art 12(1)-(3)), but it strongly supports the documentation aspects that Recital 71 ties to record-keeping. I'll go with 84.\n",
