entation synchronized with actual experiments. This directly addresses the \"kept up to date throughout lifetime\" requirement in Recital 71.\n- However, there's a caveat: fully automatic population of \"general characteristics, capabilities, and limitations\" from project metadata alone may be insufficient. Some fields (like risk-management system documentation, or qualitative descriptions of limitations) won't be derivable purely from MLflow telemetry. So the tool would produce a draft that still needs human review — but even a 70-80% auto-populated template is a massive productivity win over starting from scratch each time.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal says it \"reduces manual record-keeping overhead\" which is true, but it doesn't address the full scope of Article 12 (which also requires automatic logging of events — that's more about runtime instrumentation than documentation generation). This proposal seems to target the technical documentation aspect mentioned in Recital 71 rather than the logging requirement in Art. 12(1)-(3) specifically. Still, it addresses a real and necessary component.\n- The \"prevents funding losses due to non-compliance during audits\" is a business framing; from my engineering perspective, I care that the technical solution works and can be prototyped quickly.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically sound, builds on existing infrastructure, has clear scope, and can be prototyped quickly with high confidence of success. The main risk is underestimating how much content in regulatory documentation isn't derivable from automated telemetry — but even a partial automation (say 60-70% auto-fill) represents enormous value. From my vantage point on technical viability and prototyping speed, this clears the bar comfortably.\n\nI'd give this a high promise score. It's not a moonshot, it's well-scoped, technically straightforward, and leverages tools we already have. The main engineering challenge is template design and mapping, wh