rn is whether this can be done more efficiently — perhaps by integrating logging validation into existing CI/CD pipelines rather than building a separate \"testbed.\"\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is my key concern. The proposal says \"dedicated staging environment\" and \"specific compute clusters.\" Does the lab already have staging environments? Most R&D institutions with AI systems likely have some form of testing/staging infrastructure. Building a NEW dedicated environment for compliance logging validation seems like it could be more efficiently achieved by adding specific test cases to existing pipelines.\n- The \"simulated failures\" aspect is interesting — fault injection testing can be done in many ways, and doesn't necessarily require a whole new cluster if you have existing staging environments.\n- On the other hand, if the lab's existing infrastructure was never designed with compliance-specific stress-testing in mind (e.g., it doesn't capture all the Article 12(3) fields: start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, verification person identification), then some dedicated setup might be justified.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea — early validation of logging capabilities to prevent late-stage redesigns — is sound and aligned with efficient resource use. The problem is the framing as a \"dedicated staging environment\" with \"specific compute clusters.\" This suggests a significant new capital expenditure when the lab may already have infrastructure that can be extended at lower cost. \n\nHowever, I should judge this on its merits. If the lab truly lacks any mechanism to stress-test logging under failure conditions, then some form of dedicated test environment is necessary. The proposal is well-motivated by Article 12's requirements (automatic recording over lifetime, specific fields for Annex III point 1(a) systems).\n\nThe risk from my vantage: \"dedicated compute clusters\" is expensive and may not be 