man-in-the-loop interactions require integration with whatever UI/workflow system handles those verifications.\n- The scope of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems is specific: start/end times, reference database, input data that matched, identification of natural persons verifying results. The proposal seems broader than what's strictly required for some categories but addresses the general requirements well.\n- \"Precise timestamping\" — technically trivial with NTP-synced clocks, though there are subtleties around distributed systems (event time vs. processing time).\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- A centralized middleware approach could actually HINDER prototyping speed if it becomes a bottleneck or a monolithic dependency. If every new prototype must integrate the logging framework before it can be tested, that adds friction to the development cycle.\n- However, once built, it does remove a recurring manual step from each project, which speeds up delivery of compliant systems over time.\n- The \"without relying on inconsistent discipline\" framing is right — making it automatic (as Article 12(1) requires: \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording\") means engineers don't have to remember to add logging calls. This is a good engineering principle.\n- Risk: building this as a single monolithic project before any system ships could delay prototyping significantly. A better approach might be incremental — build the core, integrate with the highest-priority systems first.\n- The proposal says \"a standardized software library\" which suggests it's meant to be reusable and non-invasive, which is good for prototyping speed (drop-in instrumentation rather than pipeline redesign).\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe technical approach is sound and well-motivated. The core idea — automated, centralized logging middleware — is a standard, proven software engineering pattern that directly addresses Article 12(1)'s requirement