ng approach strengthens your compliance posture.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal says \"timestamps\" specifically - Article 12 requires recording of events broadly (start/end dates AND times for biometric systems per 12(3)(a), reference databases, input data matching results, identification of natural persons in verification). A library focused on \"event logs and timestamps\" might not be sufficient if it doesn't capture the specific data fields required by Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n- \"Small cross-functional engineering team\" - is this resourcing adequate? If the library needs to handle the full breadth of Article 12 requirements including the specific fields in paragraph 3, it might need more than a small team. But then again, if it's truly just a logging middleware layer (not implementing the AI system logic), a smaller team might suffice for the infrastructure piece.\n- \"Existing Python stack\" - this is practical and reduces integration friction, which supports adoption. If teams don't adopt it, compliance gaps remain.\n- The proposal focuses on \"event logs and timestamps\" but Article 12(3) requires recording of specific data types (reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons). Does the library scope include these, or just generic event logging? This is a gap in the proposal as written.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\n- The direction is strongly aligned with Article 12's requirements. A unified approach to automatic recording is exactly what the regulation demands.\n- Consistency across projects reduces the risk of systemic non-compliance and makes regulatory audits far more manageable.\n- However, the proposal as stated might under-scope the specific data capture requirements of Article 12(3). The library needs to be designed to capture not just \"events and timestamps\" but the specific fields mandated by law for different categories of high-risk systems.\n- The commitment is 