g change that could break existing running systems during prototyping phases. This directly conflicts with my concern for speed/quality of prototyping because any bug in the middleware becomes a blocker for ALL teams simultaneously.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The upside: once built, it removes repetitive logging boilerplate from every new project. Researchers don't have to implement Article 12 compliance from scratch each time. This genuinely speeds up prototyping of compliant systems.\n- The downside during development: building this middleware itself is a significant engineering project that diverts resources. And the \"update base container images across the lab\" step creates a coordination bottleneck — no team can ship until the image update is validated.\n- Quality risk: if the middleware has bugs, every project inherits them simultaneously. A logging failure in the middleware means compliance failure for ALL systems at once. This is a single point of failure that could be catastrophic from a regulatory standpoint.\n- The \"wrapper libraries\" approach adds abstraction layers that can slow down debugging and prototyping iteration. When something goes wrong in a prototype, you're now debugging through middleware + wrapper + actual model code.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe concept is right — centralizing compliance logging is the correct engineering pattern for regulatory requirements that apply to all systems. It's technically feasible. However, from my vantage point:\n\n1. The scope (\"across ALL high-risk projects\") makes this a large, multi-quarter build before it delivers value to any single team.\n2. The \"update base container images across the lab\" is operationally heavy and creates a prototyping bottleneck during the transition period.\n3. Single point of failure risk: if the middleware has a bug, compliance is broken everywhere simultaneously. For a regulatory requirement where non-compliance has real legal consequences, this concentration of risk is concerning.\n4. However, the long-term payoff for prototyping speed is real — teams won't need to rebuild logging each time.\n\nI think this clears my bar as technically viable and directionally correct for improving prototyping speed over time. The concerns are manageable with proper phasing (pilot on a few projects first before lab-wide rollout), but the core idea is sound engineering. It's not a research bet — it's applied infrastructure work that will pay dividends.\n\nI'll approve it, but my promise score should reflect that while I see the value, there are significant execution risks and the short-term impact on prototyping speed could be negative (resources diverted to build the middleware, container image updates causing downtime). It's a solid 62-70 range for me — genuinely useful engineering work with clear regulatory necessity, but the \"across all projects\" scope and big-bang deployment approach introduce risks that concern me.\n\nLet me settle on 65. It's clearly viable and necessary, reduces long-term prototyping friction, but the execution plan as described (update ALL base images across the lab) is risky for my core mandate of keeping prototyping moving quickly.\n",
