        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2895\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The building blocks here (content-addressed storage, dataset versioning via DVC/LakeFS-style tools, cryptographic checksums, MLflow/W&B integration) are all mature and well-understood engineering patterns, so a working prototype is achievable in weeks rather than months. It maps cleanly onto Article 12(3)(b) (recording the reference database checked against) and Recital 71's data-documentation requirements, making it a concrete, testable compliance feature with low technical risk.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 82\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2895 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Automated Data Lineage and Provenance Pipeline\" — an automated tracking system for training/testing/validation datasets used in high-risk model versions, with version-controlled data lake using cryptographic hashing, integration with experiment trackers, creating immutable audit trails.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Version-controlled data lakes are a well-established pattern (e.g., DVC, LakeFS, Delta Lake). This is technically mature and proven.\n- Cryptographic hashing for dataset immutability is straightforward — SHA-256 checksums on file sets, content-addressed storage. No novel research needed.\n- Integration with experiment trackers (MLflow, W&B, etc.) is a known integration pattern. These tools already have hooks for data versioning.\n- The \"automated\" aspect is the key value: instead of manual documentation, you get automated provenance tracking as datasets flow through pipelines.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is highly prototypable. You can stand up a basic version in weeks, not months. DVC or LakeFS + a hashing layer + MLflow integration = working prototype quickly.\n- It directly addresses Article 12(3)(b) — \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system\" — and supports Recital 71's requirement for documentation of \"data, training, testing and validation processes.\"\n- It also supports Article 12(1)'s automatic recording requirement in a data-provenance sense.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal says it \"satisfies the technical documentation requirements of Recital 71\" — this is somewhat overclaiming. Recital 71 requires much more than data lineage (algorithms, risk management, etc.). But as a component, it's a necessary piece.\n- The scope could balloon: tracking \"all training, testing, and validation datasets used in high-risk model versions\" across an entire R&D institution is a large engineering effort if done comprehensively. However, the core pipeline is straightforward to prototype.\n- \"Transparent map from raw data to final weights\" — this is aspirational. Full provenance from raw bytes to model parameters (including which specific training runs used which data slices) requires deep integration with training pipelines. A first pass could cover dataset-level provenance without going into sample-level lineage, which would be much harder technically.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- Technical viability: HIGH. The building blocks exist and are well-understood. No fundamental technical uncertainty.\n- Speed of prototyping: HIGH. You can have a working MVP in 2-4 weeks with existing tools (DVC, content-addressed storage, MLflow).\n- Quality: A well-implemented data lineage pipeline is a quality-of-life improvement that also directly serves compliance. It's not a throwaway prototype — it becomes operational infrastructure.\n- The proposal is appropriately scoped for a first implementation phase.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically viable, quickly prototypable, and directly addresses a specific regulatory requirement (Article 12(3)(b) and Recital 71's data documentation requirements). The engineering effort is well-understood with existing tooling. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 82. It's very viable and quick to prototype, but the \"transparent map from raw data to final weights\" part has some scope risk — full end-to-end provenance at sample level would be much harder than dataset-level lineage. Also, institutional-wide rollout across all teams using different pipelines adds integration complexity. But the core idea is sound and prototypable quickly.\n",
