acy-compliance tension, but this proposal seems more oriented toward finding ways to reduce data exposure rather than ensuring full compliance while managing privacy appropriately (e.g., access controls, encryption at rest, data minimization within legal bounds).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. A compliance officer would want:\n1. Systems that comply with Article 12 — yes, logs must exist and be specific.\n2. But also compliance with GDPR and other data protection laws that apply to the data in those logs.\n3. Market reputation — showing we handle privacy responsibly while being compliant is good for reputation.\n\nThe proposal's focus on SMC and differential privacy is quite extreme. For forensic/compliance logging, you typically need actual identifiable data (who verified what, when). Differential privacy adds noise that could make individual events unidentifiable, which directly conflicts with Article 12(3)(d) requiring \"identification of the natural persons involved.\"\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point, this proposal:\n- Is research, not implementation — doesn't move us toward a compliant system\n- Risks exploring techniques incompatible with Article 12's specificity requirements\n- The publication aspect could create regulatory attention without clear compliance benefit\n- However, it does touch on a legitimate concern (privacy in logs) that we need to manage\n\nI think I'd lean toward rejecting this. It's not aligned with the direct compliance implementation needs. It's exploratory research into techniques that may well conflict with the letter of Article 12. The privacy concerns are better addressed through standard data protection measures (encryption, access controls, retention policies, purpose limitation) rather than through obscuring the log content itself. A compliance officer would want logs to be complete and traceable per the Act, while ensuring proper governance around who accesses them.\n\nMy decision: reject.\nThe promise score: maybe 25-30? It