't necessarily a flaw — it addresses one aspect of record-keeping.\n- It says \"upon every major commit\" — what about minor changes? What about data drift in production? The Act requires documentation to be current throughout the lifetime, and \"major commit\" might miss incremental changes that affect compliance.\n- It doesn't explicitly mention risk-management system documentation (also required by Recital 71).\n- It doesn't address the specific logging requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (period of use, reference databases, input data matches, identification of natural persons in verification).\n- The scope seems limited to training/development documentation rather than full lifetime operational records.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis is a solid, practical proposal that addresses a real compliance requirement (keeping technical documentation current per Recital 71). It's not a complete solution for all record-keeping obligations under Article 12 and Recital 71, but it doesn't claim to be — it specifically targets the technical documentation aspect. The automation approach is sound: reducing manual steps reduces the risk of documentation drift or omission.\n\nFrom my vantage point (compliance, institutional survival, market reputation):\n- This directly supports our ability to demonstrate compliance to regulators\n- It creates an auditable trail tied to version control\n- Reducing manual documentation burden means less chance of gaps that could be flagged in a regulatory audit\n- Market reputation: showing we have systematic compliance processes is a positive signal\n\nHowever, it's not comprehensive enough to fully satisfy Article 12 on its own. But the proposal doesn't claim to be — it addresses the technical documentation update requirement specifically. As a standalone initiative, it clears my bar because it meaningfully advances our compliance posture on a specific, important requirement without introducing risk.\n\nI'd approve this. It's pragmatic, directly mapped to a regulatory requirement, and reduces compliance risk. The promise score should reflect that while it's good, it's not a complete solution — maybe 72-78 range. It addresses one important piece of the record-keeping puzzle well, but doesn't cover operational logging (Article 12(1)-(3)) or all elements of Recital 71 (risk management documentation, testing/validation processes comprehensively).\n\nI'll go with approve at around 74.\n",
