tructured narrative generation.\n  - \"Synchronizes experiment metadata with final model weights automatically\" — technically feasible (content-addressed storage, hash linking) but adds complexity.\n  - The proposal says \"comprehensive internal system\" which suggests a large build scope. For prototyping speed, this is concerning — it's a big-bang architecture rather than an incremental approach.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The proposal positions itself as a single comprehensive framework. From a prototyping perspective, I'd want to see a phased approach: first capture basic provenance (already partially solved by existing tools), then add the EU-specific mapping layer, then automate documentation generation.\n- Building \"a middleware layer that hooks directly into our existing ML pipelines\" is ambitious as a single project. In an R&D lab with diverse pipelines, this could take months to get right across all pipeline types.\n- However, the core value proposition is clear: if we can auto-generate much of the Article 12 / Recital 71 documentation from execution data, that's a massive time-saver for compliance engineers and reduces the risk of stale documentation.\n- The \"real-time telemetry to a versioned database\" part is standard engineering — not particularly novel or risky technically.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Scope creep: It's trying to be both an MLOps tool AND a regulatory documentation generator AND a reproducibility platform. That's three products in one proposal.\n- \"Significantly enhancing the scientific reproducibility of our frontier research\" — this is scope beyond Article 12. While nice, it muddies the focus and increases build time.\n- The \"standardized EU-compliant ledger\" format isn't specified in the regulation in a way that maps cleanly to a single schema. The regulation says documentation should be \"in a clear and comprehensive form\" but doesn't prescribe a data model. Building the wrong schema would waste effort.\n- For an R&D 