e existing infrastructure (e.g., do we already have document management systems? Do we already have audit processes from other regulations like GDPR, ISO 27001?).\n- However, the proposal is framed at a governance/process level, not requiring new R&D funding. It's more about process engineering and operational discipline.\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency assessment:**\n- This is compliance infrastructure, not scientific R&D. From my vantage point, I need to consider whether this can be done efficiently with existing staff and systems.\n- A Living Document repository is a one-time setup cost plus ongoing maintenance. If we already have document management (likely in an R&D institution), the marginal cost may be lower than it appears.\n- Quarterly audits are predictable and budgetable. They're not as resource-heavy as continuous monitoring.\n- The proposal doesn't overreach — it's scoped to what Recital 71 actually requires. It doesn't propose building a novel AI system or new laboratory equipment.\n- It does require dedicated personnel for audit functions, which is a real cost but a manageable one.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This proposal doesn't consume R&D funding in the traditional sense (no wet-lab time, no compute clusters, no novel algorithm development). It's governance overhead.\n- However, it does pull engineering/technical staff away from research to maintain documentation and undergo audits. The quarterly cadence is a recurring drag on scientific throughput.\n- That said, without this, the lab cannot legally deploy high-risk AI systems, which would be far more costly (inability to pursue projects).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A Living Document repository can likely leverage existing document management or version control systems (Git-based, Confluence, etc.) that an R&D institution already has.\n- Quarterly audits could potentially be integrated with existing internal audit cycles if the lab has them for other compliance frameworks.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a necessary compliance investment that is reasonably scoped. It's not flashy or scientifically ambitious, but it's essential infrastructure. The main resource concern is the recurring labor cost of documentation maintenance and quarterly audits, but this is predictable and budgetable. It doesn't require novel R&D funding. From an efficiency standpoint, it's a \"must-do\" that can be implemented without major capital expenditure if we leverage existing systems.\n\nI'd rate this as moderately promising from my vantage point — it's necessary (high compliance value) but the resource implications are moderate and manageable. It's not a proposal I'd reject because it doesn't align with scientific ambition, but it also isn't something that would make me excited to allocate premium resources toward. It's a solid operational investment.\n\nI'll approve it because:\n1. It addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement (not optional)\n2. It can likely leverage existing infrastructure\n3. The resource commitment is predictable and moderate (quarterly cadence, document management)\n4. Without it, we face legal liability and inability to deploy systems — much higher cost\n5. It doesn't compete with scientific R&D for the same resources in a way that would significantly impair research output\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's necessary and well-scoped, but from my efficiency vantage point, it's overhead rather than value-creating R&D. It's a \"must\" not a \"want,\" which means it scores high on necessity but the execution could be streamlined further if we integrate with existing systems. I'll go with 74.\n",
