ndard for others is a significant opportunity cost.\n- The proposal's stated rationale is positioning (\"positions our lab as a primary scientific authority\") — that's reputational capital, not operational necessity. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this doesn't directly reduce our compliance burden or improve efficiency of our own systems.\n- \"Universal logging format\" is ambitious to the point of impracticality in the near term. Different high-risk categories (Annex III) have very different logging needs (e.g., biometric verification vs. credit scoring vs. critical infrastructure). A single universal schema risks being either too generic (useless) or too complex (impractical to implement).\n- The collaboration model with other R&D institutions introduces coordination costs, IP disputes (the proposal itself notes balancing \"regulatory transparency with intellectual property protection\"), and governance overhead that could consume years of staff time.\n- There's no mention of how this connects to our existing R&D infrastructure or how it leverages what we already have in place for logging/traceability.\n- The text is also repetitive (the \"How\" and \"Why\" sections repeat nearly verbatim), suggesting this may be a rough ideation note rather than a well-thought-out proposal with clear resource requirements, timelines, or deliverables.\n\nFrom my vantage point of resource-allocation efficiency: This proposal asks us to commit scarce implementation resources (engineering staff time) to an external standards-drafting exercise whose primary stated benefit is reputational positioning. Our actual obligation under Article 12 is to ensure OUR systems have the logging capabilities. We can achieve that compliance by building internal logging infrastructure, possibly adopting existing standards or industry practices, without needing to lead a multi-institutional standardization effort.\n\nThe balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints tips toward re