        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2100 is about \"Privacy-Preserving Audit Logs via Secure Enclaves\" - using encrypted, immutable logging with HSMs and zero-knowledge proofs to let regulators verify compliance without exposing raw sensitive input data.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Hardware Security Modules (HSMs) for log integrity: This is well-established technology. HSMs are mature, available off-the-shelf, and integrating them into logging pipelines is a known pattern. However, they add latency to every log write operation, which could be problematic at scale.\n- Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs): This is where it gets complicated. ZKPs for proving that certain data was logged without revealing the data itself... The cryptographic theory exists, but practical implementation for arbitrary AI system event logs is extremely challenging. You'd need to define exactly what property you're proving about the log entry, build a specific circuit for each type of proof, and the computational overhead is enormous. ZKPs are still very immature in production settings - we're talking about systems that take seconds to minutes to generate proofs for even simple circuits. For a logging system that needs to record events in real-time or near-real-time over a system's entire lifetime, this is a significant viability concern.\n- \"Immutable logging\" combined with encryption: This is more straightforward - append-only encrypted logs are well-understood (think of cryptographic hash chains). The immutability part is feasible.\n- The combination of all three (HSM + ZKP + immutable logs) creates a complex system that would be very hard to prototype quickly and debug when things go wrong.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- HSM integration: Moderate complexity, well-documented APIs exist. A few weeks for basic integration.\n- ZKPs: This is the killer problem for prototyping speed. Building a working ZKP pipeline that's actually useful for AI system logs would require either: (a) using existing frameworks like ZoKrates, Circom, or Halo2, but adapting them to our specific logging requirements would take months; or (b) building from scratch which is years. The prototyping timeline here is extremely long and uncertain.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what the actual log schema looks like, what events are being recorded, or how ZKPs map to those events. This vagueness suggests it's more of a conceptual framing than an implementable design.\n- Debugging encrypted + ZKP logs would be extremely painful during development. If something goes wrong in production, you can't easily inspect the logs for troubleshooting.\n\n**Regulatory fit concerns:**\n- Article 12(3) specifically requires recording \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" and \"the identification of natural persons involved in verification.\" If we encrypt these with ZKPs such that even regulators can't read them, do we actually satisfy the requirement? The Act says logs should enable traceability - if the log content is opaque to everyone except through ZKP verification, does that truly satisfy \"traceability\"? This is a legal question, but from an engineering standpoint, it introduces ambiguity about what we need to build.\n- Actually, re-reading: \"allow regulators to verify compliance without exposing raw sensitive input data\" - this suggests the regulator gets some proof but not the data itself. But Article 12(3)(c) says \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" must be recorded. If we ZKP it so only existence is provable, are we actually recording it in the required sense? This creates a compliance risk that would slow down prototyping because we'd need legal sign-off at every design decision.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe HSM + immutable log part is viable and prototypeable relatively quickly (weeks to a couple months). The ZKP component is where the proposal becomes technically risky for my function's concerns:\n1. Prototyping speed: ZKPs would add months of R&D uncertainty before we have anything working end-to-end.\n2. Quality: Debugging encrypted, proof-verified logs in development and testing environments is a nightmare.\n3. Technical viability: The specific application of ZKPs to AI system event logging is not a solved problem. We'd be pioneering here with high failure risk.\n4. Complexity: The combined system (HSM + ZKP + immutable chain) creates too many integration points for reliable prototyping on realistic timelines.\n\nHowever, the core insight - that we need privacy-preserving logs - has merit. A simpler approach (field-level encryption with regulator keys, or selective disclosure) could achieve most of the goal without ZKPs. But I'm not evaluating alternatives; I'm evaluating THIS proposal as stated.\n\nThe proposal as stated commits to HSMs AND zero-knowledge proofs. The ZKP component makes this a research project rather than a viable engineering implementation on any reasonable timeline for prototyping. From my vantage point of technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping, this is too risky and slow.\n\nI'll reject it but give it a moderate promise score because the underlying problem (privacy in logs) is real and the HSM part has some merit, even though the ZKP commitment makes it impractical for my function's priorities. Maybe a 25-35? The concept has value but the specific technical path chosen would be very hard to prototype quickly and reliably.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise around 28.\n",
