uires human judgment, narrative explanation, and domain-specific risk assessment that can't be extracted from MLflow metadata alone. The \"substantial modification detection\" is handwaved.\n\nFrom a pure development viability and prototyping speed standpoint: the basic pipeline (extract → transform → render) is very doable quickly. The full system as described has some scope creep into policy/legal territory that isn't purely engineering. But the core deliverable - automated metadata extraction and structured report generation triggered by code/model changes - is technically sound, well-understood, and prototypably fast.\n\nI'll approve this because:\n1. The technical core (CI/CD + MLflow → structured reports) is very viable\n2. It can be prototyped quickly (weeks, not months) for the basic case\n3. It addresses a real pain point (stale documentation) in a way that aligns with existing engineering practices\n4. Even if it doesn't fully solve \"substantial modification detection,\" the infrastructure it builds is necessary groundwork\n\nBut I won't give it a 100 because the \"substantial modification detection\" and full regulatory coverage are glossed over, and there's a risk of building something that looks automated but still requires significant manual curation to be regulatorily adequate. The prototyping speed for the core is high, but the path to \"regulatorily sufficient\" has uncertain engineering cost.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 72-78. Very doable technically, fast to prototype the core, but the full vision as stated has some hand-waving on the hard parts (substantial modification classification, comprehensive regulatory coverage from metadata alone).\n",
