        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1670 from the perspective of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Human-Verification Identity Tracking System\" - A secure authentication and logging portal for human verification of high-risk AI outputs, specifically to satisfy Article 12(3)(d) which requires identification of natural persons involved in verification of results.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- This is essentially a user authentication system + audit log tied to specific AI outputs. Technically, this is well-understood engineering: identity management (OAuth2/SAML), session management, audit logging, data association between user actions and system results.\n- The \"digital sign-off interface\" is a straightforward web/mobile UI component.\n- The automatic logging of user ID, time, and specific result verified is standard application-level logging with relational or NoSQL storage.\n- This is not technically novel or particularly risky from an implementation standpoint. It's a pattern used in regulated industries (pharma GxP, financial audit trails, etc.) for decades.\n- The main technical considerations: ensuring the log is tamper-evident/immutable (append-only), time synchronization across systems, associating the correct \"result\" to the user action reliably.\n\n**Speed of Prototyping:**\n- This is highly prototypeable. A working MVP could be built in a sprint or two: basic auth, a UI for reviewing AI outputs, and a log that captures who/what/when.\n- The scope is well-bounded: it's not an ML system, it's infrastructure/tooling around existing systems.\n- However, integrating with existing high-risk AI systems' output pipelines requires some coordination - you need to know where the \"result\" lives in your architecture and how to tie a user approval to a specific result instance.\n\n**Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The prototype would be relatively clean: it's CRUD + auth + audit log. Quality concerns are around data integrity (can't delete/modify logs), time accuracy, and correct association.\n- Security considerations matter here (authentication must be robust), but that's standard practice.\n\n**Does it actually address the regulatory requirement well?**\n- Article 12(3)(d) requires \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results, as referred to in Article 14(5).\" This proposal maps directly to that.\n- It's a focused, narrow solution for one specific sub-requirement. That's good from a prototyping standpoint - clear scope, clear deliverable.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Secure authentication\" could balloon in scope if not constrained (MFA, hardware tokens, etc.). But for prototyping, standard auth is sufficient to demonstrate the concept.\n- The proposal says it's \"specifically for Annex III point 1(a)\" systems - that's a good scoping decision, keeps it narrow.\n- One concern: this assumes there IS a human verification step already built into the system workflow (per Article 14(5)). If the broader system doesn't have that workflow, you're building a log for an action that may not exist yet. But from my vantage point, I'm evaluating THIS component's viability, which is solid.\n\n**Verdict:**\nThis is technically very viable, well-scoped, and quickly prototypeable. It maps to a specific legal requirement clearly. From a development engineering perspective, this is low-risk, high-clarity work that could be prototyped rapidly. The main risk is integration with the broader AI system's output pipeline, but the component itself is straightforward.\n\nI'd approve this. Promise score: This is solid, well-scoped technical work. It's not groundbreaking but it's necessary, clear, and quick to build. I'd give it maybe a 78-82 range. It's very doable but also somewhat \"plumbing\" - important b