al; retrofitting logging into deployed systems is expensive and risky\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The proposal says it verifies logs meet \"minimum legal requirements before release\" — but Article 12 requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system,\" not just at deployment. A pre-release test suite only validates initial capability; ongoing compliance still needs monitoring. However, this is a reasonable first step and doesn't make the proposal bad.\n- It says it simulates \"high-risk events\" — for Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically (biometrics), there are very specific minimum logging requirements (paragraph 3). The test suite would need to be tailored per system category. But this is an implementation detail, not a flaw in the proposal.\n- It's scoped as a \"test suite\" within CI/CD — this is a bounded, resource-efficient commitment compared to building an entire compliance monitoring platform.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\nThis is directly on-mission for my function. Article 12 is a \"shall\" requirement (mandatory). Having automated verification that logging capabilities meet the legal standard before deployment is exactly the kind of control a regulator would expect to see as part of a risk management system (Article 9) and quality management system. It's a low-cost, high-impact compliance control that directly reduces enforcement risk and supports our ability to demonstrate compliance during regulatory audits or market surveillance.\n\nThe resource commitment is reasonable — it's a test suite within an existing pipeline, not a new platform. The ROI on compliance risk reduction is high.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: This is very well-aligned with my function. It's not a complete solution (it doesn't address ongoing lifetime monitoring), but as a discrete, actionable proposal that directly maps to a \"shall\" requirement and reduces legal exposure, it's strong. I'd say around 82-85. It's highly aligned but scop