ically scoped to the \"most stringent\" part.\n- \"Digital signatures of natural persons verifying results\" - this maps to Article 14(5) identification requirement in 12(3)(d), but I need to verify: does \"digital signature\" fully satisfy \"identification of natural persons\"? The regulation says \"identification,\" which could be broader than digital signatures. This is a minor point but worth noting.\n- Building this as a separate \"module\" vs. integrating into the system's native logging - Article 12(1) requires the high-risk AI system to \"technically allow for the automatic recording.\" If this is an external add-on module, it might not satisfy the \"technically allow\" requirement which implies the system itself must have the capability. This is a significant compliance concern.\n- However, as a compliance officer, I'm not designing the architecture - I'm assessing whether the proposal advances our compliance position. A specialized audit module for the most critical category IS valuable and addresses real gaps.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification) are where regulators will focus first because they're highest risk\n- Having a dedicated, immutable, access-controlled logging solution for these specific requirements demonstrates proactive compliance posture\n- In case of a market surveillance inspection or complaint, being able to produce clean, tamper-evident logs with restricted access is critical\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Showing customers and partners that we've built specialized compliance infrastructure for the most sensitive category builds trust\n- \"Immutable ledger\" language resonates well in enterprise sales conversations about AI governance\n\n**Resource commitment concern:**\n- This is a focused, bounded scope (one sub-category of one article). Not a sprawling program.\n- The specificity suggests it's implementable with clear acceptance criteria\n- However, it's only usefu