l metadata such as session durations and input matches required by the Act\" — but Article 12(3) also requires (b) reference database against which input data was checked, and (d) identification of natural persons involved in verification under Article 14(5). The proposal mentions \"session durations and input matches\" but doesn't explicitly confirm it captures all four sub-points of Article 12(3). However, this could be a simplification in the summary.\n2. Article 12(2) requires logging for traceability appropriate to intended purpose, identifying risk situations (Art 79(1)), facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 72), and monitoring operations (Art 26(5)). The proposal mentions \"operational events\" broadly but doesn't explicitly address all three sub-requirements of Article 12(2).\n3. The scope says \"all high-risk AI deployments\" — good, comprehensive coverage.\n4. Recital 71 emphasizes logs over \"the duration of the lifetime of the system\" — the proposal's immutable database and centralized nature support this.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point specifically:**\n- This is a foundational, must-have implementation. Without it, we are in direct violation of Article 12(1), which is an absolute obligation (\"shall technically allow for\"). Non-compliance here means the system cannot legally be placed on the market or used within the EU. Institutional survival is at stake.\n- The immutable database design addresses evidentiary integrity — critical if the AI Office or national market surveillance authorities request log access.\n- Standardization across deployments reduces compliance risk of having inconsistent logging practices that could be flagged in audits.\n- Removing researcher manual intervention eliminates a human-factor failure mode that could lead to missing logs (a common finding in regulatory inspections).\n\n**Reputation angle:** If we're found non-compliant on Article 12, it signals poor governance and could affect our ability to operate in the