rithms.\" From my resource-allocation perspective, that's open-ended and hard to scope. The compliance obligation (Article 12) requires me to BUILD the logging system and ensure it captures certain events. It does NOT require me to develop novel forensic analysis tools for diagnosing failure modes. That's a research ambition layered on top of a compliance requirement.\n\nThe attractive part: leveraging already-generated logs for scientific insight is efficient use of infrastructure. If the lab is going to store these logs anyway (and must, per Article 12), mining them for patterns has marginal cost once basic analytics tooling exists.\n\nThe concerning part: \"develop novel analytical algorithms\" and \"explore how high-granularity logs can be used to diagnose emergent risks\" are vague, open-ended research goals. There's no clear deliverable, no defined scope, no bounded timeline. From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is the kind of proposal that could consume significant engineering time while producing uncertain outputs. The lab has limited resources and must prioritize compliance implementation first.\n\nHowever, I should judge on its own merits. Is there merit? Yes — understanding failure modes from logs is genuinely valuable for the lab's post-market monitoring obligations (Article 72) and for operational safety. But the framing as a \"research project\" with \"novel algorithms\" suggests it's more exploratory than applied. A bounded version of this (e.g., \"develop basic alerting rules on our logging infrastructure to flag anomalous patterns\") would be much more resource-efficient.\n\nGiven my vantage point emphasizing resource-allocation efficiency and funding constraints:\n- The proposal is scientifically interesting but under-specified as a commitment\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (the logs we must keep anyway) — that's a plus\n- But \"novel analytical algorithms\" is an open-ended R&D bet, not a bounded implementation task\n- T