        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2363\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The intent of avoiding per-team re-implementation of logging is sound, but the stated scope—a single 'plug-and-play' middleware that uniformly handles event capture across all heterogeneous high-risk AI architectures (biometric matching, NLP decision-support, CV pipelines, etc.) with different Article 12(3) minimums and distinct risk-event taxonomies—is technically unviable as a near-term deliverable; the integration surface per system is large enough that 'by default' capture will miss domain-specific events required under Art. 12(2)(a)-(c). From a prototyping-speed standpoint, committing scarce engineering headcount to a big-bang shared library before it has been validated against even one concrete pipeline would delay every team's ability to ship and iterate, the exact opposite of what the proposal claims to enable.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 38\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2363 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution whose domain is \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n\"Unified Compliance Logging Middleware. A standardized software library designed for automatic event recording across all high-risk AI projects. The engineering team will develop a plug-and-play API that captures timestamps, input references, and system states by default throughout the lifecycle. This prevents redundant effort across different research teams and ensures uniform compliance efficiency.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A \"unified middleware\" that works \"across all high-risk AI projects\" is an extremely ambitious claim. Different high-risk AI systems (Annex III categories) have vastly different architectures — some are NLP pipelines, some are computer vision systems, some are biometric matching systems, some are decision-support tools. The logging requirements differ by category (Article 12(3) has specific minimums for biometric identification under Annex III point 1(a), but other high-risk categories have different needs).\n- \"Plug-and-play API\" is a strong claim. In practice, instrumenting diverse ML pipelines with a single unified library is technically challenging. The \"input references\" and \"system states\" are highly system-specific — what constitutes an \"event\" in a real-time biometric matching system vs. a hiring decision-support tool is fundamentally different.\n- Capturing \"timestamps, input references, and system states by default throughout the lifecycle\" sounds reasonable at a high level but glosses over enormous implementation complexity. Data retention, storage scaling, performance overhead in inference pipelines — these are non-trivial engineering challenges.\n- The \"by default\" aspect is interesting — automatic recording without per-team configuration could mean missing domain-specific events that Article 12(2) requires (events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring facilitation, operation monitoring).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- On the positive side: if it works, a shared middleware library would save each research team from building their own logging infrastructure. This is genuinely valuable for prototyping speed — teams wouldn't need to figure out compliance logging from scratch every time.\n- On the negative side: developing this unified middleware itself is a massive engineering project that would consume the very scarce implementation resources I'm supposed to allocate carefully. Before it can help any team prototype faster, months (likely longer) of development go into building it. This is a classic \"boil the ocean\" risk.\n- The \"plug-and-play\" framing suggests it's meant to be drop-in, but in practice, integrating middleware into existing ML pipelines often requires significant refactoring. This could actually slow down prototyping initially.\n- There's also a risk of over-engineering: building a generic solution before understanding the specific logging needs of 2-3 concrete projects may produce something that doesn't fit well.\n\n**My assessment:** The intent is sound — shared infrastructure to avoid redundant compliance work across teams. But from a technical viability and prototyping speed standpoint, this is a high-risk, long-horizon investment. The \"unified\" and \"plug-and-play\" framing significantly undersells the engineering complexity of making one logging layer work across heterogeneous AI system architectures with different risk profiles and different Article 12(3) minimum requirements. It would consume substantial implementation resources before delivering value to any specific project, and the generic nature of the solution risks being too rigid or too loose for individual teams' needs.\n\nI'd lean toward reject from my vantage point, but it's not a terrible idea — just premature and over-scoped. A more viable version would be: start with logging infrastructure in 1-2 concrete high-risk projects, extract patterns, then consider shared components. But as stated — \"unified,\" \"across all,\" \"plug-and-play\" — this is a big-bang infrastructure bet that I can't vouch for technical viability on.\n\nPromise score: The underlying idea has merit (shared logging to avoid redundancy), but the execution framing (\"unified, plug-and-play, across ALL projects\") makes it technically risky and slow to deliver value relative to resource consumption. From my speed/prototyping vantage point, I'd give this maybe a 35-40. It's not garbage, but I wouldn't commit scarce implementation resources to it as stated. Let me settle on 38 — the concept is reasonable but the scope and \"plug-and-play\" framing make it technically unviable as described for near-term prototyping speed.\n\nDecision: reject. The proposal as scoped would consume significant engineering capacity before any team benefits, the technical feasibility of a truly unified plug-and-play solution across heterogeneous AI architectures is questionable, and it doesn't accelerate protoping — it potentially delays it by creating a dependency on infrastructure that isn't built yet.\n",
