r CI/CD pipeline\" - this suggests leveraging existing build infrastructure rather than creating new systems from scratch.\n- Version control is likely already in place for code; extending it to data records and validation metrics is a natural extension.\n- The \"integrated toolset\" language could mean building something new, but the phrasing suggests integration with what exists.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a solid proposal that addresses a genuine compliance requirement (Recital 71) using an approach that leverages existing infrastructure (CI/CD pipeline). From a resource-allocation standpoint, automating documentation updates in the build process is more efficient than manual tracking, especially for an R&D lab that iterates frequently. The upfront engineering cost is justified by the recurring compliance need and the reduction in ongoing manual effort.\n\nHowever, I should note:\n- \"Integrated toolset\" could be scope creep - what exactly does this include? But the description is reasonable.\n- It focuses on technical documentation (Recital 71) rather than the logging requirements of Article 12 itself. Recital 71 discusses keeping documentation up to date, which is a different (though related) obligation from Article 12's automatic event logging. The proposal addresses the \"living technical documentation\" aspect correctly.\n- From my vantage point on resource efficiency: this is a good use of engineering time because it converts recurring manual work into automated pipeline steps. It doesn't require new infrastructure per se, just extension of existing CI/CD capabilities.\n\nI'd rate this as a reasonably strong proposal. It's compliance-mandated (not optional), leverages existing infrastructure, and provides long-term efficiency gains. The main risk is scope definition - \"integrated toolset\" is vague enough to potentially balloon in cost. But the core concept is sound and well-aligned with my domain concerns.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's