y addresses my concern about not diverting researcher time from core work.\n\n4. **Scalability**: As the lab takes on more high-risk AI projects (which it will, given the EU AI Act's expanding scope), this framework scales without linearly increasing compliance overhead.\n\n**Concerns:**\n1. **Upfront development cost**: Building a robust, modular library with API wrappers, immutable ledgers, and integration into templates is not trivial. It requires dedicated engineering resources up front. However, compared to N teams each building their own solution, the amortized cost is much lower.\n\n2. **Scope creep risk**: \"All high-risk AI projects\" is ambitious. Different Annex III categories have different logging requirements (Article 12(3) specifies minimums for biometric ID systems specifically). A truly unified framework needs to handle varying compliance profiles. This adds complexity.\n\n3. **\"Immutable, time-stamped ledger\"** — this sounds good on paper but introduces storage and maintenance costs that grow over the system's lifetime (the Act requires records throughout the system's lifetime). Need to consider long-term data retention infrastructure.\n\n4. **Integration burden**: Even if the framework exists, integrating it into diverse research codebases still takes effort. \"Standardized API wrapper\" helps but different model architectures and deployment patterns may require varying adapter work.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a high-leverage proposal that directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by preventing N-fold duplication of compliance engineering. It uses existing infrastructure (research templates) as the integration point rather than building parallel systems. It protects scientific ambition by automating compliance at the middleware level, so researchers aren't pulled into regulatory implementation tasks. The upfront investment is justified by the multi-project amortization.\n\nThe main risk is over-scoping — tr