erly cadence is a concern for fast-moving AI research where things can shift rapidly.\n- That said, the proposal's stated intent to protect early-stage R&D from premature compliance demands is aligned with preserving scientific freedom and intellectual exploration. It doesn't propose new technical work or alter the science — it proposes governance around when logging obligations kick in.\n- From a broader scientific impact perspective: if this protocol actually works as intended, it could help the lab maintain research velocity while still being compliant. But there's a risk that the classification process itself becomes a bottleneck, and researchers may self-censor or frame their work narrowly to avoid triggering higher compliance tiers.\n- The proposal doesn't contribute anything to the knowledge frontier directly — it's purely an internal governance mechanism. Its value is instrumental (protecting research freedom) rather than substantive (advancing knowledge).\n\nFrom my vantage point, I'm somewhat torn. The spirit of protecting early-stage research from premature regulatory burden aligns with my values around intellectual freedom and the knowledge frontier. But the mechanism — a quarterly review board that assigns tiers based on \"intended use case\" — introduces a gatekeeping function that could stifle exactly the kind of exploratory, boundary-pushing work I care about. The very act of having to classify your research into compliance tiers at an early stage may constrain how researchers think about and pursue their work.\n\nI think this is borderline. It's not a proposal that advances knowledge directly. It's a governance proposal. From my vantage point, its value depends entirely on whether the implementation preserves or constrains research freedom. The stated intent is protective, but the mechanism (quarterly review board, tier assignment based on intended use case) introduces friction and potential chilling effects.\n\nI'll lean toward rejecting it from my