al documentation\" aspect mentioned in Recital 71.\n- Article 12(2)-(3) require specific event logging capabilities built INTO the high-risk AI system itself (start/end times of use, reference databases, input data matches, verification personnel IDs). A pipeline-level doc generator doesn't inherently solve this — the system needs to be instrumented at runtime to capture those events. This proposal seems to address the \"technical documentation\" side more than the \"automatic event logging\" side.\n- However, as a development engineer focused on prototyping speed and technical viability: this is a well-scoped, achievable integration project. It leverages existing tools (experiment trackers) with custom glue code. The architecture is sound.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This directly reduces the overhead engineers face when iterating on models — if documentation auto-generates from the pipeline, you don't have to manually write update reports each time you retrain or deploy.\n- It's a force multiplier for prototyping: you can iterate faster knowing that the compliance trail is maintained automatically.\n- The \"real-time\" aspect means engineers don't accumulate documentation debt between iterations.\n- However, building this pipeline itself takes engineering resources up front. The ROI question: is the one-time build cost justified vs. manual documentation? For a lab with multiple models in flight, yes.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Real-time\" is a bit vague — what's the actual trigger mechanism? Webhook on deploy? Cron job? This needs specification but isn't a viability blocker.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) auto-generating technical documentation, and (2) ensuring the system itself has the logging capabilities required by Art 12(1)-(3). These are related but distinct engineering challenges. The runtime event capture (Art 12(3)(a)-(d)) requires instrumenting the inference/deployment layer, not just the training pipeline.\n- But as a 