lized approach reduces the chance of missing a project or having inconsistent implementation.\n- Market reputation: A standardized approach shows institutional maturity and regulatory seriousness.\n\nThe proposal doesn't need to be perfect in its description - it's an architectural direction. The specific gaps (full input data vs hashes, person identification for biometric systems) would be addressed during detailed design. The fundamental architecture is sound from a compliance perspective.\n\nMy decision: This clears my bar. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1), supports the traceability requirements in Article 12(2), and creates an institutional mechanism that significantly reduces regulatory risk across the portfolio. The CI/CD integration ensures it's not optional. I approve.\n\nPromise score: This is very strong from a compliance perspective. It addresses the exact letter of Article 12(1) with \"automatic recording\" via software library, supports traceability (Recital 71), creates institutional consistency that reduces audit risk, and the immutability feature is excellent for demonstrating integrity to regulators. I'd give this a high score - maybe 88-90. It's not a 100 because it doesn't explicitly address all sub-requirements of Article 12(3) (particularly person identification for Annex III 1(a)), and the \"input hashes\" vs full data point needs clarification. But the architecture is fundamentally sound and directly on-point.\n\nI'll go with 88.\n",
