ents of Article 12 (which has very specific fields like start/end timestamps, reference database references, input data matches, person identification) could yield novel results in schema-aware compression.\n\n**Intellectual merit:**\n- The framing \"converts a regulatory burden into a new area of publishable research\" is a bit cynical from a pure research standpoint, but the underlying questions are legitimate: How do you design logging systems that are information-theoretically sufficient for traceability while being computationally and storage efficient? This touches on information theory, database systems, streaming algorithms, and regulatory compliance engineering.\n- However, \"selective sampling techniques to maintain full traceability\" is somewhat contradictory as stated. If you sample selectively, by definition you don't have FULL traceability — you have partial traceability with some statistical guarantee. The intellectual merit depends on whether the research can rigorously define what level of statistical completeness suffices for compliance. This is a genuinely hard and interesting problem but it's also potentially at odds with the literal text of the regulation, which seems to demand complete recording (\"over the lifetime of the system\").\n- The proposal frames this as \"core R&D track\" which signals commitment of resources.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:**\n- If successful, compressed compliance logging could be broadly applicable beyond EU AI Act — any regulated industry (finance, healthcare) faces similar traceability vs. efficiency tradeoffs.\n- Published research on provably-safe selective sampling for audit trails could contribute to the broader literature on streaming data management and compliance systems.\n- However, the impact is somewhat bounded: this is an engineering optimization problem more than a fundamental scientific advance.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The \"selective sampling\" language is potentially at odds wi