e efficiency.\n- It covers the \"lifetime of the system\" requirement in Article 12(1).\n\n**Concerns from my perspective:**\n- Building an \"open-source framework\" is a significant engineering commitment. Is this the most efficient path to compliance? Could we instead integrate with existing tools (MLflow, W&B, DVC) rather than building our own? The proposal doesn't address whether existing infrastructure already covers much of this.\n- \"Across all experiments\" — this is ambitious scope. In a resource-constrained lab, instrumenting *every* experiment could be expensive and slow adoption. Researcher buy-in matters for efficiency.\n- Article 12(3) has very specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). The proposal talks generally about \"hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime events\" but doesn't specifically address those granular requirements (start/end timestamps of each use, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons in verification). This is a gap.\n- \"Designed to meet Article 12 standards while remaining flexible for exploratory work\" — this is the right instinct (we don't want compliance overhead to kill research velocity), but the tension between \"strict logging\" and \"flexibility for exploration\" needs careful design.\n- The open-source aspect: while potentially cost-saving long-term, it also means we're committing to public maintenance, documentation standards, community support — ongoing resource commitments that could be non-trivial in a lab with tight funding.\n- However, the proposal is about *building* the tool, not just exploring whether it might work. It's a concrete implementation commitment.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- We NEED to comply with Article 12 for our high-risk systems. This isn't optional R&D — it's a regulatory obligation. So some investment here is mandatory regardless.\n- The question is: what's the most efficient path? Building an