ual notes.\n- Implementation complexity: moderate-low. You need to define the schema, write the hooks, set up the database. For a competent team, this is weeks of work, not months.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 specifically:**\n- Article 12(1) requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - yes, capturing experiment metadata automatically satisfies part of this.\n- Article 12(2)(a)-(c): identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, monitoring operations - the provenance data supports these by providing the context needed to trace why a model behaved a certain way.\n- Recital 71 specifically mentions \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" - this proposal directly addresses that: hyperparameters (training), dataset versions (data), validation metrics (testing/validation).\n\n**Concerns/limitations from my vantage:**\n- The proposal focuses on the DEVELOPMENT phase documentation. Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system\" which includes deployment/runtime logging, not just training-time metadata. So this addresses a subset of the requirement but is a valid and necessary component.\n- It doesn't address runtime event logging (the actual \"logs\" of a deployed system), but that's a different engineering problem. The proposal is scoped to what it claims: provenance/training documentation.\n- For a high-risk system under Annex III point 1(a) (biometrics), Article 12(3) requires specific runtime logs (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, verifier identification). This proposal doesn't cover those runtime requirements either.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is technically very viable. It's a well-scoped, low-risk engineering task that addresses a genuine part of the regulatory requirement. From my perspective on prototyping speed and technical feasibility:\n- It's buildable with existing patterns (MLflow-style tracking)\n- It won't slow down experimentation if designed as lightweight hooks\n- It provides immediate value for documentation compliance\n- The scope is appropriately bounded\n\nHowever, it only addresses the development/training documentation aspect of Recital 71 and Article 12(1)-(2), not the runtime logging aspects. But that's fine - it's a valid component of the overall solution. As a standalone proposal to pursue, it clears my bar: it's technically sound, implementable quickly, and doesn't impede prototyping velocity.\n\nI'd say this is a solid approve with high promise. It's not revolutionary but it's exactly the kind of practical, buildable infrastructure work that makes regulatory compliance achievable without derailing the research pipeline. I'll give it around 82-85. It's clearly viable and useful, but it's only one piece of the puzzle (doesn't cover runtime logging), and it's relatively standard engineering rather than novel R&D.\n",
