s:**\n- Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics-related). The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data, and system versions\" but doesn't explicitly mention the reference database against which input data was checked, or identification of natural persons involved in verification. However, this might be a scope limitation — the proposal is about the general framework, and project-specific extensions could handle Annex III specifics.\n- The proposal says \"timestamps, input data, and system versions\" — but Article 12(2)(a) requires logging events relevant to identifying situations that may present a risk or substantial modification. Just capturing timestamps and input data might not be sufficient if the framework doesn't capture *events* (e.g., model drift detection triggers, confidence threshold breaches, anomaly flags). The proposal is somewhat narrow in what it captures.\n- \"Centralized software library\" — from a compliance standpoint, this is actually good because it creates a single source of truth and makes audits easier. But it could also be a single point of failure if the library has a bug that silently drops logs.\n- Integration into \"standard ML training and deployment templates\" — this is good for deployment-phase logging but what about training-phase documentation? Recital 71 mentions \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used.\" The proposal focuses on runtime/deployment logging primarily.\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\nThis is a strong, directly relevant proposal. It addresses the core mandatory requirement of Article 12(1) in a scalable, systematic way that reduces compliance risk across the institution. The \"without manual intervention\" aspect is particularly important because it removes human error as a failure mode for log generation. From an institutional survival standpoint, having a unified framework means if we get audited, we 