ng research funds, it indirectly protects the lab's ability to push the frontier. But that's a very indirect connection.\n\nMy assessment: From the perspective of someone whose domain is the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact, this proposal is an administrative/financial mechanism. It has no direct bearing on any of those three criteria. It doesn't propose any new method, technique, or research direction. It's a budget management suggestion. While it might indirectly protect research capacity by not letting compliance costs eat into discovery budgets, that's a secondary effect and the proposal itself contains no intellectual content related to research advancement.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: if compliance engineering is done poorly or squeezed, it could create technical debt or force researchers to spend time on unglamorous logging work, pulling them away from frontier research. Ring-fencing that work does protect the research mission. But the proposal itself doesn't advance any knowledge — it's purely organizational.\n\nI think this should be a reject with a low promise score. It's not harmful, but from my specific vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, scientific impact), it simply doesn't clear the bar. It's an operations/finance proposal, not a research or technical proposal that advances understanding or capability in AI systems.\n\nLet me set:\n- Rationale: This is a financial structuring mechanism with no direct contribution to advancing the knowledge frontier, generating intellectual merit, or creating broader scientific impact. While it indirectly protects research budgets by ring-fencing compliance costs, the proposal itself contains no technical or methodological content relevant to my domain.\n- Decision: reject\n- Promise: Maybe 10-15? It's not harmful and has a small indirect benefit to protecting research time, but from my specific professional vantage point it essentially doesn't regis