cle requires documentation updates before proceeding, that slows down iteration. But it's a reasonable tradeoff for compliance.\n- Monthly audits by compliance officers are a process step, not really an engineering deliverable.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping impact:**\n- The mandatory sign-off during each training cycle introduces a gating dependency. If documentation isn't ready, the model can't be \"released.\" This could slow prototyping cycles.\n- However, if the documentation system is well-designed (automated metadata extraction from training runs, templated doc updates), the overhead could be minimal.\n- The proposal doesn't specify how much of this is automated vs. manual. If it's mostly manual researcher sign-off on capability/limitation statements for every training cycle, that's a significant bottleneck for fast prototyping.\n\n**Does it actually address Article 12?**\n- Recital 71 mentions keeping technical documentation up to date - yes, this addresses that.\n- But Article 12(1) specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" over the lifetime. This proposal is about documentation updates, not logging infrastructure. It addresses the recital's documentation requirement but not the core Article 12 logging mandate.\n- That said, the proposal frames itself as satisfying Recital 71's documentation requirement, which is a legitimate component of the broader record-keeping obligation.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my technical viability and prototyping speed perspective:\n- The engineering effort is modest and technically feasible (version-controlled docs linked to model versions).\n- But it introduces process overhead that could slow prototyping cycles without specifying automation.\n- It's more of a governance/process proposal than a technical solution. The actual technical implementation (a Git-based doc repo with links to model artifacts) is straightforward but the proposal doesn't detail the technical architecture.\n- The mandatory sign-off per training cycle is a potential bottleneck for fast iteration.\n- It partially addresses the regulatory requirement (documentation currency) but not the core logging requirement of Article 12(1).\n\nI think this is moderately viable technically - the engineering isn't hard - but it's primarily an administrative/process proposal that will add friction to prototyping without clear technical architecture. It's not a strong \"technical solution\" from my vantage point. It's more of a compliance process that we'll need to accommodate, and the engineering work to support it (versioned docs linked to models) is straightforward but not particularly innovative or high-value from an R&D perspective.\n\nI'd lean toward approving it because:\n- The technical implementation is feasible and low-complexity\n- Linking docs to model versions aligns with existing practices\n- It addresses a real regulatory requirement\n\nBut I'd give it a moderate promise score because:\n- It's more process than technology\n- It could slow prototyping without sufficient automation\n- It doesn't address the core Article 12(1) logging requirement\n- The technical architecture isn't specified - it's vague on HOW documentation gets updated (automated? manual?)\n\nI'll approve with a moderate promise score, maybe around 45-55. The engineering is doable but the proposal as stated is more administrative than technical, and could introduce friction to prototyping speed. It addresses part of the regulatory picture but not the core logging mandate.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 48. It's technically feasible and aligns with existing versioning practices, but it's primarily an administrative process that adds gating to training cycles without specifying sufficient automation to protect prototyping velocity.\n",
