        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2932\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A single unified logging layer integrated into our existing CI/CD pipelines is the most capital-efficient path to Article 12 compliance: it eliminates per-team duplication, leverages infrastructure we already operate, and directly recovers researcher hours that would otherwise be spent on manual documentation. The main reservation from a resource-allocation standpoint is that building model-call interception middleware across heterogeneous pipelines is a non-trivial engineering commitment with ongoing maintenance cost, and the 'consistent interface' must still accommodate the system-specific event requirements in Article 12(3) without degenerating into a per-pipeline fork; however, even accounting for those risks, the alternative of fragmented per-team logging would consume far more aggregate researcher time and create no shared asset.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2932 from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is for a \"Unified High-Risk Logging Infrastructure\" — a standardized middleware layer that intercepts model calls to log metadata into an immutable database, integrated into CI/CD pipelines, providing consistent interfaces for timestamps, input matches, and user verifications.\n\nLet me assess this from my specific vantage points:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- A single unified infrastructure serving all high-risk AI pipelines is fundamentally more efficient than having each research team build their own logging. This eliminates duplication.\n- However, \"middleware APIs that intercept model calls\" is a significant engineering undertaking. Building an interception layer into every pipeline is non-trivial — it requires modifying how models are invoked across the lab, which could disrupt ongoing research.\n- The integration into existing CI/CD pipelines is smart from a resource perspective — leveraging what we already have rather than building parallel infrastructure.\n- \"Immutable, version-controlled database\" adds operational overhead (storage costs, maintenance) but is necessary for compliance and audit trails.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is a compliance-driven build, not a science-enabling one. It doesn't advance research capability per se.\n- However, it's framed as reducing \"manual documentation burden on individual researchers,\" which frees up researcher time for actual science. That's a genuine resource reallocation benefit.\n- The cost of building this middleware is substantial and ongoing (maintenance, updates as models change). In a constrained funding environment, committing engineering resources to compliance infrastructure means fewer resources for R&D. But the alternative — having each team handle logging individually — would be even more expensive in aggregate researcher time.\n- The \"unified\" aspect means one build serves all teams, which is the most capital-efficient approach.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Integration into existing CI/CD pipelines is a strong point — it doesn't require new deployment infrastructure, leverages what's already in place.\n- However, intercepting model calls at the middleware level may not always be clean with our current architecture. If we have heterogeneous model serving (different frameworks, different deployment targets), building one interception layer that works across all of them could be more complex than it sounds.\n- The \"standardized software layer\" aspiration is good but the reality of retrofitting logging into diverse existing pipelines may require significant per-pipeline adaptation work.\n\n**Risks from my vantage:**\n- Scope creep: \"all high-risk AI pipelines\" is a large surface area. If we don't define boundaries clearly, this could become an ever-expanding project that consumes engineering resources indefinitely.\n- The proposal says it will \"ensure baseline legal compliance\" — but Article 12(3) has very specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). A generic middleware may need significant customization per system type to capture the right events. The \"consistent interface\" might be too rigid or too loose.\n- Building this in-house vs. using existing logging/observability tooling: the proposal doesn't explicitly say build-from-scratch, but \"middleware APIs that intercept model calls\" suggests custom development. If we could leverage existing observability stacks (e.g., OpenTelemetry-style instrumentation), it might be cheaper and faster.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable, well-scoped proposal from an efficiency standpoint. The unified approach is the right call — building once for all pipelines beats N separate solutions. CI/CD integration leverages existing infrastructure. Reducing researcher burden is a real benefit. However, I have concerns about:\n1. The engineering cost of the interception layer across heterogeneous systems\n2. Whether \"unified\" truly captures the system-specific requirements in Article 12(3) without becoming overly complex\n3. Ongoing maintenance costs eating into R&D budget\n\nIt's a good proposal but not without significant execution risk and ongoing cost. From my vantage, it's worth pursuing because the alternative (fragmented per-team logging) is worse for resource efficiency, but I'd want tight scoping and phased delivery. It clears my bar as something that improves long-run resource allocation even if it costs upfront engineering time.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's aligned with my priorities (efficiency, leveraging existing infra, freeing researcher time) but has execution risks that prevent me from giving it a very high score. I'd say around 72-78.\n",
