l and robust approach to ensuring you can prove which version was used at any given time.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- It only addresses Annex III, point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). If the lab develops other high-risk AI systems under different categories, this wouldn't cover them. However, the proposal is scoped to what it claims to do, so that's not necessarily a flaw in the proposal itself.\n- Article 12(3)(a) requires recording of start and end date/time of each use - the proposal doesn't explicitly mention timestamping of sessions (though \"real time\" tracking might imply this).\n- Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons involved in verification - not addressed here. But again, this is a focused component, not necessarily claiming to be a complete solution.\n- The proposal says it satisfies the mandates regarding \"database references and input data matches\" - which maps to 12(3)(b) and (c). It's honest about its scope.\n- From a compliance perspective, having an immutable, hash-based lineage is actually stronger than what the minimum requirement asks for. The Act requires you can identify the reference database; a versioned hash system provides that with cryptographic integrity.\n- \"Real time\" integration adds operational robustness - logs are created at the moment of inference rather than potentially reconstructed later.\n\n**Institutional survival perspective:** This is a focused, implementable technical solution to a specific regulatory requirement. It's not overreaching, it's not speculative. It maps directly to Article 12(3)(b) and (c). The immutability provides defensibility if audited or if a complaint arises.\n\n**Regulatory compliance perspective:** Directly addresses two of the four minimum requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems. The versioning approach is robust and exceeds minimum requirements in terms of verifiability. However, it's only one component - you still need timestamps (3a) and person identification (3d). But as a discrete proposal, it does what it says it does.\n\n**Market reputation perspective:** Having immutable data lineage demonstrates serious compliance commitment to partners, regulators, and customers. It signals technical maturity.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a solid, focused proposal that directly addresses specific regulatory text. From my compliance vantage point, I would approve this because:\n1. It maps precisely to Article 12(3)(b) and (c)\n2. The immutability/hash approach provides stronger evidence than the minimum requirement\n3. It's technically specific and implementable\n4. It supports the traceability purpose stated in Recital 71\n5. It integrates with existing event logs (Article 12(1)) rather than creating a parallel system\n\nThe scope limitation (only Annex III 1(a), only two of four sub-requirements) is appropriate for a focused proposal - it doesn't claim to be the complete logging solution, just one component.\n\nI'd rate this around 82-85. It's strong, directly compliant, well-scoped, and technically sound. It's not a 90+ because it only covers part of the requirement set, but as a discrete proposal doing what it says, it clears the bar decisively.\n",
