e \"all high-risk AI projects\" — but in an R&D institution, systems are heterogeneous. Different models use different training pipelines, different data sources, different deployment environments. A truly universal logging layer might be over-engineered or under-functional for specific edge cases.\n\nHowever, the proposal is scoped to what Article 12 actually requires: timestamps (start/end of use), database references, input data matching, and user identification. These are relatively standardized operations that CAN be abstracted into a common API. The middleware doesn't need to understand the AI system's internals — it just needs to capture these metadata events at the right hooks.\n\n**Risk assessment from my vantage point:**\n- Risk: Over-engineering. Building a \"unified\" framework for everything might take too long before it's useful, and teams might build stopgap solutions in the meantime.\n- Mitigation: The scope is reasonably bounded (timestamps, DB refs, user IDs) — these are standard concerns. A phased rollout is feasible.\n- Risk: One-size-fits-all doesn't fit all R&D environments. Research prototypes vs. production systems have very different logging needs.\n- Mitigation: The proposal says \"plug-and-play module\" suggesting it's modular and can be adapted per project.\n\n**Resource commitment:** This requires committing infrastructure engineering time to build the library, maintain it over time, and provide support to research teams. But the alternative — each team building its own logging compliance layer — would consume far more total engineer-hours across the institution. The ROI is clearly positive if there are multiple high-risk AI projects (which in an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, there almost certainly are).\n\n**Verdict:** This clears my bar strongly. It's a textbook example of how to use scarce engineering resources efficiently by creating shared infrastructure that serves many downstream consumers. The scope is appropriately bounded by what Article 12 actually requires. It doesn't compete with scientific ambition — it removes a compliance burden from research teams so they can focus on science.\n\nMy promise score: This is very strong from my vantage point. I'd say 85-90. It's not a perfect solution (heterogeneity concerns, maintenance burden, potential for the framework to lag behind evolving regulatory guidance), but it's clearly the right structural approach and the ROI math works out. I'll go with 87.\n",
