cording, Art. 12(1))\n2. The architecture is sound for scaling compliance across the institution\n3. Decoupling compliance from research is a critical governance principle\n4. Versioned database supports audit and traceability\n5. Universal coverage eliminates gaps\n\nThe main risk is that it's described at too high a level to guarantee full Art. 12(2)-(3) compliance without further specification, but as an implementation commitment with the stated architecture, I can approve it while noting the need for detailed event taxonomy during implementation.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's directionally excellent and architecturally sound from a compliance standpoint, but the under-specification of which specific events (beyond I/O) are captured gives me some concern about full coverage of Art. 12(2)(a)-(c) and especially 12(3). It's not a \"without hesitation\" 90+ because I need to see that it captures more than just inputs/outputs — it needs internal state events, risk-triggering events, reference database provenance, etc.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
