or logging (which should be low-overhead, reliable, and fast), MPC would add substantial friction.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- ZKPs are notoriously difficult to implement correctly. Circuit design for general-purpose log verification would be an enormous undertaking. The prototyping timeline for even a basic PoC would be months to years, not weeks.\n- MPC implementations in production AI systems are extraordinarily rare and complex. The engineering overhead for prototyping within \"existing AI architectures\" is severe - you'd need to rearchitect logging pipelines around cryptographic primitives that add orders of magnitude more computation.\n- The proposal says \"prototype these methods within our existing AI architectures to test feasibility.\" This is a massive undertaking masquerading as a prototype. ZKP circuits for log verification are not off-the-shelf; they require custom circuit design, verifier integration, and ongoing maintenance.\n\n**Does it actually solve the problem?**\n- The core tension described (Article 12 vs GDPR) is somewhat overstated. Article 12 requires logging of specific events. GDPR applies to personal data in those logs. The standard solution is access controls, data minimization, retention policies, and encryption at rest/in transit - all well-established engineering practices that don't require ZKPs or MPC.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), the logging requirements are specific: start/end times, reference database, matched input data, identification of verifying persons. These are factual operational records. Making them \"zero-knowledge\" would defeat their purpose - the regulator and the operator need to be able to see what happened to verify compliance.\n- The proposal frames this as a research initiative rather than an engineering solution. From my vantage point, committing scarce implementation resources to explore whether ZKPs *might* work for logging is not the right use of engineering bandwidth when standard cryptographic and access-control solutions already address 90%+ of the concern.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint, this proposal conflates two very different problems: (1) implementing compliant logging (which is straightforward engineering with existing tools), and (2) solving an edge-case privacy tension in log storage/access (which can be addressed with standard security practices). The proposed solutions (ZKPs, MPC, differential privacy on logs) are technically either inappropriate for the actual requirement or so complex that prototyping them would consume enormous resources without clear regulatory benefit.\n\nThe \"competitive advantage\" framing is a business/marketing concern, not a technical viability one. From my vantage point, this is a high-risk, low-reward engineering commitment with unclear path to a shippable prototype and questionable alignment with what the regulation actually requires.\n\nI'll reject this. The promise score should reflect that while the underlying problem (privacy in logs) is real, the proposed technical approach is poorly matched to the actual regulatory requirement and would be extremely difficult to prototype meaningfully within reasonable timeframes. I'd give it maybe a 15-20 - there's a kernel of a valid concern but the solution path is technically misaligned and the prototyping burden is enormous for uncertain return.\n\nLet me settle on 15. The problem identification has some merit (privacy in logs matters), but the technical approach is fundamentally mismatched to what Article 12 requires, and the engineering cost to even prototype ZKP-based log verification would be prohibitive relative to standard solutions.\n",
