ication step needs careful design.\n- Actually, re-reading: Article 12(3)(d) says \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\" This is about recording WHO verified, not necessarily building a whole new portal. The proposal slightly over-engineers this as a \"gated review portal\" when it might be more of an integration into an existing system's workflow.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- The proposal frames this as a standalone \"portal,\" which could create integration challenges with existing systems. From a prototyping standpoint, building a separate portal vs. integrating audit logging into the existing pipeline are very different efforts.\n- \"Preventing costly regulatory sanctions\" is motivation, not a technical requirement — it doesn't change the engineering approach.\n- The proposal correctly identifies that this targets Annex III 1(a) systems specifically (biometric identification, age estimation, emotion recognition, etc.), which narrows scope appropriately.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point: This is technically very viable (mature tech stack), fast to prototype (standard web app patterns), and the scope is well-bounded. The main risk I see is over-engineering it as a standalone portal vs. integrating into existing workflows, but that's an implementation detail, not a viability issue.\n\nThe proposal is solid from a prototyping standpoint — low technical risk, clear requirements, established patterns. It's not groundbreaking, but it doesn't need to be. The question is whether the lab should commit resources to this specific build vs. whether it might overlap with other proposals or existing systems. But I'm told to judge each on its own merits without comparing.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar for approval: technically viable (high confidence), fast to prototype, well-scoped to a specific regulatory requirement. My promise score would be moderate-to-high — maybe 72-78. It's solid but not exciting; it's a \"