e model changes rapidly. Generating full regulatory dossiers in real-time during prototyping could be over-engineering and add friction to the development loop. The documentation requirement applies to the lifetime of the system, but during rapid iteration/prototyping, you don't need a fully generated compliance dossier for every experiment.\n- A more pragmatic approach would be: track everything (which experiment tools do), then generate the formal regulatory document at specific milestones or when needed, rather than \"in real time during R&D.\"\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) tracking data/parameters (easy, already done by MLflow etc.) and (2) generating formatted compliance reports in real time (harder, potentially unnecessary friction).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Real time\" is vague and potentially over-scoped. What does \"real time\" mean for a regulatory dossier? If it means every time you change a hyperparameter, the formal document updates — that's technically possible but adds infrastructure complexity without proportional benefit during prototyping.\n- The proposal doesn't address Article 12 specifically (the logging of events, start/end times, reference databases, etc.) which is the actual regulatory text being addressed. It focuses on Recital 71's documentation aspect. That said, it does contribute to record-keeping broadly.\n- \"Compliance reports in real time\" — who defines what a compliance report contains? The Act requires specific elements (algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk management). Auto-generating all of this is a significant engineering effort.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point:\n- The core idea (automate documentation sync from experiment tracking) is technically sound and would save engineering time in the long run.\n- The \"real time\" framing is problematic for prototyping — it suggests adding overhead to every development step, which slows iteration.\n- It's a good supporting infrastructure piece but not a complete solution to Article 12 (which requires specific logging capabilities).\n- As a prototype: building an MVP that tracks hyperparameters and data lineage and can generate a snapshot document is very doable in a few weeks. Building \"real time compliance report generation\" is a much larger undertaking.\n\nI'd say this is technically viable in its core concept but over-scoped as stated. The real-time aspect adds complexity without clear prototyping benefit. I lean toward approve with moderate confidence, because the underlying pattern (automate doc generation from experiment metadata) is genuinely useful and technically achievable, even if \"real time\" needs to be scoped down. From a pure technical viability standpoint, the core is sound. The speed/quality angle: it could help prototyping by reducing manual documentation burden, but the \"real time\" requirement might add friction.\n\nI'll approve it because the core engineering pattern is viable and useful, with moderate promise (the real-time aspect needs scoping, but the fundamental approach works). Maybe 62-68 range for promise. Let me settle on 65 — technically feasible, useful for reducing documentation overhead, but the \"real time\" framing is over-engineered for a prototyping context and doesn't fully address Article 12's specific logging requirements.\n",
