is proposal doesn't implement any of these requirements in our own systems. It's a research project about measuring logging effectiveness externally.\n\nHowever, there's a compliance angle: if we can demonstrate to regulators that we've been actively studying and advancing the state-of-the-art in traceability, that could support our \"state of the art\" arguments and show good faith. Also, if this benchmark becomes an industry standard, it might inform future technical standards (harmonised standards) under the Act. Being involved in shaping those standards is strategically valuable for compliance.\n\n**Market reputation:** This is where the proposal shines. Positioning as a \"global leader in AI safety\" and defining \"the scientific standard for regulatory science\" is a significant reputational play. Regulators, customers, and partners would view us more favorably. In a contested regulatory environment, being seen as a thought leader who's helping implement the Act (rather than just passively complying) can be very valuable.\n\n**Resource commitment concern:** The lab \"cannot pursue everything\" and approving means committing scarce implementation resources. Creating a public benchmark dataset with synthetic challenge cases is a substantial research project. It requires data scientists, engineers to create realistic failure scenarios, and domain experts in AI architectures. This is a multi-person-month (or more) effort that doesn't directly produce a compliance artifact for our own systems.\n\n**Risk assessment:** Is there any downside? Publishing a benchmark where we measure \"logging accuracy\" against injected malfunctions could potentially reveal information about how we think system failures work, which could be useful to adversaries. Also, if the benchmark is flawed or biased, it could embarrass us and damage reputation. There's also the risk that regulators might expect our own systems to meet whatever standard we set in the benchmark, creating a moving t