le-specific adapters on top of the SDK.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is where the proposal really shines from my perspective. If every new high-risk AI project has to build its own logging infrastructure from scratch, that's significant engineering overhead in early prototyping cycles. A shared SDK means:\n  - Researchers can add compliant logging with minimal code changes (a decorator or two).\n  - The \"engineering tax\" on individual projects is reduced — you don't need to figure out the right log schema each time.\n  - Consistency across teams means less rework when compliance reviews happen.\n  - Faster prototyping: if logging is already handled by the SDK, researchers can focus on model development rather than infrastructure.\n- Risk: If the SDK becomes a bottleneck (e.g., every project needs to wait for an SDK feature before they can log something new), it could actually *slow down* prototyping. The \"universal\" constraint might mean the SDK is slow to adapt to novel system architectures.\n- Quality: A well-built SDK with good abstractions improves quality because everyone gets the same baseline compliance logging, reducing the chance that one team misses a required field.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid, technically feasible engineering investment. The pattern (shared infrastructure library for cross-cutting concerns) is well-proven. It directly addresses a real pain point in my domain: ensuring prototyping speed isn't hampered by compliance overhead while maintaining quality and consistency. The main technical risk is over-abstraction — trying to make one decorator handle biometric verification logging AND general risk-event logging might be awkward. But as a foundation layer with project-specific extensions, it's very viable.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a practical, well-scoped engineering solution that directly serves my domain concerns (prototyping speed and technical viability). The promise score should reflect stron