usness to partners, customers, and regulators. It signals institutional maturity.\n\nThe main reservation is whether the proposal explicitly covers all sub-requirements of 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems (reference database, human identifiers). But as a framework design, it seems like those would be configurable fields within the logging schema. The proposal describes the architecture and approach rather than exhaustive field-level specifications, which is appropriate at this level.\n\nOverall: This is a strong, directly relevant compliance enabler. I would approve it. It's not a perfect specification (could use more explicit mapping to 12(3) fields), but as an implementation commitment, it's exactly what a Compliance Officer would want to see — a technical mechanism that makes compliance the default state rather than something dependent on human vigilance.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 85-90. It's very well-aligned with my concerns. The slight deduction is for not explicitly enumerating all 12(3) fields and for the implementation risk of \"all pipelines\" actually being covered (though the \"mandatory\" language helps). Let me go with 87.\n\nDecision: approve.\n",
